N2  - Campylobacter jejuni has been identified as one of the etiologic agents of Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), but recent experimental study showed that B.mellitensis produced anti GM1 ganglioside antibodies and developed flaccid limb weakness. The authors presented 7 patients whose final diagnosis was GBS associated with brucellosis. The mean time between the initiation of brucellosis symptoms and GBS symptoms was 15.8 days. All patients showed motor deficit but glove and stocking sensory deficits were present in 5 of them. Facial diplegia occurred in three patients and disartria in two. The preferred therapeutic schedule was rifampin plus doxycycline and IV immunoglobulin simultaneously. Axonal motor involvement was evident by electroneuromyography (ENMG) in two patients while others were mix sensorymotor involvement. Visual evoked potential (VEP) testing showed bilateral prolonged latency in one patient which might develope secondary to demyelination. The response to treatment was mostly recovery except one who did not take immunoglobulin. In experimental study several genes for lipooligosaccharide biosynthesis in specific GBS-associated C.jejuni strains were found to be similar to lipooligosaccharide biosynthesis genes of B.mellitensis. In the light of these seven cases we can suggest that especially in endemic regions brucella should be considered in GBS patients and treated with antibiotics beside immunomodulatory therapy. © Universitätsverlag Ulm GmbH 2009.
