N2  - In summary, patients with resistant tubotympanic disease, atticoantral disease, suspicion of cholesteatoma or evidence of complications should be referred. Further hospital investigation involves audiometry and CT imaging to delineate the extent of the disease and reveal any occult cholesteatoma. Treatment may be medical or surgical. Irrespective of the means, the ultimate aim is to eradicate disease and create a safe dry ear and possibly restore hearing. Patients referred with tubotympanic disease unresponsive to medical treatment may get a further medical therapy (topical or systemic antibiotics, chemical cautery to control granulation tissue). However a myringoplasty may be needed to create a safe dry ear in a group of patients who want it. Patients with cholesteatoma or atticoantral disease require surgery to eradicate disease. This may involve an atticotomy, atticoantrostomy or mastoidectomy (Figure 3). Once the ear is safe and dry, procedures such as myringoplasty and ossiculoplasty may be considered to restore hearing. © Copyright 2008 Rila Publications Ltd.
