N2  - Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome includes triad of symptoms: granulomatous inflammation of the lips, facial palsy and linqua plicata. This disease is chronic and treatment remains difficult. Both local therapy i.e: intralesional corticosteroid injection, surgery and general therapy: corticosteroids, clofazimine, dapsone, azatiophrine, methotrexate, lymecycline, metronidazole are used. We present the rare case of 55-years women with all symptoms of Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome, diabetes type II and numerous foci of infection in oral cavity. Laboratory tests without important abnormalities. The result of histopathological investigation from lower lip reveiled dermal oedema with pronounced perivascular mononuclear infiltrates and few foci of sarcoidal granulation. The picture consistent with granulomatous inflammation of the lips. During hospitalization Plixym 2 x 1,5 g i.v. was used with some improvement. In eighth day of the therapy Dapsone (100 mg per day) was added, which after 21 days was withdrowen due to high level of methemoglobine. The patient is follow-up. Actually dental extractions are performed.
