N2  - To rehabilitate patients with facial nerve paralysis means to prevent facial synkinesis and contracture during the first 4 months after the onset. Pathophysiology of the facial malfunction is derived from the characteristics of the facial nerve and mimetic muscles with lack of neural fascicles and muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs, respectively. Facial synkinesis and contracture result from aberrant regeneration over the mimetic muscles following neurotmesis on Seddon's classification, hyperexcitability of the facial nucleus following the facial dysfunction, and physiologically constant facial movements including blinking, eating, and talking. It is indispensable to suppress aberrant regeneration from the onset with no gross facial exercises, frequent manual massage with stretching and touching, and eye lid opening exercise with the levator palpebrae superior muscle or an antagonist of the orbicularis oculi muscle. With these techniques, a new physical approach fundamentally keeps facial nucleus excitability and aberrant regeneration suppressed. In contrast, muscle strengthening exercises have been conventionally used to treat flaccid facial paralysis with electrical nerve and/or muscle stimulation to accentuate axonal regeneration and to prevent atrophy. We compared the efficacy of the two physical approaches between the conventional and the suppression or massage, for patients with ENoG being less than 30 % at 2 weeks after the onset. The conventional group consisted of 28 patients with a mean age of 56.9±11.5 years and the massage group 25 patients with a mean age of 53.0±16.2 years. Etiologies consisted of Bell's palsy 37 and Ramsay Hunt syndrome 16. Clinical outcome evaluated by the Sunnybrook facial grading system in one year after the onset showed that the suppression or massage group was significantly superior to the conventional group with higher composite score and less resting asymmetric and synkinetic socres.
