N2  - Bilateral simultaneous facial nevre paralysis (FNP) is an extremely rare clinical entity. FNP might be a special finding in a symptom complex of a systemic disease. Acquired FNP can occur in several diseases of infectious, neoplastic, autoimmune, metabolic, traumatic and neurologic origin. A 20-year-old man applied to our clinic with the complaints of weakness of facial muscles, chewing difficulty, dry eye and undescribed sense on his face. In physical examination, his nasolabial folds and forehead wrinkles bilateraly flatted and he could not close his eyelids. Bell's Phenomenon was bilaterally positive. Systemic examinations and laboratory results did not revealed any pathological finding which could explain the etiology. He was diagnosed as idiopathic bilateral simultaneous facial nevre paralysis (FNP) grade 5 in House Brackmann classification system. Methyl prednisolon (60 mg/day for 10 days) and 30 pyhsical treatment and rehabilitation sessions were performed but he was not sufficiently improved. After three months, there was no sufficient recovery of bilateral cranial nerves functions, and the patient was accepted as bilateral facial nerve demage. Because of etiological and prognostic features, bilateral simultaneous FNP should be currently evaluated. Copyright © 2008 by Türk Tibbi Rehabilitasyon Kurumu Derneǧi.
