N2  - The recent approval by the FDA of rhIGF-I and rhIGF-I/rhIGFBP-3 has raised questions of the utility of these preparations beyond the absolute and rare indications of GHRD, GH-GHR signal transduction defects, IGF-I mutations, and GH inactivating antibodies.29 With the exception of patients with Noonan syndrome who have mutations of the PTNPN11 gene, there is little evidence for partial GH insensitivity affecting response to rhGH treatment. Treatment experience with GHRD and animal data indicate that endocrine IGF-I alone is inadequate for normal growth, and that both IGF-I and GH are required. The administration of rhIGF-I to persons who are GH sufficient may be counterproductive by suppressing GH secretion with resultant reduction in IGFBP-3 and ALS synthesis, and, especially, reduction of direct GH effects on bone and local IGF-I secretion. Recombinant IGF-I, however, in combination with rhGH, may have a role in the treatment of children with severe idiopathic short stature who have very low IGF-I levels and who are not responding adequately to rhGH. © 2007 Mosby, Inc. All rights reserved.
