N2  - Persistent conductive hearing loss after head injury is usually seen after fracture to the temporal bone. Fractures may be classified as longitudinal, transverse or mixed. Longitudinal fractures are more commonly associated with a conductive loss, transverse fractures with a sensorineural loss, vertigo, and facial nerve palsy. Loss of consciousness, bleeding from the ear and subjective hearing loss are key symptoms likely to indicate a conductive loss. A resolving hearing loss may be due to fluid and debris in the middle ear, perforation or ligamentous healing; persistence indicates ossicular disruption requiring exploratory tympanotomy. Initial investigation should include an audiogram, tympanometry and CT-scan, and the initial management is usually conservative, with surgery reserved for a persisting hearing loss.
