N2  - In this report, a cross-face nerve graft was placed between the zygomatic branches on both sides, accompanied with an interpositional jump graft between the buccal branch and the hypoglossal nerve. The nerve graft was made using the sural nerve. The nerve suture between the grafted nerve and the hypoglossal nerve was made in an end-to-side mode through the perineural window without axonal rupture of the hypoglossal nerve. Eleven patients (38-71 y. o.) who presented with complete facial paralysis after the resection of the acoustic tumor underwent this procedure. The duration between the onset of paralysis and the operation ranged from 3 to 32 weeks. Functional recovery via the jump graft was confirmed between 4 months and 11 months after the operation. Functional recovery via the cross-face nerve graft was seen between 8 months and 18months after the operation. In our series, reinnervation via the jump graft was seen earlier and more conspicuously than via the cross-face nerve graft. The facial movement was very natural, without accompanying mass movement of muscles and tongue atrophy. The patients can also smile without closing their eyes. But long term facial paralysis patients in whom much time had elapsed since the onset showed poor functional recovery. This procedure is useful for recovering the natural facial expression without the accompanying functional disturbances of the tongue. But, we recommend that it should be performed within three or four months after the onset of paralysis.
