N2  - Aim: To verify that facial nerve of patients with complete facial paralysis is not complete degeneration, may existing a part of the conduction block fibers in facial nerve after the electrophysiological observation in patients with complete Bell palsy that accepted the decompression before and after operation. Methods: Thirty-one unilateral total Bell palsy patients, who were treated at the Facial Nerve Research Room, Beijing Shijitan Hospital from March to June 2005, were selected. Mean progress was 97.3 days. All the patients were classified by House-Brackmann (I-VI grades, the higher the grade, the more severe of level of facial paralysis was.), and they were all VI grade, and all treated with facial nerve decompression through mastoid subtemporal approach. Action potential and F wave at the bilateral depressor anguli oris muscle were detected before operation. Amplitude of vibration and duration were compared. Re-examination was performed at the 7th day after operation. Changes of parameter of action potential before and after operation were compared. The House-Brackmann grading was assessed. Results: Thirty-one patients were all involved in the result analysis. 1 Action potential of the injured side: It was found in 26 cases before operation, and in all the cases after operation. The mean amplitude of vibration after operation increased significantly than that before operation [(324.73±384.02), (94.48±88.15)μV,P < 0.01]. Mean duration after operation became rapidly obviously than that before operation [(11.64±7.86), (4.10±3.08)ms, P<0.01]. 2 F wave: There was no F wave before operation, while it appeared in 3 cases (10%) after operation. 3 In the first 7 days after operation, 27 of 31 (87%) patients had the visible voluntary movements of facial muscle. Twenty-two patients reached House-Brackmann V grade, and 5 patients reached House-BrackmannIV grade. Conclusion: 1 Total facial paralysis is not equal to total facial fiber degeneration. Quite a few conduction block fibers still remain in most of patients, so 100% degeneration of facial nerve fibers cannot be defined carelessly. 2 The patients with disappeared F wave have bad prognosis, and conservative therapy is ineffective. Prompt facial nerve decompression can effectively relieve conduction block so as to have early clinical rehabilitative process appeared after operation. 3 When the level of facial nerve functional restoration is assessed in an early period after operation, the free action potential of electromyography is more sensitive than F waves.
