N2  - Crohn's disease (CD) is a non-specific, inflammatory bowel disorder. Pathological changes can occur within the whole gastrointestinal tract and clinical symptoms depend on the location of the lesions. Most frequently the patients affected with Crohn's disease suffer from spastic abdominal pain and diarrhoea. In most cases pharmacological therapy is applied, however in case of complications surgical treatment is necessary. Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome (MRS) is characterized by recurrent or persistent swelling of the face and lips (cheilitis granulomatosa), relapsing facial palsy and a fissured tongue. The association between MRS and CD is controversial. A case of a 54-year-old woman with the record of recurrent orofacial swelling and facial palsy, which developed many years before CD was diagnosed, is reported. On the basis of our own and other anthors experience it can be concluded that in patients with MRS regular gastrointestinal examinations should be performed.
