N2  - We report a rare case of granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF)-producing mucoepidermoid carcinoma of the parotid gland. A 73-year-old woman presented with a gradually increasing ulcerative mass in the left subaural region. She also manifested complete facial nerve palsy of the affected side. CT and MRI revealed an 80 × 66 mm ill-defined parotid tumor extending to the parapharyngeal space. Blood examination showed marked neutrophilia (white blood count 23,060/μl, neutrophil 81.5%). The histological diagnosis of a biopsy specimen was mucoepidermoid carcinoma. Immunohistochemically, the tumor cells expressed G-CSF in their cytoplasm. Although a temporary remission was obtained after neoadjuvant chemotherapy using cisplatin and 5-fluorouracil, the patient died of carcinomatous pleuritis 11 weeks after admission. Reported cases of G-CSF-producing carcinoma of the head and neck were reviewed, and possible pathophysiology of the tumor was bibliographically discussed.
