N2  - Surgical treatment of vertigo is directed at disorders affecting the peripheral vestibular system from the root entry zone, medially, to the oticcapsule, laterally. BPPV is the most common cause of vertigo in patients of all ages. A particle repositioning maneuver or physical therapy usually is effective in its management. On rare occasions, surgical intervention is necessary. Endolymphatic hydrops, or Meniere's disease, often responds to medical treatment. Surgical intervention is warranted when severe and frequent vertigo attacks persist despite attempts at medical management for 4 to 6 months. The other pathologies described (superior canal dehiscence syndrome, cholesteatoma, and perilymphatic fistula) require surgical intervention based on the defined nature of the pathology. Consultation with otolaryngologists is helpful in the evaluation and management of patients who have vestibular disorders, particularly when medical management fails. © 2005 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
