N2  - The pitfalls of the endoscopic forehead midface surgery include: frontal branch paresis/paralysis, hypesthesia/dysethesia, glabelar irregularities, abnormal brow position, unmasking of ptosis, infection, hematoma, prolonged edema, chemosis, canthal distortion, dry eye syndrome, alopecia, engorgement of periorbital veins, and masticatory tenderness. In our experience, none of these complications led to serious or permanent sequalae, and most of them can be entirely avoided by strict adherence to good technique. Frontal branch paresis occurs in 1% of cases; permanent paralysis has never occurred. Both can be avoided by careful dissection, a thorough understanding of the nerve's course, and the judicious use of cautery. Hypaesthesia and dysasthesias of the forehead are universally experienced, can be minimized with preservation of the supraorbital and supratrochlear neurovascular bundles, and are followed by complete recovery in most cases by 6 months and all cases by 1 year. Glabelar irregularities do occur and can be avoided by more homogenous resection of the corrugator muscle. Careful attention to pre-existing brow asymmetries and placement of brow incisions/screw placement are critical to preventing unacceptable brow asymmetries postoperatively. Identification of the patient with a hyperactive frontalis is critical to avoiding futile attempts to make the brows symmetric. Upper lid ptosis can be unmasked by this operation and the patient must be made aware of preoperatively. Frank infection occurs in much less than 1% of cases and is more likely to manifest as a suture abscess. Hematoma is very rare, can be difficult to recognize, but does not compromise flap viability due to the deeper plane of dissection. Prolonged edema of the lateral temporal/periorbital area occurs in 5% of patients and resolution may be aided by diuretics and lymphatic drainage. Chemosis happens in 30% of patients but universally resolves and can be managed by hypertonic saline and steroid drops. Canthal distortion requiring revision occurred in 3 of 900 cases. Revision can be accomplished by temporal release. A more aggressive midfacial dissection with zero-tension superolateral midfacial suspension, preservation of a lateral cuff of pericanthal tissue, and massage has helped dramatically to limit the number of revisions and increased patient satisfaction. Temporary dry eye syndrome may be related to increased lateral scleral show and tension across the lower lid but universally resolves with canthal massage. Permanent alopecia can be avoided by proper incision placement in the direction of hair follicles and avoiding excessive tension in forehead suspension. Telogen effluvium may occur. Engorgment of periorbital veins can be avoided by preservation of the sentinel and bridging veins. Masticatory tenderness can be minimized by careful dissectio of the masseter aponeurosis. It usually resolves within 48 hours, but if it persists it can be managed with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and mouthopening exercises. Although the endoscopic forehead midface lift is not without its complications or pitfalls, all of these can be minimized, easily managed, or avoided completely through the intraoperative techniques and postoperative care described in this article. Careful and deliberate preoperative counseling of patients regarding the possible "bumps" in the road to recovery is critical. By using the techniques available to limit and manage complications and setting appropriate patient expectations should these complications occur, the endoscopic forehead midface lift can become an extremely powerful safe technique in the facial cosmetic surgeon's armamentarium with a high degree of patient satisfaction. © 2005 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
