N2  - The correction of lagophthalmos is an urgent problem in facial nerve palsy. This deformity often develops keratitis and ulceration of the cornea, and may lead to blindness. A passive, circumorbital sling of fascia lata is a useful method of correcting lower lid deformity. However, lagophthalmos is not only due to the drooping of the lower eyelid but also to the overaction of the upper eyelid caused by the absence of the levator muscle antagonist. Reconstruction of the levator muscle antagonist is accomplished using a temporalis transfer. It is one of the most useful methods of correcting both lower and upper eyelid deformities. The absence of mimetic muscles resulting from long-standing atrophy was thought to be the best indication of regional muscle transposition. When facial nerves are injured due to parotid cancer resection, we usually repair them using autogenous nerve grafting. If the nerves are repaired, lagophthalmos typically afflicts elderly patients for over six months. Corneal trauma may develop in this period. Therefore, we sometimes use a temporalis transfer in combination with nerve repair just after the resection of parotid cancer involving the facial nerves.
