N2  - Bell's palsy is the most common cause of peripheral facial nerve paralysis. However, it is a diagnosis of exclusion, and other causes of facial nerve paralysis should be ruled out. A case report is presented of a patient with facial nerve paralysis caused by a primary adenocarcinoma of the middle ear. Delay in diagnosis and treatment in such cases may result in increased rates of mortality and morbidity. Therefore, acute facial nerve paralysis mimicking Bell's palsy which fails to resolve should lead to further examination and re-evaluation of the diagnosis.
