N2  - Introduction: Zona is a viral infection rarely affecting healthy children. Its course is generally not severe on this ground. We report six observations of zona affecting immunocompetent children one case of which complicated with facial paralysis. Patients and methodes: this retrospective study deals about young patients affected with zona and not more than 15 years of age over a period of 5 years. Results: three girls and three boys, 6,5 years of median age, present no immunodeficiency. The zona occured in the intercostal area in two cases, lumbarfemoral in two cases, cervicobrachial in one case and in the Ramsay-Hunt area in the sixth case where the zona was accompanied by a facial paralysis. The course was favourable under symptomatic treatment in 5 cases. As to the sixth case complicated with facial paralysis, we had to resort to an antiviral treatment associated with general corticotherapy. Comments: the course of zona in the child is usually benign. Nevertheless, we should not be unaware of the existence of complications such as facial paralysis observed in our patient. The treatment is symptomatic in the common forms and requires resort to early antiviral treatment in the complex forms so as to prevent after-effects. © Copyright 2005, CIC Edizioni Internazionali, Roma.
