N2  - A patient with Facial diplegia and paresthesias, a rare regional variant of Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS), is described. A 37-year-old woman developed paresthesias in the distal limbs and subsequently bifacial weakness. She had had a preceding episode of laryngitis. Neurological examination showed severe facial diplegia with loss of taste sensation on the tip of the tongue. Limb muscle power was preserved. Deep tendon reflexes were generally absent. She complained of paresthesias in the distal limbs, but sensory examination was normal. Cerebrospinal fluid showed albuminocytological dissociation. Electrophysiological studies revealed severe facial nerve involvements and demyelinative findings in her limbs. Intravenous immunoglobulin rapidly improved the abnormal sensation in her limbs. Although facial diplegia gradually lessened after the therapy, mild residual weakness was noted 6 months after the neurological onset. To diagnose facial diplegia and paresthesias, it is important to clarify the findings common to typical GBS such as the antecedent illness, acute and monophasic course, distal paresthesias, areflexia, cerebrospinal fluid albuminocytological dissociation, and demyelinative conduction abnormalities in the limbs.
