N2  - Aim: To explore the value of blink reflex(BR) and facial electroneurogam (ENoG) in evaluating the degree and location of facial nerve damage in patients with Bell palsy. Methods: BR and ENoG were conducted in 20 patients with Bell paralysis respectively 1 - 14 days after episode. Results: The ipsilateral R1, R2 and R2′ waves were absent in 5 cases, and in the remaining 15 cases, the latencies of R1, R2 and R2′ were prolonged in the ipsilateral side as compared with those in the uninjured side, and the abnormal rate was 100%. There were longer latent period and lower wave amplitude of facial nerve in the ipsilateral side, significantly different from those in the uninjured side(P < 0.01). The ratio of R1/M lateral indicated that there were 10 cases of proximal(inside of stylomastoid foramen) injury, 5 cases with distal(outside of stylomastoid foramen) injury and 5 cases with extensive injury. Conclusion: BR can be used as a sensitive index for early diagnosis of Bell paralysis. BR and ENoG can evaluate the degrees and location of facial nerve damage wholly and objectively in patients with Bell paralysis, and it is helpful for the clinical treatment and prognostic evaluation.
