N2  - This article describes our clinical experience with two cases of transient total facial palsy after bilateral sagittal split ramus osteotomy in mandibular prognathism. In case 1, the paralysis was caused by Bell palsy, of which the possible underlying cause was increased susceptibility to a viral infection of the facial nerve after the surgical intervention. In case 2, although the exact cause was unknown, the likely cause of the palsy was the stretching of the facial nerve as a result of her mandibular ramus being set back deeply. We did not consider immediate surgical exploration or decompression surgery, but rather took the approach of conservative physical therapy treatment and administration of high-dose steroids. Our conservative treatment approach proved to be effective for both of our patients, as they recovered almost completely from their facial palsies within 3 weeks and 3 months, respectively.
