N2  - Case: The patient, a 35-year-old male, injured his head and face in a traffic accident. He was treated for a lacerated temporo-fasial wound by a surgeon at Yao Tokusyukai General Hospital. Although the wound healed, the patient was unable to lift his right side eyebrow. After 6 months, he was referred to us by the surgeon for plastic surgery. On examination, he was diagnosed as having right temporal branch facial nerve paralysis. He underwent surgery, but because there was an approximately 1 cm defect in one of the face nerves, a nerve graft was performed. The auriculo-temporal nerve on the right side was selected as a donor. The facial palsy fully disappeared 9 months after the operation. The advantage in selecting the auriculo-temporal nerve as the donor is that it is in the same operating field as the graft side. The disadvantage is that the donor is insufficient in the case of facial nerve injuries involving many branches. It was thought that the auriculo-temporal nerve will become one choice as a donor in nerve grafts for nerve defects of 1 or 2 distal branches of the facial nerve.
