N2  - Mucosally active vaccine adjuvants which will prime a full range of local and systemic immune responses against defined antigenic epitopes are much needed. Cholera toxin (CT) and lipophilic immune stimulating complexes (ISCOMs) containing Quil A can both act as adjuvants for orally administered antigens, but through separate pathways, as evidenced by the dependence on IL-12 for the effect of ISCOMs, whereas CT is independent of this cytokine. Unfortunately the toxicity of CT and recent findings of accumulation of CT in the olfactory nerve and bulb after intranasal administration precludes the clinical use of CT. However, we have been successful in separating the adjuvant and toxic effects of CT, by constructing a gene fusion protein, CTA1-DD, that combines the enzymatically active CTA1-subunit with a B cell targeting moiety, D, derived from Staphylococcus aureus protein A. The present review gives a background to mucosal immunization and the use of adjuvants in general, followed by a description of a strategy to rationally design a vaccine adjuvant vector that fulfils the criteria of targeting and immunomodulating innate immunity in order to boost a strong adaptive immune response. We have combined CTA1-DD and ISCOMs into a new highly promising vaccine adjuvant vector, CTA1-DD/ISCOMs. The combined vector is immunogenic when given by the subcutaneous, oral or nasal routes, inducing strong cell-mediated and humoral immune responses, including local mucosal IgA. It requires the ADP ribosylating property of the CTA1-enzyme and the effect of the combined vector greatly exceeded the effect of either ISCOMs or CT used alone. Antigens could be incorporated into or just admixed with the new vector. Thus, we have demonstrated that rationally designed vectors consisting of CTA1-DD and ISCOMS may provide a novel strategy for the generation of potent and safe mucosal vaccines.
