N2  - Background: The embolic stroke essentially affects one of the middle cerebral arteries. The patients are generally individuals with risk factors such as diabetes, hypertension or transient ischemick attacks. Based on scientific papers published since fibrinolythic therapy has been introduced in 1983, one could say that the best time to introduce the therapy is 6 hours after the onset of the stroke. Case Report: The condition of a 46-year-old woman rapidly deteriorated. After a headache the patient become unconscious. The woman was admitted to hospital with suspected subarachnoid hemorrhage. The clinical evaluation revealed left-sided spastic haemiparesis, paresis of the VII right cranial nerve, and symptoms of meningitis (Hunt&Hess III, GCS 10 pts.). The CT examination and lumbar punction provided no significant data or revealed pathology. The arteriography, performed in the Seldinger technique with right femoral approach, revealed an embolic stroke of right middle cerebral artery. Using the Smagic micocatheter, the Actilyse (Boehringer Ingelheim) was administered directly to the embolic vessel in overall dose of 50 mg in fractioned manner (diluted with 50cc of water for injections) over the time of 30 minutes. The control examination showed recanalization of the cerebral artery. The patient's condition improved gradually during the first day of hospitalization. On the second day, the woman was mobilized and started to walk. For therapeutic purposes the hypervolemy and anticoagulants were introduced (Fraxiparine, Acard). The authors have not found such a case recorded in Polish literature so far. Therefore they hope that this case report is the first one to be presented in Poland. Conclusions: The direct administration of the fibrynolithyc agent (intraarterial injection) allowed us to obtain full therapeutic effect in case of embolic stroke.
