N2  - The petroclival region is located in the skull base. Because of its depth and vital structures adjacent and within it, surgical exposure was difficult and often questionable in past years. Access to this region has been achieved recently by several newly described approaches. The transpetrosal surgical approach has been applied and evaluated in the treatment of petroclival lesions at the Shiraz Neurosurgery Center. In a prospective study from March 1991 to March 2001, 20 patients were operated on by the combined supra- and infratentorial transpetrosal approach. Fourteen patients were female and 6 were male, with an age range from 5 to 60 years. Access to the lesion, symptoms, signs, type of pathology, extent of resection, location of tumor, complications, and mortality were determined and compared with outcomes of other studies. The most common symptom was headache, the most common sign was facial hypesthesia, and the most common postoperative complication was abducens palsy. Access to the lesion was achieved in all cases. The extent of resection was gross total (45%), subtotal (30%), and partial (25%) in comparison with other studies that reported ranges from 35% to 85%, from 29% to 47%, and 31%, respectively. Eleven (55%) patients developed complications, and there was 1 (5%) mortality compared with other studies that have reported approximately 50% complications and 0% to 10% mortality. The advantages of the transpetrosal approach are 100% accessibility, wide and excellent exposure, short distance from the brain surface, acceptable complications, and low mortality. This approach is suggested for petroclival lesions.
