N2  - Although the deformity of hemifacial microsomia (HFM) is widespread and varies in extent, most treatments focus on the mandible and ear abnormalities, rather than on the associated facial paralysis. Thus, surgical treatment for the facial paralysis associated with HFM is quite underdeveloped. The neurovascular free muscle transfer is now a standard procedure for dynamic smile reconstruction in long-standing facial paralysis patients. This type of strategy has considerable potential in the treatment of facial paralysis in patients with HFM. We present here, our experience with neurovascular free muscle transfers for smile reconstruction in 8 patients with facial paralysis associated with HFM. Either a two-stage procedure combining a gracilis muscle transfer with cross-face nerve grafting or a one-stage transfer of the latissimus dorsi muscle was performed. Muscle contraction was evident in all patients between 4 to 8 months after muscle transfer. Our present series revealed that the neurovascular free muscle transfer is a good option not only for smile reconstruction but also for restoration of the facial contours of patients with HMF. Compared with the two-stage method combining a gracilis muscle transfer with cross-face nerve grafting, the one-stage method using the latissimus dorsi muscle poses some advantages including a one stage operation and a shorter recovery period.
