N2  - We report two cases of vocal fold paralysis due to varicella-zoster virus infection. Both patients complained of otalgia and hoarseness and the serum antibody titers for varicella-zoster virus were significantly elevated. Neither patient showed facial palsy, and laryngeal electromyography showed reduced recruitment patterns in cricothyroid muscles and thyroarytenoid muscles on the affected side. These findings indicated the paralysis of the vagus nerve, but not the recurrence nerve. Acyclovir was administrated in both cases and the patients recovered from vocal fold paralysis within 3 months.
