N2  - Diagnosing neuroborreliosis in children is not easy. Various clinical presentations may be seen and often they differ from clinical presentations seen in adults. Frequently used serology tests do not always confirm diagnosis. To get a better insight into its clinical presentation and possible diagnostic means, and also to set up clear guidelines for diagnosing neuroborreliosis in children, we performed a descriptive, retrospective study and compared these data with those from the literature. The clinical profile of neuroborreliosis in Belgian children corresponds to that in other European populations: the most typical presentations of neuroborreliosis in children in Belgium are a facial nerve palsy and a lymfocytic meningitis. These presentations are mostly seen during the months of April until September. Besides a complete history and clinical examination, an examination of the liquor and the search for specific antibodies in both serum and liquor are necessary to confirm the diagnosis. Intravenous treatment with third generation cefalosporins for at least fourteen days is the golden standard.
