N2  - Aim. To provide proper recovery diagnosis and treatment through analysis of recovery related factors in temporal traumatic facial paralysis. Methods. Clinical data of 196 patients with temporal traumatic facial paralysis were evaluated retrospectively. The results were analyzed with single factor and multiple factor statistic method. Results. According to the result of rehabilitation, the related factors that effected the rehabilitation were divided into 3 groups: group I, group II and group III. The group I included 70 cases of mild and moderate facial paralysis and I and II degree of autonomic control, treated with drug decompression. 61 out of 70 cases in the group I clinically recovered and 9 out of 70 cases recovered well. The group II included 87 cases of severe facial paralysis, III degree of autonomic control, treated with facial nerve decompression and drug decompression. 60 out of 87 cases in the group II clinically recovered, 26 out of 87 cases recovered well and 1 out of 87 cases efficiently recovered. The group III included 39 cases of severe facial paralysis, autonomic control III and deafness, treated with facial nerve decompression at 4 months after facial paralysis and late drug decompression. 27 out of 39 cases in the group III efficiently recovered and 12 out of 39 cases inefficiently recovered. The efficient and inefficient rates of rehabilitation showed significant difference between the group II and III. Conclusion. Facial nerve paralysis and degree of autonomic control are basic criteria in recovery diagnosis. Early facial nerve decompression is a significant effective method in raising recovery level.
