N2  - In this article the author focuses on the qi mechanism (qiji), its potential disorders and the possible methods of treating these by using acupuncture. In his opinion, the basic requirement for a physiological equilibrium in accordance with the principle of "the balance of the yin and the stability of the yang" (yinping yangmi) is the harmonious raising and lowering by the qi mechanism (qiji). With this in mind, the author presents the widely applicable method for acupuncture and moxibustion "Raising and lowering the qi mechanism" (qiji shengjiang), which is subdivided into ten procedures: boosting the qi, raising, containing "desertion" (tuo), mobilizing the qi, release of qi stases, development of the qi, lowering the qi, neutralising counterflow, cohesion of the qi as well as the absorption of the qi. For each of these procedures he provides indications and the acupuncture points to be chosen, and he describes the needle and moxibustion techniques to be used. In the second part of the article the author describes three case studies in which his approach was most successful. The first patient was suffering from secondary infertility attributable to "glare" (huo) with blockage of the qi and a lack of interaction between the "renal" and "heart" functional systems (xin and shen). In this case it was necessary to soothe and regulate the "liver" functional system (gan), to drain away the "glare" and to link up the "renal" and "heart" functional systems again. To achieve this a number of acupuncture points had to be needled, some of them drawing blood, and it was necessary to apply cupping and moxibustion with the intention of deliberate expulsion and supplementation. The needle technique in each case is explained in detail as well as the corresponding effect of the treatment at each acupuncture point. The second patient suffered from chronic hiccups as a result of rising, counterflowing stomach qi (weiqi). The therapy applied by the author consisted of regulation of the qi, arresting of the hiccups, harmonisation of the stomach qi (weiqi) and lowering of counterflow, which involved balanced expulsive and supplementive needling at the two acupuncture points REN 12 "Middle Cavity" (zhongwan) and REN 6 "Sea of qi" (qihai) and the use of the "Method of Fire that burns down the forest" (shao shan huo fa). Here, too, the acupuncture points chosen, the needle techniques used and the effects of the method are all described in detail. The third patient was a particularly difficult case of facial paralysis resulting from rising "glare" (huo) in the liver and gall bladder functional systems (gan and dan) and bi-obstructions of the network channels (luomai). Following initial success by calming the liver functional system, draining glare and releasing of blockages in the main and network channels which involved the needling of a number of acupuncture points, in some cases drawing blood, and the cupping of others, the treatment had to be interrupted owing to problems in the patient's family. When therapy was resumed, the physician found there to be depletion (xu) accompanied by bi-obstruction in the network channels (luomai). The qi now had to be boosted, the yang to be raised and the main and network channels to be unblocked so that energy could flow freely again. Once again a number of specific acupuncture points had to be needled and now also to be treated with moxibustion. At this time the patient was again prone to suffering psychological stress so that after a while stases of the liver qi (gangqi) developed together with an inadequate supply of qi to the network channels. Subsequent therapy consisted of soothing of the liver qi (ganqi), regulating and boosting the qi, and enabling the free flow of energy through the network channels, which required needling and moxibustion at a number of acupuncture points. This treatment finally led to the patient's return to good health.
