N2  - Objectives: To review our experience of benign tumours of the facial nerve over a thirteen year period to enable recommendations on the timing of surgical intervention, the approach to surgical resection, the technique of reconstruction of the facial nerve, and the expected outcomes of surgery. Materials and methods: Fifteen patients with benign tumours of the facial nerve were identified from a database of 820 patients with facial nerve disorders. Ten patients who underwent surgical intervention were studied. Results: Patients with prolonged complete facial paralysis prior to surgery were found to make no clinical recovery following surgical resection and facial nerve grafting. Surgical resection should occur when clinical nerve function deteriorates but before complete paralysis. Patients with normal facial function should be observed clinically and with serial high resolution magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium. The pathological extent of the tumour may be greater than predicted by MRI appearance. Hearing preservation using transmastoid middle cranial fossa approach is feasible. Conclusion: Benign tumours of the facial nerve are rare, affect young people, are frequently misdiagnosed and lead to progressive loss of facial function. Resection when there is residual function gives partial recovery whereas resection when paralysis is complete gives poor functional recovery. Hearing preservation surgery is possible following removal of these tumours.
