N2  - This report described a new surgical procedure of temporalis myoplasty for the treatment of facial paralysis, based on Labbé's technique. The new method consists of muscle rotation and lengthening, partial release of the muscle at the temporal line of fusion, and endoscopic transfer of the muscle tendon attached to the coronoid process to the corner of the mouth. With this approach, osteotomy of the zygomatic arch is unnecessary. Ten cadaver dissections were performed to develop surgical skills and measure progression of the technique. Results in two patients were reported. The technique requires familiarity with the endoscope, and it permits excellent visualization of the neurovascular structures. This approach seems to be less painful and less likely to result in neurovascular injury, and it can be performed through smaller incisions.
