N2  - The authors retrospectively report results of a series of 28 patients (mean age 52 years) with sequels of facial paralysis which were treated with a standardised open forehead and brow lift. Follow-up was at three, six and 12 months after surgery. Only patients with a minimum of House-Brackmann grade III were accepted for surgery. The forehead lift was done under general anaesthesia. The mean hospital stay was two days. Patients had a working disability after surgery ranging from two to four weeks. All 28 patients who preoperatively had reported a reduced visual field and sensation of pressure at the eyelid-brow area of the affected side experienced postoperative improvement. All patients described an improved symmetry of the forehead. The scar at the scalp area was not negatively commented about although small areas of alopecia were observed in three patients. The postoperative sensation of scalp tension disappeared six months after surgery in all cases. Recurrence of some eyebrow drop at rest within one year after surgery was noted in two of the 28 patients. These two patients were dissatisfied with the result. A relationship between the degree of preoperative asymmetry and the recurrence of eyebrow drop was not found. Twenty-six patients were satisfied with the aesthetic outcome and would recommend this procedure to others. The open forehead lift is a safe procedure with high patient acceptance and can be recommended to improve forehead symmetry in facial paralysis.
