N2  - Facial palsy is one of the most challenging problems in plastic surgery. Traditional methods for the treatment of chronic facial palsy use a two-stage muscle flap (mostly the gracilis muscle), which is time-consuming and burdensome to patients, who might be averse to waiting 8 to 12 months between the two stages. In this study, a new technique is introduced for one-stage treatment of facial paralysis in which 25 g of the gracilis muscle (measuring 7 to 10 cm long and 2 to 4 cm wide) is used. The flap is placed in a reversed position and rotated in one stage. The procedure is performed without using a cross-facial nerve graft. During nearly 2 years, five patients were treated with this technique and their functional results were compared. After 2 years of follow-up,.the functional results of our patients are reportedly good to excellent. The expressive movement of the soft tissues of the face can be seen on the paralyzed side by 6 months after treatment. Therefore, we recommend this technique as the procedure of choice for the treatment of chronic facial palsy.
