N2  - Peripheral facial palsy (PFP) is commonest a idiopathic condition, Bell's palsy, often with a good prognosis. Nevertheless an accurate diagnosis is very important, in the most of cases it can be made on the basis of clinical evaluation. In this article the author emphasises the relevant role of general practitioner who take a practical approach with a early effective prescription. It is important to remind uncommon forms of PFP with others symptoms and abnormalities on neurological examination, that has different aetiology, and require always an approach by neurologist.
