N2  - In addition to the 12 main meridians (paths of conductance), TCM also describes tiny collateral connection paths, so-called extraordinary conductors (vessels) and the tendinomuscular conductors. Corresponding to the 12 main conductors, we know 12 tendinomuscular conductors with specific properties. We consider diseases of the musculature, tendons and joints due to penetration of so-called pathogenic factors as an indication for use of the tendinomuscular meridians, by way of which we also treat surface diseases such as facial paralysis and trigeminus neuralgia. From studies done by Heine, we know that projection of the fascia perforations of classic acupuncture points onto the underlying muscles represent fasciomyotendinokinetic chains. The connecting line between the acupuncture points of such a chain corresponds exactly to the course of a meridian. Inserting an acupuncture needle therefore induces reflexive mechanisms in the nervous system.
