N2  - In summary, it can be said that there are insufficient data to establish with certainty the incidence of rare, serious complications (e.g. uterine rupture, fetal of maternal death) associated with the use of misoprostol in the induction of labor. The number of anecdotal reports of uterine rupture after misoprostol administration is increasing, predominantly in women with prior CS. From the data currently available, we have calculated a frequency of this complication in patients with prior CS of approximately 4.5%. At this time a cautious approach to the use of misoprostol in these patients is recommended. In patients without uterine scar it is recommended that misoprostol be administered vaginally at a dosage of 25 μg every 3-6h for the induction of labor, but only with close monitoring of the patient and preferably within the framework of clinical studies. However, consideration should also be given to the fact that misoprostol has been reported to exhibit teratogenicity, although this has been observed mainly when large oral doses (400-800 μg/day) are given in early pregnancy, and appears to be associated with particular malformations (e.g. Mobius syndrome).
