N2  - Over time criteria for surgical options in cases of parotid neoplasias have been modified. They are determined by the presence of the facial nerve and the apparently marked tendency of the more common forms to recur. Since the 1950s onwards exofacial parotidectomy has been considered treatment of choice but is associated with adverse sequelae such as transient facial paresis, depression in the parotid area and Frey's syndrome. Recently several international studies have reported good results with conservative surgery of the parenchyma i.e. enucleation after facial dissection, asportation of only half the parotid, and so on. Excellent disease eradication is associated with a lower morbidity impact in terms of facial mobility and esthetic results. We present our results in a series of 173 patients affected by primary parotid gland neoplasias. Using the above criteria we adopted a wide range of surgical approaches, opting in most cases for a conservative approach to the glandular parenchyma. A transient deficit in facial mobility occurred in 71% cases of total parotidectomy, in 26% of exofacial parotidectomy, in 2.6% of enucleation after facial nerve dissection and in 1% of enucleation alone. Permanent paresis was observed in 1% of patients who underwent total parotidectomy. Frey's syndrome developed in only three patients, all of whom treated with exofacial parotidectomy. The relapse rate (excluding malignant neoplasias) was 5%. Five of the 13 patients with malignant tumors are alive and well after a 3-5 years follow-up.
