N2  - A 4-year-old girl with a brainstem tumor was diagnosed at other clinic as Bell's palsy. Right mild sensorineural hearing loss and nystagmus toward the left strongly suggested a central lesion for the cause of right facial palsy. Space occupying lesion was revealed in brainstem by MRI. Unfortunately surgical removal and postoperative irradiation were not effective and she died in one year later. Histopathological diagnosis was pilocytic astrocytoma. It is necessary to rule out neoplastic origin before making a diagnosis of Bell's palsy in small children.
