N2  - Temporal bone malignancy is rare, with squamous cell carcinoma the most common cancer of the external auditory canal, middle ear, and mastoid. There is no gender predominance, and most cases occur in the fifth and sixth decades of life. Otorrhea is the most common symptom of external auditory canal cancer. Diagnosis can be delayed because cancer mimics more common conditions of the external auditory canal, such as otitis externa. There is no universally accepted staging system, but several systems have been proposed and are useful for prognosis. Skull base CT scanning and MR imaging are essential for staging and treatment planning. Surgery is the primary treatment modality for temporal bone carcinoma. Lateral temporal bone resection is the most commonly recommended treatment for T1 lesions of the external auditory canal. Opinion is split as to the extent of resection for advanced disease. The majority of otologists surveyed would perform a piecemeal or STBR procedure, whereas a minority believed that TTBR was indicated in all cases of advanced disease. Half the otologists would resect the carotid artery for invasive disease to the carotid canal, and half would resect dura or temporal lobe for brain invasion. Postoperative radiotherapy is recommended for all T2, T3, and T4 lesions and is considered for T1 lesions depending on the site of the tumor and histologic evidence of invasion. In the past 50 years, survival rates for advanced lesions have improved in most series, from a 25% 5-year survival rate to a 50% survival rate. Poor prognostic factors include facial nerve paralysis, positive tumor resection margins, dural involvement, regional lymph node involvement, multiple cranial nerve involvement, and severe pain. There are a variety of recontructive techniques, but the temporalis flap is the favorite local, pedicled muscle flap.
