N2  - Purpose. Neuroblastoma is often accompanied by bone or bone marrow metastasis already at the time of the initial diagnosis, but meningeal involvement is relatively rare. We experienced 5 patients of neuroblastoma with primary bone marrow metastasis who showed meningeal involvement presenting disturbance of central nervous system from 9 months to 10 years after initial treatment without cranial involvement at the time of initial diagnosis. Material and Methods. The symptoms were sudden convulsion and disturbed consciousness in 4 patients and facial paralysis in one. In each patient, pseudorosette forming tumor cells were present in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). Neuroimaging findings were 2 dilatation of meningeal space and ventricle, 1 high intensity imaging in the bilateral occipital region, and 2 discreate mass. Results. Total brain irradiation was performed in 4 patients, local irradiation on the tumor area in one, and resection of the recurrent brain tumor in one. Cancer chemotherapy was performed in all patients. Four patients died of multiple organ metastases after 6 to 12 months, and the course has been observed in the remaining patient. Conclusions. These findings suggested that meningeal involvement may occur more frequently in the children with primary bone marrow metastasis neuroblastoma, the important findings of these complication were appearance of tumor cells in CSF and poor prognosis, of these cases.
