KW  - weight
N2  - Background: Implantation of gold weights in upper eyelids is a technique used world-wide for the treatment of paralytic lagophthalmos. It allows closure of the eyelids through an increased gravitational pull on the relaxed eyelid, whereas complete opening by the m. levator palpebrae superioris remains intact. Purpose: The majority of the patients is probably treated with commercially available implants which are designed for universal application and have a standard curvature. We report our experiences with 98 patients treated with individually adapted custom-made gold weights to correct paralytic lagophthalmos. Methods: Ninety-eight patients with unilateral facial paralysis treated with gold weight implantation in the upper eyelid were retrospectively studied. Sixty-three patients also had other facial surgery performed, e.g. lower-lid shortening and eyebrow suspension. The mean follow-up duration is 14.1 months (range 2 months - 10 years). The mean weight of the implants is 1.36 grams, and the mostly used implant weighs 1.4 grams (range 0.8 - 2.1 grams). Results: In 78 patients (79.6%) good eyelid closure and relief of symptoms were achieved. In 6 patients (6.1%) the gold weight was removed because of recovery of their paralysis. There were 4 extrusions (4.1%), 2 occurring in the same patient. Four patients (4.1%) developed infection. Three times (3.1%) migration occurred. In 8 patients (8.2%), the weights inserted turned out to be too heavy and 6 of them had lighter weights implanted. Three patients (3.1%) had the weights replaced more deeply. One patient (1.0%) reacted with a gold-allergy, thus the weight was removed, coated with hydroxy-apatite and re-implanted with excellent results. Conclusions: Eight out of 98 patients (8.2%) developed complications as extrusion, infection and migration. One patient (1.0%) reacted with a gold allergy. No relationship can be shown between the complication rate and other performed surgery such as eyebrow suspension and lower eyelid shortening. Upper lid loading with custom-made gold weights to correct paralytic lagophthalmos is a reliable and successful technique.
