N2  - Background and Objectives. Either open or closed (conservative) reduction is performed when treating the mandibular condylar process fractures. In order to evaluate the clinical results of open reduction, the authors conducted a review of the mandibular condylar process fractures that had been treated at their institution via posterior mandibular ramus skin incision - the retromandibular approach - using a titanium lag screw and plate system. Materials and Methods. Using patient records, the authors examined the treatment methods and results of 35 patients treated at their institution during a 3-year period. Of these, 13 patients with 15 joints were treated using the retromandibular approach. Results and/or Conclusions. Complete recovery was achieved in 11 of the 13 patients; resorption of small bone fragments was revealed by x-ray examination of the remaining 2 cases during the follow-up period. No postoperative complications, resulting from the surgical procedure (eg, facial nerve palsy or parotitis), were present. There were no postoperative complaints about functional efficacy. When compared to the traditional method, it was concluded that use of the lag screw and plate system reduces surgery time and avoids postsurgical adverse effects, such as scarring and facial palsy. The authors recommend provisionally that maxillomandibular fixation be performed even when satisfactory occlusion is achieved.
