N2  - The surgical manipulation of tissues inevitably causes damage to the cells, micro and even macro anatomical structures. This damage is the cause of the greater part of secondary effects in facial ageing surgery. These secondary effects are particularly notorious and undesirable in the middle third: alterations of mimicry, modification of the shape of the eye, problems with lachrymal drainage and lubrication of the eyeball, anomalous cutaneous folds, dystonias, persistent oedema and even acceleration of the normal ageing process. In this article we describe the anatomy of the middle third of the face, particularly of the orbicularis muscle and its functions, we analyse briefly the physiopathology of facial ageing and illustrate how some surgical tecniques can affect the physiology and even the normal ageing process of the tissues. In order to understand and explain the secondary effects of surgery in the short, median and long term, it is not sufficient to have knowledge of anatomy and physiology, but it is also necessary to understand the physiopathology of ageing tissues.
