N2  - Facial nerve schwannomas are slow-growing tumors that may involve any part of the facial nerve. When they present with moderate to total facial palsy, complete resection is clearly indicated. However, in cases with mild or no facial dysfunction, the best course of treatment is less obvious. A series of 22 patients with facial nerve schwannoma is presented, of whom 12 underwent definitive excision and 10 were managed more conservatively. The best postoperative facial function in the group who had tumor removal was a House-Brackmann grade III, while 8 of the conservatively treated group had normal facial function up to 10 years after presentation. As well, no significant tumor growth was noted on serial radiologic imaging of those being observed. Delaying surgical resection of facial nerve schwannomas may allow patients to retain normal facial function indefinitely.
