N2  - We report on a case of orbital apex syndrome with optic neuritis and oculomotor palsy in the left eye, which followed a posterior scleral thickening in the right eye. The patient was a 34-year-old female who had complaints of epiphora, near visual disturbances and visual field defects in her right eye on admission. The posterior scleral thickening was detected in the right eye at the initial examination. Oculomotor palsy occurred after 3 days and optic neuritis after 11 days in the left eye. An inflammatory lesion in the left orbital apex was detected using MRI. Her symptoms improved after treatment with systemic corticosteroids. However, right facial nerve palsy and scleral thickening occurred after 8 months. Although the cause of her multiphase multiple lesions was not clearly detected, it was thought that they were related to systemic imflammations, such as sarcoidosis, malignant lymphoma or Churg-Strauss syndrome.
