N2  - Hyperbaric oxygen therapy is of significant benefit in the setting of an ischemic flap of the head and neck. Mechanistically, hyperbaric oxygen decreases local tissue edema while improving oxygen delivery to the compromised flap. Experimentally, both capillary and fibroblast ingrowth occur at a greater rate with hyperbaric oxygen therapy, and there is an increase in the tensile strength of the wound. Reperfusion injury also is blunted. Additional indications in the head and neck include traumatic composite amputations, necrotizing infections of the head and neck, and osteoradionecrosis of the facial skeleton. Complications of hyperbaric oxygen therapy are rare, especially if sessions are limited to 90 minutes at no greater than 2.5 ATM. Prospective randomized studies may demonstrate additional usefulness for hyperbaric oxygen, including acute hearing loss, tinnitus, and facial palsy.
