N2  - Rhinocerebral mucormycosis is a rare and often fatal fungal infection. Until now, reconstruction of necrotic tissue due to mucormycosis has not been reported. This report describes the treatment of mucormycosis and the subsequent reconstruction of the intraoral and intranasal defects due to the mucormycosis invasion. A 57-year-old man suffering from diabetes mellitus visited our hospital with complaints of facial swelling, right facial palsy and right exophthalmos. A diagnosis of rhinocerebral mucormycosis was determined by biopsy of the palate mucosa. Curative treatment for the mucormycosis consisted of the intravenous and local administration of amphotericin B, and surgical debridement. During the treatment, complete necrosis of the alveolar process of the maxilla and hard palate ensued. Therefore, the patient suffered dysfunction in chewing, swallowing and his speech. In August 1998, the patient's maxilla and hard palate were reconstructed using a radial forearm osteocutaneous flap. Prior to the reconstruction, all remaining scar tissue was excised widely. The skin island was folded in order to cover both the oral side and the nasal side. Additionally, the radius was grafted to support an artificial denture. Function and facial contour have improved postoperatively and the Mucormycosis has not recurred during a two-year follow-up period.
