N2  - A 48-year-old man complained of oscillopsia three months following a brainstem hemorrhage, located predominantly in the lower pontine tegmentum on the right side. On examination, he showed a 2 Hz-vertical ocular myoclonus (OM), which was prominent in the primary position but decreased on vertical gaze. Other neurological findings were an atypical one-and-a-harf syndrome, right peripheral facial palsy and a slight right hemiparesis as well as marked truncal and left limb ataxia. Initially, palatal myoclonus (PM) was not noted, but appeared about eight months after onset of the stroke. Cranial MRI demonstrated inferior olivary hypertrophy showing a high-intensity signal on T2 and proton-weighted images, in addition to an old hematoma located from the midline to the right in the lower pontine tegmentum. OM was dramatically suppressed with the administration of sodium valproate (1200 mg/day) and trihexyphenidyl (6 mg/day), although PM persisted. It is conceivable that oculopalatal myoclonus (OPM) in the present case might be caused by the deafferentation of the inferior olivary nucleus due to the injury of the central tegmental tract, or a lesion of cell groups in the paramedian tracts, mainly located in the median region of the caudal pons and medulla.
