N2  - Facial nerve paralysis, as a result of trauma or surgery, exacts a dramatic physical and emotional toll from patients. Loss of facial nerve function represents the most outwardly noticeable cranial neuropathy, often leading to social isolation and reduced self-image. Attempts to restore normal facial nerve function after traumatic injury have been unsatisfactory; however, marked improvements in facial tone, symmetry at rest, protection of eye exposure, and voluntary motion can be achieved. This article reviews approaches to the paralyzed facial nerve as a result of trauma or surgery. New variations on longstanding techniques such as temporalis muscle transposition and hypoglossal-facial nerve repair have limited donor morbidity. Particular advances have been made recently using innervated microvascular free muscle transfer and a critical review of their role is presented. © 2001 Lippincott Williams & Wilkins, Inc.
