N2  - Objectives: Over the past few years there has been a change in the signs and symptoms of acute mastoiditis. The aim of the present study was to investigate this change in symptoms and incidence. Patients: The records of 84 children who underwent mastoid surgery for acute mastoiditis over a 20-year period (1979-1998) were retrospectively investigated. Results and Conclusions: Taking into consideration the decline in the birth rate in the East German countries after 1991, there was no significant change in the incidence of acute mastoiditis. The mean age of patients was 2.6 years. The classical symptoms like retroauricular swelling, tenderness and protrusion of the auricle were only noted in 34 children (40.5%). Preoperative complications included facial palsy, abscess and septical lateral sinus thrombosis in 9 children. Bacterial cultures from samples obtained during surgery yielded Streptococcus pneumoniaein 56%. Therapy consisted of early surgical intervention combined with antibiotics, mainly cephalosporins. Copyright © 2001 S. Karger AG, Basel.
