N2  - Having found a toxi-infection by B. Pertussis in the 95,47% out of 92 patients affected by Multiple Sclerosis (MS) (1), and expecting to find "negative" the patients with neuropathies different from Multiple Sclerosis, as a comparison/check I searched for anti-pertussis antibodies in 10 neurologic patients whom MS had been excluded (the clinic diagnosis were: Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis, Pseudobulbar Syndrome, Motoneuron Disease, Neurogenous Muscolar Dystrophy; Hemiparkinson, Spastic Tetraparesis, Balo's Concentric Sclerosis). In all these patients NRM had excluded the presence of patches; in two of them it had highlighted a gliosis. The research of Anti-Bordetella antibodies, I asked for, demonstrated that in all the cases there was a "taking place" infection by B. Pertussis. Referring to the role of astrocytes in the induction of experimental allergic encephalitis and in the onset of MS (see: my Internet site: www.domenicofiore.it), revaluing the metabolic and immunologic function of astrocytes in CNS's physiopathologies, keeping in mind the pathogenic power of the main pertussis toxins and the laws of general biology which regulate the relationships between Bordetella and Human Host, in the case of reinfections; one can explain the pathogenesis of pertussis neuropathies without patches. In subjects with a muco-ciliar barrier defect, a re-infection from Bordetella may cause: A) in people with astrocytes which produce HLA-Ags of II class: the Multiple Sclerosis; B) in people with astrocytes which do not produce HLA-Ags of II class, according to the relationship toxins/antibodies (zone phenomenon, Arthus reaction) and according to the phenotype (that is, according to the metabolic and receptor features) of the neurons involved and preferentially damaged by pertussis toxins, the various clinic forms of the following without patches neuropathies: Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis, Pseudobulbar Syndrome, Motoneuron Disease, Neurogenous Muscolar Dystrophy; Hemiparkinson, Spastic Tetraparesis, Balo's Concentric Sclerosis. In all neuropathies which cannot be etiologically qualified, as the prognosis can be positively modified (quoad vitam and quoad valetudinem), thanks to a precocious etiologic diagnosis and to the subsequent specific antibiotic therapy, it becomes compulsory to search also for anti-Bordetella antibodies with appropriate methods.
