N2  - On the evaluation in a child with facial paralysis an ENT doctor should follow the same diagnostic procedures as used in adults, but will find great difficulties which are related with children inability to co-operate and also with some etiologic-pathogenic peculiarities which are typical on this age. Therefore for a precocious diagnosis it's really important to take a reliable history before the physical examination, which will be carried out with some shrewdness. The facial paralysis in children can be divided into congenital and acquired. Among the acquired paralysis the most frequent is the Bell's idiopathic although is represented in a lower percentage than in adults. Other acquired paralysis are infectious-inflammatory, traumatic or tumor- related paralysis. The proper investigations for assessing facial paralysis in children are not different from adults but, may find the lack of cooperation really inconvenient so often need to sedate them. The surgical treatment of facial paralysis has limited application in children because the presence of congenital forms, so on the time of diagnosis there is already an irreversible compromised function of the nerve.
