N2  - We have developed a method of single-stage latissimus dorsi transplantation with microneurovascular anastomoses for the treatment of long-standing facial paralysis. This method is effective for patients with incomplete paralysis, as well as for patients with complete paralysis. There are several technical key points to bear in mind when performing this procedure. When dissecting the subcutaneous facial layer for the graft, it is important to take care not to impair the normal residual movements of the face. The thickness of the latissimus dorsi muscle should be tailored to less than 1cm. The graft should be attached with no tension in the face. The branch to the zygomatic major muscle should be selected as the donor nerve in the contralateral side of the face. We have applied this single-stage latissimus dorsi transplantation in 32 cases of incomplete facial paralysis and have followed up these cases for more than 1 year. In 25 of the cases, well-balanced movements of the grafted muscle was recognized, and therefore, satisfactory results were obtained. We do not think that there is any reason to hesitate to apply this method to incomplete paralysis patients, as long as attention is paid to the key technical points.
