N2  - Only 6% of basal skull fractures are isolated, whereas in most cases (94%) they are extensions of skull vault fractures. Frontal or occipital impact products a fracture running along the sagittal plane whereas injury to the vertex causes a fracture running on the coronal plane. The symptoms of basal skull fracture are related to the site of injury. Anterior basal injury gives rise to anosmia, periorbital haematoma and CSF rhinorrhoea, whereas posterior fractures cause bleeding through the external auditory meatus, hypacusia,vestibular dysfunction, peripheral paralysis of the facial nerve and CSF otorrhoea. CSF fistula is much more common in patients with basal fractures (11%) than those with generalised head injury (3%). The incidence of CSF rhinorrhoea is much greater in fractures of the median anterior basal skull: high resolution CT coronal scanning with a thickness of 1-3 mm is the investigation of choice and can disclose the presence of fractures in 70% of cases. In the remainder, MR cisternography, cisterno-CT or radionuclide cisternography can be used- Carotid cavernous fistulae or pseudo-aneurysms of the internal carotid artery may be the result of basal skull fractures involving the intrapetrous or intracavernous segments of the artery. Endovascular treatment is the method of choice in both cases. Lastly, injury to the posterior basal skull may cause fractures of the temporal bone: the most common are longitudinal fractures (70-90%) running along the main axis of the petrous bone with a lateral course to the auditory capsule, often causing transmissive hypacusia (due to involvement of the tympanum or ossicular complex) and sometimes peripheral paralysis of the facial nerve (20% of cases). Less common (10-30%), but more severe, are transverse fractures running orthogonally to the main axis of the petrous bone causing neurosensory hypacusia (due to involvement of the cochlea), vertigo, nystagmus or CSF otorrhoea (due to involvement of the semicircular canals) and lastly paralysis of the facial nerve (50% of cases).
