N2  - Objective: To report a patient with an unusual presentation of central nervous system sarcoidosis. Methods: We present a case report of a woman who had a recurrence of a pituitary mass 8 to 10 years after the initial diagnosis. Results: A 46-year-old African-American woman presented with headache, right-sided paresthesias, slurred speech, facial asymmetry and unsteady gait. MRI showed a pituitary mass with inhomogeneous enhancement and a thickened pituitary stalk. Laboratory evaluation revealed central hypothyroidism. Biopsy of the mass showed extensive atrophy of the pituitary tissue, chronic inflammatory cells and numerous noncaseating epithelial granulomas. Subsequently, she developed partial central diabetes insipidus. Conclusion: Central nervous system sarcoidosis, though a rare cause of pituitary mass, should be considered in the differential diagnosis, especially in the presence of a thickened stalk.
