N2  - Tuberculosis rarely affects the middle ear cleft primarily and therefore in the absence of a pulmonary lesion the diagnosis is often delayed resulting in various complications, such as irreversible hearing loss, facial nerve paralysis or intracranial complications. Even though, it's incidence is considered to be low it is probable that it's incidence is not the same worldwide and may vary from country to country, depending on a number of factors such as socio-economic status, under -diagnosis and under-reporting. The amazing feature of middle ear tuberculosis is the change that has occurred in the clinical behavior. The description given by various authors several years ago differs, considerably from the one found today. Most of the cases present to us in an advanced stage with complications due to misdiagnosis and therefore failure to start antituberculous treatment. In this paper, we present a case of an 11 year old child with advanced primary tuberculosis of the middle ear cleft with a huge cerebral abscess, who required gastrostomy to improve his nutritional status as he was only 15 kg in weight, which was closed after one month when his general condition improved. He underwent burrhole surgery with aspiration of abscess followed by a canal wall down tympanomastoidectomy under the cover of anti-tuberculous treatment.
