N2  - OK-432 therapy is performed as a first choice of therapy for lymphangioma because it provides good results without serious complications. However, when OK-432 therapy is not effective, surgical excision becomes necessary. The surgical excision of lymphangioma has two major problems, postoperative facial palsy and recurrence of the tumor. It is difficult to remove giant lymphangioma completely, because the tumor often involves the facial nerve. However, the incomplete excision of the tumor invites recurrence of the lesion. We report two cases of giant lymphangioma in the face that did not respond to OK-432 therapy. In both cases, the tumor was safely removed after the facial nerves were exposed. However, it was impossible to remove the tumor completely because the tumor had invaded the facial nerve and deep tissue. The two cases did not show tumor recurrence.
