N2  - Fifty patients of hydrocephalus, managed over a period of 5 years are analyzed. Most of the children were in the age group of 1 month to 1 year, with congenital hydrocephalus being the commonest cause. Significant number of patients also had associated meningocele or encephalocele. Cranial ultrasound was the most useful investigation for diagnosis. Ventriculoperitoneal shunt was the primary procedure done in all patients. Fifteen patients had shunt related complications, blocked shunts being the most common. Shunt revision was done on 10 occasions (6 patients) and one patient each underwent ventriculoatrial and ventriculo-cholecystic shunts.
