N2  - Vascular malformations are treated by surgical resection, arterial embolization or sclerotherapy. If complete excision is possible, surgical resection offers definitive results. However, radical excision is often limited in facial lesions because of postoperative problems such as facial paralysis, deformities, or poor cosmetic results. Although sclerotherapy is superior to surgical resection for these reasons, there may be suspicions about its safety or its efficacy. The effectiveness of sclerotherapy depends on intralesional hemodynamics, which are at present divided into only two groups : high-flow and low-flow. The authors describe their pretherapeutic evaluation of flow characteristics of vascular malformations, and subdivide venous malformations into intermediate type and low-flow type by means of different patterns in their time-activity curves obtained by radionuclide angiography. Patient management was determined according to this hemodynamic classification. The authors recommend the combination of arterial embolization and sclerotherapy for high-flow type and intermediate type venous malformations. In this paper, we also describe our procedure of percutaneous injection of a sclerosing agent, ethanolamine oleate, guided by digital subtraction angiography, and present two clinical cases. The prevention of complications and effectiveness of the sclerotherapy are discussed.
