N2  - We report the case of a 65-year old man with non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (NHL) not only in the brachial plexus but also in the central nervous systems and parotid gland. He was referred to our hospital for evaluation of a right parotid mass. He also presented with bilateral facial palsy and paralysis of the left superior limb. Computed tomography scan and magnetic resonance imaginf revealed mass lesions in the right parapharyngeal space, the deep lobe of the right parotid gland, and the left brachial plexus. A gallium-67 citrate scan demostrated abnormal uptake in the leftbrachial plexus. These symptoms anfd lesions improved during steroid therapy. However, the symptoms worsened again after steroid therapy was discontinued. We performed a right parotidectomy to confirm the diagnosis. Histopathological study revealed NHL. He was treated eith combination chemotherapy, ang most of the lesions and symptoms except bilateral facial palsy, improved. Despite follow-up treatment, a brain metastasis occured, and he died 16 months after the onset of symptoms.
