N2  - Peripheral facial paralyses are often related to the dissemination of an infectious agent. The epidemiological studies of the a frigore paralysis, form most frequently met, a viral aetiology, implying particularly the Herpes-1 virus. The standard treatment for Bell's palsy is a corticotherapy 1 mg/kg/day prednisone for 10 days, aiming at reducing the inflammatory oedema responsible for the suffering of the facial nerve as well as the associated immune reaction. Following the highlighting of the viral aetiology, trials with anti-viral treatments were implemented. Available studies show the interest association to aciclovir (400 mg five times per day) and prednisone. This association proves to be higher than the single use of prednisone, but the comparative single use treatments of aciclovir and prednisone is in favour of the prednisone treatment. The use of antiviral drug in Bell's palsy seems justified, because of a faster recovery of the sensibility as well as motor functions, but especially by a less significant number of after- effects.
