N2  - Idiopathic facial paralysis is believed to be caused by a virally mediated cranial neuritis. Minimum diagnostic criteria include paralysis or paresis of all muscle groups on one side of the face, sudden onset, and absence of CNS disease; the diagnosis is made after other causes of acute peripheral palsy have been excluded. Variable manifestations include otalgia, hyperacusis, decreased tearing, dysgeusia, facial or retroauricular pain, and other cranial neuropathies. Audiometric examination (but usually not CT or MRI) is indicated for all patients. Corticosteroid and acyclovir therapy are given routinely; most patients recover completely with or without treatment.
