N2  - We report on a patient who developed a severe peripheral facial paresis 16 days after a closed traumatic brain injury including longitudinal fracture of the petrous bone. Symptoms regressed partially within two weeks without neurosurgical treatment. As other causes of peripheral facial paresis could be excluded, local edema or disturbances of arterious or venous circulation were supposed to be the pathogenetical causes of paresis. Our case report confirms the assumption of a favourable prognosis of >>late<< peripheral facial paresis treated conservatively.
