N2  - Temporary complications following an inferior alveolar nerve block are reported. A 31-year-old female patient consulted our hospital with pain in lower right third molar. Her medical history was arrhythmia (paroxymal tachycardia). Drug therapy had been used and her preoperative ECG was within the normal range. A right inferior alveolar nerve block was performed by using 2% lidocaine with no epinephrine. Within 5 minutes, the patient complained of not being able to close her right eye and pain. Protection against drying may be accomplished by simply closing the eye and covering the eye with wet gauze. Fifteen minutes after injection, the patient became able to close her eye, and the blink reflex appeared to be normal. The symptoms observed in this case seemed to be caused by the direct action of the local anesthetic on the facial nerve. The relationship between the parotid gland and the facial nerve (Fig. 1) may play an important role in understanding the cause of this facial paralysis. The cause of facial nerve within the substance of the parotid grand is interest. In the parotid grand, the facial nerve trunk divides into two divisions. Upper division of facial nerve are R temporalis and R zygomaticus. Lower division are R buccalis and R marginalis mandibulae and R colli. If the needle inject too deeply, penetrate the parotid grand. Once in the parotid grand, the anesthetic may diffuse readily and come in contact with the upper division of facial nerve and result in the symptoms. When such cases do occur, the patient should be reassured, comforted, and made to understand that the symptoms will generally disappear in an hour or less. It is important to protect the conjunctiva from drying.
