N2  - We report about an eleven-year-old boy suffering from facial nerve palsy. He presented with sudden otalgia, headache and herpetic vesicles in the right auricle. The magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan showed a high concentration of the applied contrast agent in the region of the facial and auditory nerves. The cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) showed signs of inflammation; lymphocytic pleocytosis, activated lymphocytic cell forms and intrathecal immunglobuline synthesis. We identified the disease as one form of Varicella-Zoster virus infection, better known as Zoster oticus. Also known as the RAMSAY-HUNT syndrome, the disease is very rarely described during childhood. The treatment today is intravenous injection of Acyclovir. In our case we saw a nearly complete recovery.
