N2  - Early diagnosis and appropriate treatment of traumatic facial nerve paralysis can lead to a favorable result. The prognosis or facial nerve recovery depends upon early identification of facial nerve injury, localization of the site of injury, treatment of the affected site and close follow-up examinations. Intratemporal injuries occur as a result of longitudinal temporal bone fractures, often affecting the perigeniculate region. In transverse temporal bone fractures the site of injury may be the labyrinthine, internal auditory canal, or segments. Depending on the time of onset and degree of paralysis, surgery may or may not be indicated. Facial nerve recovery from an extratemporal injury also depends on early diagnosis and repair.
