N2  - The case history of a patient with orofacial oedema is presented, together with the findings on extensive investigation. A diagnosis of Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome (MRS) was made. This case demonstrated the difficulties in diagnosis in monosymptomatic cases, and the importance of further investigation (EMG). MRS is a rare condition with a triad of symptoms: orofacial oedema, facial palsy, and plicated or 'scrotal' tongue. The pathogenesis is unknown. The vital prognosis is good. Treatment has not been specific. It relies on local (intra-lesional) and systemic steroids, and elective eyelid surgery. Clofamizine has proved an effective alternative treatment.
