N2  - Our objective was to propose a strategy to screen HIV-infected African people for biological immunodeficiency easily. In a cross-sectional study, we analysed the patterns of diseases and of CD4 counts among 266 HIV-infected adults. peripheral facial paralysis and chronic cutaneo-mucous diseases were the earlier B-stage diseases. Pulmonary tuberculosis was close to B-stage diseases, and chronic diarrhoea was borderline between B and C stages. Cachexia was the most frequent C-stage symptom (47.8%). Ninety per cent of CDC-C stage people had CD4 counts below 350/μl, whereas only 75% had CD4 counts below 200/μl. Regression analysis identified the lymphocyte count, clinical stage and platelet count as predictors of CD4 count below 350/μl. A simple score (lymphocyte count ≤2500/μl and clinical stage ≤B) is proposed to determine this CD4 threshold (positive predictive value: 83%) and to determine those patients needing treatment to prevent wasting and opportunistic infections.
