N2  - Lyme borreliosis is caused by the spirochaete Borrelia burgdorferi and is transmitted by ticks. The disease most frequently affects the skin, the central and peripheral nerve system, the heart and the joints. Three clinical stages can be distinguished: The main clinical feature of stage I is the erythema migrans. Stage II is characterized by a disseminated infection of the nerve system often with meningoradiculoneuritis (Bannwarth's syndrome) and facial palsy and of the heart and the musculoskeletal system with arthralgia and myalgia. Stage III of Lyme borreliosis can occur months to years after infection and affects the skin with acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans or large joints, especially the knees with chronic Lyme arthritis. Involvement of both central and peripheral nerve system may also persist in stage III. In stage I, Lyme borreliosis is commonly treated with oral antibiotics such as doxycycline, minocycline, amoxycillin, cefuroxime, ceftriaxone and azithromycin; disseminated infection in stage II and the late stage III may require i.m. treatment with ceftriaxone or intravenous treatment with ceftriaxone, cefotaxime of penicillin G.
