N2  - This paper has discussed the peripheral and central mechanisms of the various neuropathic orofacial pain conditions. To make an accurate diagnosis of head and neck pain and render treatment, all organ systems need to be considered and evaluated. Peripheral and central sensitization have been discussed as they relate to neuropathic pain. It is suggested that treatment of these disorders be problem oriented, addressing both peripheral and central mechanism if present. A discussion was presented of peripheral sensitization with a rationale for peripheral interventions. The CN neuropathies were discussed with differential diagnostic criteria and treatment strategies. Neuropathic pain that does not respond to topical or local agents is more profoundly centralized. These conditions will need to be treated with centrally acting agents. In addition, if there is no response to medications, sympathetic involvement needs to be ruled out with sympathetic ganglion blockade.
