N2  - A 63-year-old male with diabetes mellitus had 4 episodes of diabetic ophthalmoplegia and 1 episode of facial nerve palsy during a 10-year period. Most attacks were accompanied by frontal headache and the abducens and oculomotor nerves were most often affected. Ophthalmoplegia and facial nerve palsy improved within 6 months without any specific treatment. The episodes of cranial nerve palsy in this case seemed to have no close relationship to the patient's control of blood sugar level, but they were associated with other diabetic neuropathies such as disorders of the autonomic and peripheral nervous systems. Reports of diabetic patients with recurrent cranial nerve palsy, such as was seen in this case, are rare.
