N2  - Prostheses can be used to augment the facial contours of patients with facial paralysis. These prostheses offer the advantages of restoring fullness and proper contour to the cheek, preventing trauma to the buccal mucosa during mastication, and stretching the cheek, which may indirectly improve oral hygiene in the buccal sulcus area. Most of the prostheses currently in use for augmenting the facial contour in patients with facial paralysis are removable partial denture type prostheses. To be successful these prostheses require permanent alterations of the teeth in the form of guiding planes, rest preparations, and undercuts and patient compliance. This article describes the technique of fabricating prostheses that are retained on the teeth by orthodontic bands to augment the facial contour of patients with facial paralysis. This type of prosthesis eliminates problems with patient compliance and does not require permanent alteration of the teeth. The prosthesis offers the advantages of improved facial contour and prevention of lip and cheek biting. These prostheses are also inexpensive and easy to fabricate.
