N2  - Eighty-nine cases of parotid neoplasms (77 benign and 12 malignant tumors) observed between 1978 and 1994 are reported. The incidence of facial nerve injuries and tumor recurrence with regard to histology and type of surgical resection is evaluated. Thirty minor excisions (atypical resections, enucleations), 44 superficial parotid lobectomies, 11 total parotidectomies with preservation of the facial nerve and 3 radical total parotidectomies with sacrifice of the facial nerve were performed. Postoperative morbidity, including salivary fistulas (0-4.5%), hematomas (0-9%) and Frey's syndrome (0-33%), was not significantly related to type of surgical resection. Results. Transitory facial nerve paralysis (healed within 6 months) was more frequent after total parotidectomy (54%) than after minor excision (17%) or superficial lobectomy (18%), (χ2 2 x 3 = 7.568; p < 0.023). Permanent facial nerve paralysis (lasting more than 6 months) was more common after total parotidectomy (18%) than after superficial lobeotomy (0%) or minor excision (3%), (χ2 2 x 3 = 8.549, p < 0.014). This is partly due to total parotidectomy performed for recurrent tumors in 4 cases out of 11. No permanent facial nerve paralysis was observed in 7 patiens in which total parotidectomy was the initial treatment of neoplasm. Permanent facial nerve paralysis was much more frequent when resection was performed for recurtent tumors (29% vs 1.3%), (Fisher's test: p < 0.027). Incidence of transitory and permanent facial nerve paralysis observed after minor excision or superficial lobectomy was not significantly different. On the other hand, benign tumors recurrence was more common after minor excision (9%) than after superficial lobectomy (0%). In particular, mixed tumors showed recurrence more often after minor excision than after superficial lobectomy (respectively: 6.7% and 0%). Conclusions. The conclusions is drawn that superficial lobectomy is the best treatment of all benign parotid neoplasms, because it implies a low risk of facial nerve injury and a better radicality. Tumor recurrence was the main factor conditioning risk of facial nerve injury.
