N2  - The purpose of this study was to discuss the main characteristics of pneumococcal acute otitis media (AOM) in children and the antimicrobial resistance of the strains that were isolated from ear cultures during one year (1-10-95 to 30-9-96). There were 113 cases during this period, 11 of which required hospitalization. The cultures were performed with conventional methods and the antibiotic susceptibility with Kirby-Bauer method and MIC (with E-test) for penicillin (additionally to oxacillin disk testing). Thirteen isolates were found intermediately (11,5%) and 5 highly resistant (4,4%) to penicillin. The children with resistant strains were mostly under 18 months old. The resistance rates to other antimicrobials was: cefaclor 5,3%, erythromycin 9,7%, cotrimoxazole 8,8%, clindamycin 3,5%, tetracycline 8%, chloramphenicol 1,8%, vancomycin, rifampicin and cefotaxime 0%. The predominating serogroups/serotypes of the resistant to penicillin strains were the 19, 9, 23, 6 and 14 and of the susceptible strains were the 19, 14, 3, 6 and 9. The complications that were noticed were: ear-drum perforation (84 children), mastoiditis (4), facial nerve paresis (1), bacteremia and septic arthritis (2). Conclusions: The S. pneumoniae isolates from AOM in children show reduced sensitivity to penicillin in a high proportion. The serotypes of the resistant strains are included in the vaccine which is now available, but its efficacy is poor for children aged under 2 years and to immunocompromised ones.
