N2  - Background: The lymphoma which derives from mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) is a subtype of non-Hodgkin lymphomas. Methods: We describe a patient who was initially diagnosed as suffering from Sjogren syndrome. Because of the development of a bilateral monstrous swelling of her parotid glands, she underwent complete parotidectomy on her right side and was irradiated on the left side after receiving the final diagnosis. On this basis, it is possible to compare surgery and radiation therapy in the same patient. Results: The advantages of surgery are complete resection of the tumor and the absence of xerostomia and mucositis caused by irradiation. Radiotherapy produces neither a scar nor an indentation in the parotid region. Since more than 2 years, there is no recurrence of disease on both sides. Conclusion: Patients with bilateral swelling of the parotid gland and suspicion of a MALT lymphoma should receive an open biopsy with facial nerve monitoring and consecutive irradiation.
