N2  - Sagittal split mandibular osteotomy has become a common maxillofacial surgical procedure, but complications occasionally result. Infraalveolar nerve palsy has frequently been reported, although facial nerve palsy has been reported only 12 times since 1972. We report a case of facial nerve palsy following sagittal split mandibular osteotomy and review the literature. A 19-year-old-man who had cleft lip and palate underwent sagittaI split mandibular osteotomy (modified Obwegeser-Dalpont technique) and genioplasty to correct malocclusion and facial asymmetry. When horizontal osteotomy of the ramus was performed, the chisel hit the parotid grand and came into the proximity of the mastoid process because a retractor slipped from the lateral border of the ramus. Facial nerve palsy of the left side occurred on the first postoperative day. Temporal branch palsy improved slightly 2 weeks after surgery, but buccal branch and marginal mandibular branch palsy remained. Full recovery from facial palsy was observed by 6 months after surgery without treatment. It was suspected that the cause of facial nerve palsy was the direct local myelin damage by the chisel. Therefore the importance of care during surgery is emphasized.
