N2  - Bell's palsy is an acute, unilateral, lower motor neuron paralysis of the seventh cranial nerve. Principal manifestations are loss of facial expression and of voluntary facial and scalp movement on the affected side, facial numbness, excessive tearing of and inability to dose the ipsilateral eye, and pain behind the ear. Variable manifestations include loss of taste, hyperacusis, and dryness of the ipsilateral eye. Although corticosteroid therapy is given routinely, 80% to 85% of patients with Bell's palsy recover completely without treatment. More recent evidence implicates herpes simplex virus as the chief cause of this disorder; treatment with acyclovir plus corticosteroids may prove beneficial.
