N2  - Primary tumours of the facial nerve are relatively uncommon and frequently misdiagnosed as a consequence of their subtle and widely variable clinical manifestations. Slowly progressive facial palsy remains the most frequent presenting symptom. Advances in temporal bone imaging using high resolution CT and MRI technologies have led to an increase in preoperative diagnosis. The treatment is complete microsurgical removal at a multidisciplinary centre. The options for facial nerve reconstruction include interposing a cable nerve graft, performing a re-routing procedure of the facial nerve with primary anastomosis, or facial hypoglossal anastomosis. The aim of this paper is to contrast the spectrum of clinical features and radiological findings of these patients and to discuss their management strategies and the facial nerve outcome in relation to a review of the literature.
