N2  - Malignant lymphoma of the temporal bone is very rare. A 66 year old woman complained of right ear fullness, otalgia, facial palsy, dizziness, dysphagia and hoarseness. The clinical examination revealed a gray and fragile tumor obstructing the right external auditory meatus, and extensive cranial nerve palsies on the right side like Garcin syndrome. CT scan and MRI showed a large soft tissue mass extremely destructing the right temporal bone. Pathological diagnosis was malignant lymphoma (B cell, diffuse large cell, immunoblastic type). A clinical staging work up presented no other source of lymphoma, that corresponded to stage I of the Ann Arbor's classification. So, radiation therapy was done for 50Gy. However, the lymphoma disseminated, though primary tumor partially reduced and resulted in 4 months survival following the treatment.
