N2  - We review differential diagnosis and therapy of facial palsy. The most common disease is Bell's palsy, the cause of which is unknown. Facial paralysis may also occur in acute polyradiculoneuritis (Guillain-Barre syndrome). Further differential diagnoses include infectious causes, tumours, granulomas, toxic and vascular lesions. Therapy of Bell's palsy includes protection of the eye and administration of glucocorticosteroids, which are thought to decrease swelling of the nerve in its tight canal. Unfortunately, well-designed controlled studies are not available. More than 80% of patients recover within a few weeks. Early denervation is an unfavourable prognostic sign and may result in aberrant reinnervation.
