N2  - The EMG feedback technique was used for re-eduction of damaged functions in subjects with facial palsy. Therapeutic results were observed during 5 weeks in 14 patients with facial palsy and compared to those obtained in a control group treated by a conventional rehabilitation method. The efficacy of EMG feedback therapy was statistically proved by measurable EMG parameters: amplitude of motor unit potentials and conduction velocity. During 24 sessions, 12 patients made a considerable improvement in the amplitude of motor unit potentials. The innervation pattern and the ability to sustain contraction improved in all patients. Also, the conduction velocity increased and the frequency of unwanted movements decreased in the patients with synkinesis. This study helped in pointing to an objective method far the control of clinical improvement in subjects with facial palsy.
