N2  - The authors report the case of a 77-year-old male with multiple injuries of the face and hand as a result of bear bites. He presented a puncture wound of the oral cavity, and lacerations of the face, as well as maxillary and metacarpal bone fractures. The wounds were washed with saline, after which antibiotic therapy was provided. The metacarpal bone fracture was set and fixed by wiring. As for his facial wounds, since bits of skin had been torn away, facial nerve injuries were suspected but could not be immediately verified. All wounds healed without infection, but slight facial palsy and a sensory dullness of the left mandible were the squelae, of this incident. In cases of facial injuries from an animal bite, it is important that infection, be prevented, so that such wounds should be thoroughly washed, given debridement, if needed, and antibiotic therapy.
