N2  - Diagnosis of Lyme disease in sometimes difficult, particularly in those countries where the circulation of B. burgdorferi is sporadic. The authors report six cases of infection (4 male, 2 female; age 19-65 years) observed during five years in Piedmont (north-western Italy), a region where the disease is considered extremely rare if not absent altogether. For each patient the clinical features, serological picture and therapeutic approach are discussed thoroughly. A wide range of clinical symptoms, ranging from typical ECM to polyarthritis, facial palsy, meningoencephalitis and retinal vasculitis was observed in the patients. From their data the authors conclude that the true incidence of Lyme disease is probably underestimated in Italy, and that this diagnosis should always be considered in patients presenting with a typical picture even in non-endemic areas, taking into account both the wide possibilities of treatment offered today and the severe complications of untreated disease.
