N2  - The aggressive and erosive properties of cholesteatoma can lead to life-threatening complications despite its benign histology. These complications still occur despite all attempts by otolaryngologists to encourage early detection and treatment. The present study reviews three recent life-threatening or severe complications of cholesteatoma including a large cerebellar abscess, a parapharyngeal abscess and facial nerve palsy. The cause in each case was a grossly invasive cholesteatoma involving die temporal bone. The variable biology of cholesteatoma sometimes leads to marked erosion of the temporal bone with involvement of the brain, neck and cranial nerves. Current research has helped us to understand the pathogenesis and behaviour of cholesteatoma, but the triggers for this intriguing disease remain unknown.
