N2  - A statistical analysis was performed on 71 cases of facial palsy. Bell's palsy comprised 73% of the total of 71 cases (Fig. 1). Three patients received dental treatment before the onset of facial palsy. Their mean age was 43 years. A large number of the patients with Bell's palsy were between 41-50 years of age. With Hunt syndrome, a large number of the patients were between 30-40 years of age (Fig. 2). The incidence of palsy did not differ significantly between the right and left side. Stellate ganglion block (SGB) was used as the therapy first choice in 64.8% of the 71 patients (Fig. 3). In the Bell's palsy, the number of times (25 ± 24) (Fig. 4), and the prognosis of Bell's palsy was better than that of Hunt syndrome (Fig. 5). In comparing of the response to and the prognosis of differences between SGB therapy and the combination of SGB and steroid therapy, there were no significant differences in age, gender, or the number of SGB (Fig. 6 Table 1). Therefore, the efficacy of the combination of SGB and steroid therapy needs further investigation. These finding suggest that the SGB therapy is the first choice in outpatients of facial palsy.
