N2  - 'Congenital' esotropia is not typically present at birth. A heritable defect of the binocular fusional reflex can result in a congenital esotropia. Surgical correction should be offered between 5 and 12 months of age. Amblyopia therapy should be performed prior to surgery, but can be safely postponed, and sometimes avoided, if initiated following surgery. Hyperopic spectacles should be attempted when the refractive error is greater than +3.00 D prior to considering surgery. A graded bilateral medial rectus recession is the preferred surgical treatment. Inferior oblique anteriorizations should be performed simultaneously with horizontal rectus surgery if preoperative inferior oblique overaction is observed. Although bifoveal fixation is possible, the monofixation syndrome is a desirable postoperative sensory outcome for patients with congenital esotropia. Congenital esotropia represents a disorder that continues to evolve after initial successful surgical alignment. Postoperative accommodative esotropia, inferior oblique overaction, dissociated vertical deviation, and consecutive exotropia may all represent a continuum of this congenital defect of binocular vision.
