N2  - Stereotactic radiotherapy (RSM) was initiated in Lyon in October 1989. The technique was adapted from the one described by Lutz and Saunders in Boston (BRW stereotactic frame). Irradiation is delivered with 18 MV photons produced by a linac (Saturne 20). From February 1990 to December 1992, eight patients with acoustic neuroma, were treated by RSM. All of them were referred to RSM because they refused surgery or it was contra-indicated. The age ranged from 31 to 72 years (mean 52 years) and tumour size from 12 to 50 mm (mean 23 mm). Fifteen to twenty Grays were delivered on the 70% isodose line with 12.5 to 30 mm collimators. Mean follow-up was fourteen months (2 to 36 months) and one patient died from intercurrent disease two months after treatment. Symptoms (tinnitus and vertigo) resolved in two patients, were reduced in three patients and stabilised in one. Radiological controls (CT scan and/or MRI) showed a stabilisation of the tumour size in six patients with central hypodensity in two and an increased tumour size in one patient. Two patients underwent surgery three and twelve months after RSM because of tumour progression or clinical deterioration. Three patients were deaf before treatment. Hearing stabilised in two patients and three patients with rapidly progressive hearing loss before RSM became deaf in a few days after treatment. One patient developed a facial palsy which cleared almost completely in six months.
