N2  - The usefulness of intramuscular BTX A unjections in patients with focal dystonias, hemifacial spasm, and other motor disorders causing disabling muscle hyperactivity is now well accepted. Recent studies have elucidated the intracellular mechanisms of action of BTX A. The neurotoxin inhibits acetylcholine release by cleaving synaptosomal-associated protein. Contraindications to the use of BTX A include the presence of diseases or treatments with drugs affecting the neuromuscular junction. Side effects mainly result from exaggerated paresis of injected or neighbouring muscle groups. In the hands of experienced clinicians BTX A injections are a safe and remarkably effective treatment.
