N2  - A seven-stage approach to the management of lagophthalmos and other ophthalmic complications of 7th nerve palsies has been used to aid the physician in logically organizing therapy and to provide the patients with reassuring goals. The seven stages include supportive care, planning for general facial reanimation, lower lid and canthal resuspension, passive upper lid animation, dynamic lid animation, and soft tissue repositioning. The final stage, termed the Epilogue, addresses the use of Botulinum A toxin in the management of the synkinetic movements and hypertonicity which frequently develop with resolution of a facial paralysis. This staged approach has been useful in achieving corneal compensation, maintaining vision, and improving the quality of life in these patients.
