N2  - Lagophthalmos is a serious functional and cosmetic disorder in patients with facial nerve palsies. It is even more difficult and urgent to treat in additional trigeminal lesions. In a series of 1000 acoustic nerve tumors, 250 cerebellopontine angle meningiomas and 200 other cerebellopontine angle tumors, many hundreds of patients transferred from other institutions, 160 patients were treated neurosurgically and a further 80 by plastic surgery for facial nerve palsies. The indication of the type of treatment depends on (1) the knowledge of facial nerve continuity or discontinuity, (2) the duration since the onset of the palsy, (3) its completeness or signs of recovery, (4) the patient's individual tendency to atrophy and on his or her age. In case of definite nerve discontinuity, immediate nerve reconstruction by nerve transplantation is necessary and technically possible along the whole course of the facial nerve from the brainstem up to the region of the parotid gland. In 61 patients nerve transplantations were carried out at the cerebellopontine angle (CPA), from the CPA to the mastoid bony canal, from the CPA to the stylomastoid foramen, from the mastoid segment to the stylomastoid foramen or to the face region or completely extracranially. In case of loss of the proximal nerve stump or of destruction of the facial nucleus within the brainstem, facial nerve reconstruction is best achieved by reanimation with a donor nerve, either the hypoglossal nerve or the contralateral facial nerve. The achievement of facial symmetry on rest, of symmetry on movement in two face segments, of complete eye closure and limited mild synkinesia (equivalent to Grade III of the House-Brackmann Scale) is regarded as a subjectively and objectively satisfactory result and is found in 74% of hypoglossal-facial reanimations and in 70 to 77% of transplantations at the CPA. Incomplete or dysfunctional recovery may be a problem in spontaneous reinnervation as well as in reconstructed facial nerves. The reasons for this and the adjuvant therapy by plastic surgery are also presented.
