N2  - A 16-year-old male had a six day history of ear pain, frontal headaches and low grade fevers and developed a left facial weakness one day prior to admission. Significant findings on examination included a left facial palsy and four vesicles over the pinna and posterior auricular area of the left ear. The CSF had 212 lymphocytes, no RBC, glucose 59 mg/dl and protein 69 mg/dl. The association of pain, facial palsy and the few vesicles in the area of the pain led to a diagnosis of herpes zoster. The patient was treated with IV acyclovir. This was followed by oral famciclovir, a new, more effective oral form of acyclovir. Because of initial deterioration, he also received steroids. He made a good recovery.
