N2  - Traditionally, corticosteroids have been used to treat a wide spectrum of head and neck problems. With the increasing association of complications, the use of these agents has dramatically declined. However, the ability of corticosteroids to significantly reduce inflammation in many clinical situations continues to make them extremely valuable. To effectively use steroids, it is essential for the clinician to understand the possible short- and long-term complications, as well as the possible benefits, for the specific situation encountered. Steroids have been proven to be beneficial in the management of sudden sensorineural hearing loss, idiopathic facial palsy, croup, nasal obstruction, tonsillectomy, and perioperative swelling. The incidence of complications with the use of steroids is associated with the length of therapy. Risk factors for steroid use include history of peptic ulcer disease, diabetes, latent tuberculosis, psychosis, and osteoporosis. Short-term therapy is rarely associated with complications in patients without these risk factors.
