N2  - The patient in this study was a 51-year-old male with sudden double vision. He had had episodes of hypertention and antecedent infection. A diagnosis of Fisher's syndrome had been made based on the absence of ocular movement, bilateral ptosis, dilated pupils, bilateral facial paralysis, loss of deep tendon reflex and ataxic gait. Corticosteroid therapy for reasons not clear at present, induced central serous chorioretinopathy in both eyes. Visual acuity recovered following decreased corticosteroid and focal retinal photocoagulation. Damage already incurred by the retinal pigment epithelium appeared to develop further and aggravate the chorioretinopathy after steroid therapy. Circulatory disturbance in the choroid induced by hypertension was apparently one cause for the disease. It was shown by the present case study that the careful administration of corticosteroid is essential to the treatment of Fisher's syndrome, based on due consideration of patient age and physical conditions.
