N2  - Statistical analysis was performed on the 176 patients with facial palsy treated at the pain clinic of Osaka Dental University Hospital between May 1977 anti August 1994. There were 84 males and 92 females (Fig. 2). Their mean age was 43.7 years, and most of the patients were between 20 to 69 years of age (Fig. 3). The affected side was the left in 80 cases and right in 94 cases. The incidence of palsy did not differ significantly between the right and left side (Fig. 4). Palsy developed more frequently in May than in any other month of the year, but its incidence showed no significant seasonal differences (Fig. 5). Stellate ganglion block (SGB) was used as the therapy of first choice. The response rate was higher in patients treated with a combination of SGB, steroid and low molecular weight dextran then in patients who received other therapies, although this difference was not significant (Fig. 7). Forty patients (26.3%) had suffered from dental disease or received dental treatment before the onset of facial palsy. Although we analyzed the relationship between dental disease or treatment and facial palsy, no relationship was detected. We will continue to study this relationship.
