N2  - Malignant otitis externa is an invasive, pseudomonal infection involving the petrous temporal bone, mastoid and skull base. We present 7 cases in order to highlight the continuing difficulty in diagnosis and subsequent late presentations. Three of our patients had facial nerve palsy and 2 patients had gross skull base erosion on CT scan but, surprisingly, did not present with cranial neuropathies. They died after an extended period of treatment, reflecting the advanced nature of their disease. Two of the patients with facial nerve palsy regained complete facial function and the third patient is still recovering. isolated facial nerve palsy does not appear to be as sinister as was once thought, provided treatment is appropriate. However, the mortality associated with skull base osteomyelitis and erosion remains high. Each patient here presented had a risk factor for getting this disease. An increased awareness of such a condition, followed by prompt diagnosis and treatment, cannot be over-emphasized in order to avoid later presentation with complications.
