N2  - A 23-year-old female with hemophilia B became positive HIV by repeated administration of platelet factor 9. Left facial palsy developed suddenly without any other neurological signs and was incomplete graded as 24/40 point. The facial palsy gradually improved and completely healed within eight weeks. Gadolinium enhancement was manifested in the left geniculate ganglion by MRI. Additionally, herpes simplex and cytomegalo virus antibody titers changed between acute and convalescent phase. A geniculate ganglionitis induced by this virus was speculated as a cause of facial palsy, complicating with immunodeficiency, even though a possibility of direct infection of HIV itself could not be denied.
