N2  - A 24-year-old woman and a 45-year-old man complained of bilateral otalgia, hearing loss, low grade fever and headache. They had been treated with myringotomy and antibiotics because the initial diagnosis was acute otitis media with effusion. However, their hearing impairment progressed gradually, and the first patient developed facial palsy and saddle nose. Although the histopathologic appearance of the granulation tissue in the tympanic cavity was not specific, the elevated Creactive protein (CRP) and increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) strongly suggested an auto-immune disease. Administration of prednisolone and azathioprine dramatically improved all their signs and symptoms. A localized type of Wegener's granulomatosis in the ear was suspected from their clinical course and the efficacy of immunosuppressive agents.
