N2  - Insulin resistance is a major determinant of the hyperglycaemia that occurs in patients with type II diabetes mellitus. The fact that there is no successful treatment for insulin resistance compounds the ability to treat these patients successfully. Insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I) has been shown in experimental animals and in men to exert insulin-like effects when administered at supraphysiological doses. Recombinant human IGF-I was therefore administered for 4.5-6 weeks to 12 hyperinsulinaemic patients with type II diabetes, and its effects on insulin resistance and glycaemic control were measured. At the end of treatment, IGF-I, 100 μg/kg twice daily, had lowered mean 24-hour blood glucose levels, from 267 ± 84 mg/dl to 168 ± 59 mg/dl (P < 0.001). Mean 24-hour insulin concentrations fell from 18 to 9.5 ng/ml (P < 0.001). Similarly, fructosamine levels declined from 369 ± 105 μmol/l to 302 ± 76 μmol/l (P < 0.05) after 3 weeks of treatment and declined further to 271 ± 87 μmol/l (P < 0.05) at the end of IGF-I administration. Glycosylated haemoglobin decreased from 10.7% to 8.1% (P < 0.05). Mean fasting triglyceride levels decreased from 205 ± 97 mg/dl to 128 ± 55 mg/dl (P < 0.05). The insulin sensitivity index was calculated from intravenous glucose tolerance tests and showed a 3.4-fold improvement. This improvement was accompanied by a decrease in fat mass of 2.2% (P < 0.05), calculated using dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry. Significant side- effects occurred in some patients, but nine patients were able to complete at least 4.5 weeks of treatment. In summary, IGF-I significantly lowered blood glucose, as reflected by long-term indices of glycaemic control. The results suggest that IGF-I therapy may be an effective way of improving insulin sensitivity in patients with type II diabetes mellitus.
