N2  - Acute ENT sensori-neural impairments are treated in numerous and various ways. Whilst the histopathological lesions in sonotraumas and in sudden deafness are different, their management is based on the same principles. From a review of 100 cases, including 40 sono-traumas, 40 cases of sudden deafness, and 20 facial palsies, we assessed the comparative results of two treatments: the first combined Naftidrofuryl, Dihydroergotaxine and Methylprednisolone with hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HOT); the second combined Piracetam and Pentoxifylline with carbogen. There is no significant difference between the two and our results are close to those of other teams. Good results were obtained in 61% of sonotraumas treated by hyperbaric oxygen therapy, versus 57% in patients treated with carbogen. If there is concomittant tinnitus, the best results are reported with hyperbaric oxygen therapy. For sudden deafness, the results are identical. After one month, there is no difference between the two therapies for the improvement of facial palsy (although one protocol did not include corticoids). Hyperbaric oxygen therapy twice a day is satisfactory and well tolerated by all patients.
