N2  - The results of various surgical methods for treating facial paralysis have been evaluated in 34 elderly patients more than 60 years old. The surgery consisted of a brow lift (17 cases), Kuhnt-Szymanowski's method (12 cases), a fascia suspension (4 cases), a wedge resection of the lower lip (4 cases), lid loading with gold plate (5 cases), Gillies-Anderson's method of a temporal muscle transfer (18 cases) and Rubin's method (2 cases), a masseter muscle transfer (1 case), and a vascularized muscle transfer (23 cases). The results are enumerated below: 1) Neither a fascia suspension nor a muscle transfer was found effective for ptosis of the nasolabial region; 2) A combination of various surgical methods had to be used to treat severe ptosis in aged patients; 3) For the surgical repair of paralytic lagophthalmos, lid lifting is recommended, since Gillies-Andersen's method was found to be less effective because temporal muscle contractility is weak; 4) Muscle transplantation combined with a cross-face nerve graft was found effective for many aged patients, and a one-stage muscle transplantation using a hypoglossal nerve was also found useful, since it shortened the treatment period.
