N2  - In this study we present 16 cases with multiple palsy of the cranial nerves and clinical features of herpes zoster cephalicus. Besides typical cases of Ramsay Hunt syndrome, the purpose of our study is to present some atypical cases with regard to the combination of the affected cranial nerves and the lack of herpetic rash. The absence of the typical herpetic rash in 2 cases during the disease period was remarkable, while in 1 case the herpetic rash appeared after the labyrinth was affected. The diagnosis in these cases was achieved by a virologic study using the Elisa method. The results with regard to recovery of the affected nerves suggest the following. In the cases presenting hearing loss, only 22% had complete recovery. In the cases presenting labyrinth affection, only 25% had complete functional recovery. In the cases presenting facial nerve palsy, only 50% had complete functional recovery. Therefore the prognosis for facial nerve palsy from herpes zoster virus is worse than that for Bell's palsy. In order to avoid considering atypical cases of herpes zoster as idiopathic and managing them falsely, virologic study is absolutely necessary. The other affected cranial nerves (V, IX, X) had complete functional recovery within 1 or 2 months.
