N2  - Between 1965 and 1933, 36 patients with mucepidermoid carcinoma (MEC) of the salivary glands have been treated at the ENT Clinic of the University of Hamburg (FRG). The majority were tumors of the parotid gland (n = 29) which occurred mostly in the T1 and T2 stages. The average rate of metastasis (N1 + N2) was 61.1%. Local recurrences appeared in 38.9% of the cases. Low-differentiated MEC showed a three or four times higher tendency of metastasizing and recurrence, respectively. The average interval of local recurrence was shorter in low-differentiated (12.5 months) than in high-differentiated MEC (46.0 months). Paralysis of the facial nerve (n = 2) due to aggressive tumor growth and distant metastasis (n = 2) have been observed very seldom. The principal therapy concept consists of tumor resection (n = 50) combined with functional or radical neck dissection (n = 25). A few cases have been treated by postoperative (n = 9) and primary (n = 1) irradiation. Adjuvant chemotherapy has been carried out in 2 advanced cases without success. Our analysis points out that primary tumor resection and neck dissection should be the therapy of choice in MEC. Low-differentiated MEC without clear margins and MEC of the base of the tongue should undergo pestoperative radiotherapy.
