N2  - Lyme disease is a spiroketosis caused by the transmission of Borrelia burgdorferi. It is known as the 'New Great Pretender', because of its various clinical manifestations. The goal of this paper is to comment on a new case and also to make a review on the literature about neuroborreliosis in childhood. We present a seven year-old girl who came into our hospital with conduct disturbances, psychomotor agitation and mieloradiculytis. Borrelia burgdorferi's plasma serology was positive. She manifested a rigid-acynetic syndrome, disquinesis, multiple dystonias and global apraxia. Therapy with Ceftriaxona was started early, but she did not improve. Corticoid and L-Dopa did not modify the clinical state. The extrapyramidal symptoms decreased with Artane. We have reviewed 300 cases from the literature on childhood neuroborreliosis. The most frequent clinical symptoms were: Facial Palsy, Aseptic Meningitis, Erithema Migrans, Encephalitis, Mieloradiculytis and Conduct Disturbances. It is important to make an early diagnosis of Neuroborreliosis because it is a treatable pathology and has a good prognosis. Nevertheless we must bear in mind the possibility of neurology sequels. The growing number of papers on neuroborreliosis is explained by the fact that professionals are becoming more and more sensitive at this kind of symptomatology.
