N2  - The most devastating sequelae of the facial nerve paralysis is the loss of eyelid function. The inability to blink, lubricate and protect the globe can lead to exposure keratitis, corneal abrasion and even the loss of vision. Eyelid closure is approximately 85% upper eyelid and 15% lower eyelid. In order to ensure adequate protection of the globe, deficiencies of both eyelids must be addressed. We report our experience with 20 patients with eyelid paralysis. Upper lid reanimation was performed by the placement of gold lid weights on the tarsal plate. Lower lid reanimation procedures included lateral canthopexy and horizontal lid shortening. A discussion of the above-mentioned procedures, the timing of the procedures and a critical analysis of results will be included. A comprehensive approach to the management of the paralyzed eye will be presented.
