N2  - We report our results with electroneurography (ENG) and the Maximum Stimulation Test (MST) in 200 patients with idiopathic facial palsy. Our aim was to determine the prognostic value of these tests in Bell's palsy. The MST demonstrated that, when the muscle response of the affected side is within the normal limits, the neuronal damage is slight, and complete functional recovery may be expected (grade I). When the muscle response decreases, the prognosis is less favorable. ENG showed that when the amplitude of the compound muscle action potential varies between 51 and 95% of the normal value, the neuronal damage is slight (neurapraxia), and complete functional recovery may be expected (grade I). When the percentage value of muscle response decreases, the prognosis is less favorable. The evaluation of our results and their correlation with the index of facial nerve functional recovery show that the two tests yield a high percentage of correctness, 98% for the ENG and 94% for the MST, and a low percentage of error.
