N2  - Surgical treatment of parotid gland tumors presents three main problems: 1. radicality, based on tumor extension and hystopathology; 2. iatrogenic facial nerve palsies; 3. surgical scare on the face. So, the target is to obtain the right surgical treatment with the lowest consequences rate. We describe our experience during the period january 1989-september 1991. Diagnosis has always been casual. Before operation we performed an ecotomographic study of the tumor, associated with a guided FNAB; cytologic examination showed 10 polimorphic adenomas. In two cases it didn't allow a diagnosis, so we performed an intraoperatory frosen section. The skin incision was periauricolar, C-shaped, prolonged toward the mastoid region, and permitted a complete exposure of the gland without producing scars on exposed areas of the face. We always isolated the facial nerve and its branches while dissecting the gland, performing a subtotal parotidectomy. Hystology showed 11 polimorphic adenomas and 1 Warthin's tumor. During an average follow-up period of 20 months we never observed a local recurrence, and registered only 3 temporary mandibular branch palsies that perhaps disappeared spontaneously. Our cutaneous incision permitted to perform the right surgical treatment of parotid gland tumors avoiding iatrogenic nervous lesions without leaving any scar on exposed areas of the face.
