N2  - The causes of facial numbness are reviewed according to the clinical examination: associated to other deficits or isolated. Because the incidence of trigeminal herpes zoster is high, the early clinical picture and the treatment of this infection are detailed. Other less known symptomatic facial numbness which are usually difficult to diagnose are described because of appearance of new diseases (borrelia burgdorferi infection, AIDS), atypical syndromes (Miller Fisher, mixed connective tissue diseases, metastatic infiltration) or onset of epidemic diseases. The role of electrodiagnostic procedures, cerebrospinal fluid examination and radiologic screening by CT-scan and MRI is underscored.
