N2  - Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome (MRS) represents a complex combination of clinical signs, which now exceed by far those first described by Melkersson and Rosenthal, i.e. the triad of swelling, facial palsy, and plicated tongue. Many new manifestations of this disease with its unpredictable course and still unknown etiology have been described during the last few decades. Complete review and rearrangement also of seemingly unimportant symptoms have led to an improved judgement of the disease as well as a safer diagnosis, especially in abortive forms. In our present study, we analyzed the course of all MRS patients treated in our department since 1968 and evaluated the symptoms observed during that period. We again discuss the diagnostic significance of minor criteria often not recognized during treatment.
