N2  - Modern microsurgery has allowed the surgeon to remove acoustic tumors safely in the vast majority of patients. Mortality rates are less than 0.5 percent. Complications such as hearing loss and facial paralysis occur with varying frequency. Patients are well-informed about these consequences preoperatively. The treatment of total sensorineural loss still remains a challenge. Facial nerve problems following acoustic tumor surgery are diminishing, in large part due to intraoperative monitoring. A large repertoire of rehabilitative efforts enables the surgeon to help those patients who have sustained a disabling facial paralysis.
