N2  - Diabetes mellitus in elderly patients is exceedingly common, with a prevalence in the United States of 6.9 percent in men older than 65 years and 8.9 percent in women older thant 65. Among persons older than 80, 16 to 20 percent have diabetes mellitus. The National Nursing Home Survey estimated that 14.5 percent of nursing home residents have diabetes mellitus. In the United Kingdom, two thirds of all diabetic patients who are hospitalized are older than 65 years. Diabetes mellitus is often missed in the elderly, as demonstrated by a screening program in a Canadian old people's home, where 32 percent of the residents were reclassified as diabetic over a three-year period. Furthermore, diabetic residents of nursing homes have a higher rate of hospitalization for diabetic complications compared with ambulatory diabetic patients older than 65. Thus, diabetes mellitus represents one of the major chronic disorders of the growing elderly population. Despite these impressive numbers, little attention has been paid to the special needs of elderly diabetic patients.
