N2  - In this article I will discuss peripheral facial paralysis of nontraumatic origin only. The term idiopathic facial paralysis is used generically, in place of the often ambiguously used term, Bell's palsy, to indicate all types of peripheral facial palsy/paralysis for which no cause is apparent. Included herein is a brief resume of the pertinent anatomy and pathophysiology, a synopsis of etiologic considerations, and a discussion of current therapeutic options. Written as a review, this article is aimed at practitioners of otolaryngology, neurology, and neurosurgery with the hope that it will be of value to them in caring for their patients.
