N2  - Glomus jugulare tumours are the commonest example of the otherwise rare group of tumours (paragangliomas) arising from chemoreceptor areas of the carotid body, jugulare bulb, and promontory of the middle ear. They generally arise from the dome of the jugular bulb and are usually slowly growing benign tumours that penetrate the surrounding structures. Typically these tumours cause dysfunction of the lower four cranial nerves and the ear (jugular foramen syndrome). The signs and symptoms are well documented in several studies (Cole, 1977; Ghan et al., 1983). Facial nerve involvement is usually late. We report a case where the tumour presented solely as a facial palsy.
