N2  - Facial paralysis can be a challenge to all clinicians. The therapist must be familiar with both the anatomy and physiology of the facial nerve and also be acquainted with methods to detect sites of predict prognosis. Treatment should be given on an individual basis, with cues taken from the etiology of the paralysis. At present, controversy surrounds the treatment of the most frequent etiology of facial nerve paralysis - Bell's palsy. Steroids are currently recommended for patients with no motion but no detectable nerve degeneration; although decompression is probably the most effective treatment for patients showing signs of early nerve degeneration.
