N2  - Total facial nerve (VII) paralysis requires a variety of innovative techniques for reconstruction. The selection of the most appropriate method is generally based on extensive personal experience in microneural, facial plastic, otoneurological, and oncological methods in order to achieve consistently good results. When microneurological approaches, which generally produce the best results (i.e., facial hypoglossal crossovers, facial nerve cable grafts, neuromuscular crossovers), are not possible (due to massive ablative surgery, total nerve resections, facial diplegia, absence of the facial nerve, or longstanding paralysis with neuromuscular atrophy), the use of fascial slings for static or dynamic facial reanimation may be the procedure of choice. These techniques improve facial symmetry, produce some voluntary facial mass movement, and restore oral and ocular sphincteric competence (avoiding xerophthalmia and drooling). Generally, we employ these methods in our initial planning and execution of total temporal bone resections, massive infratemporal fossa excisions, and other radical oncological procedures (i.e., total parotidectomies and neck dissections for infiltrative mitotic disease with perineural invasion). Our technique is based on 16 cases and over 4 years experience.
