N2  - A 72-year-old man who had suffered from tinnitus and deafness in his left ear for many years presented to the Department of Otolaryngology with a 2-month history of intermittent vertigo accompanied by nausea and vomiting. The hearing in his left ear had recently deteriorated rapidly. He reported weakness of the left side of his face which came on 7 days previously, and also noted a discharge from that ear. On physical examination, polypoid tissue was present in the postero-inferior part of the external auditory meatus (EAM). He had a left lower motor neurone VII palsy, but no other focal neurological signs. These signs suggested the possibility of a glomus jugulare tumour. The otological symptoms in this case focussed attention on a lytic lesion in the region of the mastoid, biopsy of which established the diagnosis of a plasma cell tumour. Further investigations demonstrated that the mastoid lesion was the presentation of multiple myelomatosis. The patient was treated with local radiotherapy to the mastoid region and systemically by the Sloane Keterring Regime of cytotoxic drugs. Since the diagnosis was made, other lytic lesions appeared in the femora and pelvis after a period of 6 months. He remains alive with disease 24 months after diagnosis.
