N2  - A case of granular cell tumor presenting as idiopathic facial nerve paralysis is discussed, with emphasis on the role of surgical exploration in the diagnostic workup of selected patients. It is suggested that in patients with acute facial paralysis of unknown origin who lose response to evoked EMG within the first 10 days of onset, surgical exploration of the facial nerve be considered as part of the diagnostic evaluation. Following this recommendation would reduce the delay in diagnosis and ensure early, appropriate treatment of those patients who might have occult tumors.
