N2  - Neurological symptoms are common in patients with hypothyroidism but cranial nerve involvement, with the exception of the auditory nerve, is rare. Peripheral facial nerve paralysis has seldom been recognised as a consequence of hypothyroidism but is important as it may be readily treatable once recognised. We present the case of a patient who suffered an acute Bell's Palsy which failed to resolve until his previously unrecognised hypothyroidism was treated. The features of this case and the two previously reported cases are compared, and the implications regarding the pathogenesis and treatment of Bell's Palsy are discussed.
