N2  - On the basis of work reported by colleagues, as well as our own clinical research studies of patients with the aforementioned syndromes, there are now some useful guidelines for appropriate remediation based on accurate initial diagnosis. Still, there is more to be learned about each of these syndromes. Furthermore, they represent but a small sample of a very large total. Recent literature has described the speech patterns of many more syndromes, as well as the genetic aspects of the more common speech and language disorders such as language delay, dyslexia, autism, and stuttering. The most common recognizable birth defect is Down syndrome and there is, fortunately, a large body of information detailing the varied language, speech, and hearing aspects. Those of us who work with children with Down syndrome have been alerted to the anticipated receptive and expressive language delays; the conductive and sometimes mixed hearing losses; the hoarse and raucous voices that are probably the result of a combination of anatomic, neurologic, and mucosal variations; the interesting disfluencies, and the amalgam of developmental and deviant articulatory errors. We know that although the tongue protrudes, it is rarely the true macroglossia which we would find in Beckwith syndrome, for example, but rather a hypotonic posture and a logical adaptation to an airway restricted by enlarged tonsils and adenoids and recurrent rhinitis. We must of necessity list the generalities and the expected finds for each syndrome. At the same time, there is a need to remain open to a wide range of variation and an occasional unlikely association, such as the child with a repaired cleft who also stutters. Some associations, however, may not occur purely by chance. In a recent consultation on the severely dysarthric speech of a teenager with facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy, I was surprised to find that he had a sharply sloping, progressive sensorineural hearing loss. At first glance, it seemed a chance association until we realized that his younger sister had the same combination of features. Although hearing loss had not been associated with any of the forms of muscular dystrophy in the past, we embarked on a review of the literature and of the case files of cooperative colleagues and uncovered a number of individuals with facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy and sensorineural hearing loss. The findings were published with an underlying plea to check the hearing of patients with this type of neuromuscular disease as soon as the diagnosis is made, in infancy if possible. The risk is generally established by the occurrence of this condition in the family since it is inherited on an autosomal dominant basis. The severe hearing impairments of our patients were not discovered until they were of school age, thereby precluding early amplification and special preschool training. In the future we will rule out hearing loss before we go on to tackle the flaccid dysarthria and progressive myopathy associated with this type of muscular dystrophy. Those of us with special interest in the communicative skills and deficits of individuals with birth defects hope that some day there will be a complete list of ready references to add to our trained and intuitive skills in the diagnosis and therapy for those with specific syndromes. As with our search to learn more about all aspects of craniofacial genetics, the task is underway.
