N2  - Of 16 patients with traumatic facial paralysis and fresh longitudinal fracture, 13 recovered well without surgery, and three required total decompression of the facial nerve. In these three patients there were fragments of fractured bone and in one case there was discontinuity of the facial nerve. Even in cases of delayed facial paralysis, the facial nerve should be explored surgically if nerve degeneration is imminent.
