N2  - Facial nerve paralysis is a disfiguring disability, and over the years many procedures have been developed to reduce it. The aims of therapy are to improve appearance by increasing facial tone, providing symmetry at rest, and restoring voluntary movement and emotional facial expression. For many years facial nerve substitution was the procedure of choice at The Otologic Medical Group (OMG) to treat facial paralysis resulting from posterior fossa surgery. For the last 8 years the technique of immediate restoration of facial nerve continuity by direct anastomosis or insertion of a nerve graft has become standard when the continuity of the facial nerve is distrupted during surgery. When facial recovery does not occur postoperatively, despite preservation of nerve continuity or failure of the primary anastomosis or nerve graft, the best means of restoring facial function is accomplished with a nerve substitution procedure. We investigated 66 patients who underwent facial nerve substitution at the OMG, paying particular attention to the timing of the operation and technique used.
