N2  - A 48-year-old man was admitted to the hospital because of facial palsy and syncopal episodes. In the previous weeks he had a transitory erythema and multiple nonspecific symptoms. Physical examination and lumbar puncture showed evidence of aseptic meningitis with cranial neuropathies. Because tuberculous meningitis could not be excluded, antituberculous therapy was started. After the diagnostic possibility of Lyme's disease was confirmed by a positive serologic test, treatment was continued with high dose intravenous penicillin. The patient's symptoms resolved without further recurrence.
