N2  - Eighteen patients with borreliosis cerebrospinalis (Bannwarth's syndrome) are described. The disease showed a seasonal incidence in the summer and autumn. Eight patients first developed localised erythema, which resembled erythema chronicum migrans in three cases. Seventeen patients experienced severe burning radicular pain. Mononeuritis multiplex mainly affecting the cranial nerves was seen in 13 patients including four with unilateral and eight with bilateral facial palsy. Characteristically there was a latency of 1-4 weeks between the beginning of the erythema, the sensory radiculitis and the focal motor deficits. Two patients had CNS involvement, in the form of transverse myelitis and focal encephalitis. Two patients had painful radiculitis only and a third patient had also myocarditis. The CSF was examined in 17 cases and invariably showed lymphocytic pleocytosis and elevated spinal protein concentration. Measuring borrelia antibodies was particularly useful in atypical cases. The diagnostic sensitivity of antibody detection seemed higher in CSF than in serum. Penicillin treatment shortened the course of the disease significantly. Borreliosis cerebrospinalis is a tick transmitted borrelia infection closely related to the American Lyme disease.
