N2  - Moebius syndrome is a rare congenital dysplasia of cranial nerves (1,2). The VI and VII cranial nerves are commonly affected, resulting in esotropia and complete or partial facial paralysis. Occasionally the V, X, XI, and XII cranial nerves are also involved, resulting in difficulties in chewing, swallowing, and coughing, and often leading to aspiration with respiratory complications. The following is a report of the anesthetic management and postoperative complications in a patient with Moebius syndrome.
