N2  - The effects of stellate ganglion block in preventing arrhythmia as well as their effects on hemodynamics were studied in animals and man. In 28 mongrel dogs, the cardiac muscle was stimulated by gradually intensifying the electricity with a high frequency alternative current through either the coaxial or bipolar electrodes placed in the endocardium or the epicardium. The minimal amperage to produce premature ventricular contraction more than once in every 5 to 6 heart beats was determined. The stellate ganglion resection (SGR) of either side brought about a rise in the arrhythmic threshold of the heart, but the effects on the hemodynamics were slightly different with the SGR of the right and left side. In 68 patients, 57 underwent stellate ganglion block (SGB) with 1% lidocaine as a treatment for facial palsy and they showed a slight change in their hemodynamics, as had been seen in dogs. In 10 of the 11 remaining patients with premature ventricular contraction, the SGB of either side produced an antiarrhythmic effect. A tentative block of the stellate ganglion with local anesthetics may be useful as a treatment for acutely ill patients with ventricular arrhythmia when conventional drug therapy is ineffective.
