N2  - Due to continued reports of birth defects with isotretinoin (Accutane), the pregnancy warning has again been strengthened, a new patient leaflet has been prepared, and FDA has advised blood banks not to accept as donors persons who are on isotretinoin therapy, or who stopped taking the drug less than a month prior to donation. Isotretinoin, a vitamin A isomer, has been contraindicated in pregnancy since its approval for the treatment of severe, recalcitrant cystic acne. At the time of approval, this contraindication was based on well-documented animal teratogenicity. Subsequently, the drug's human teratogenicity was confirmed, and the pregnancy contraindication was strengthened and put into boldface type. As of July 1, 1984, FDA had received reports of 21 major birth defects and 24 spontaneous abortions associated with the use of isotretinoin in pregnancy, usually before the woman knew she was pregnant. All the birth defect cases involve a syndrome of small or absent ears, neurological injury, and cardiovascular defect, either in combination or separately. Facial dysmorphia with a small mouth and lower jaw, sometimes with celft palate, have also been observed.
