N2  - A 55 year old male patient had been suffering from hoarseness for 10 months and from dysphagia for 2 when he suddenly developed respiratory insufficiency, caused by bilateral recurrent nerve paralysis. He had had an orthostatic hypotension, hypohidrosis and impotence for some time. Neurologic investigation revealed an essentially unilateral hypoglossal nerve paresis and soft palate paralysis, a primary orthostatic hypotension, anhidrosis, total inability to swallow with esophagus denervation and loss of ganglion cells from the esophageal musculature, all suggesting a slowly progressive disorder. The diagnosis was the Shy Drager syndrome with bulbar palsy. The differential diagnosis is discussed. Dominant bulbar cranial nerve symptoms are rare in the multisystemic Shy Drager syndrome. They can cause life threatening situations and diagnostic problems.
