N2  - Miescher's disease, characterized by recurring spontaneous swelling of one or both lips (which may become permanent), has no obvious cause. Many authors consider this phenomenon a part of the Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome, since it greatly resembles it both clinically and histologically. The manifestations of the Melkersson Rosenthal syndrome are facial swelling, facial nerve paresis, and plicated tongue. Not all the symptoms need appear, and quite often patients may show swelling of the lips and plicated tongue without having facial paresis. This article presents a case of Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome in which the patient had a permanent swelling of the upper lip, a plicated tongue, and migraine but no facial paresis. Surgical correction of the upper lip is described.
