N2  - The facial nerve is the most frequently affected peripheral nerve of the body. Its acute palsy is one of the most devastating and yet, by itself, nonlethal medical conditions. Facial nerve paralysis is associated not only with loss of normal function of the mouth and eyelids, but also with loss of the ability of facial expression. Depending on the cause and management, many of these paralyses will be permanent. For centuries, doctors have worked for ways to reverse the acute palsy or reduce deformity and morbidity of the established palsy. With advances in the understanding of the function of peripheral nerves and the benefit of microsurgery, diagnosis and management have recently improved. Such advances are made in both early repair and late reconstruction. This article addresses the management of acute extratemporal lesions of the facial nerve.
