N2  - Disorders of the facial nerve embarass both the patient and his environment. Generally, 'plus' and 'minus' syndromes can be distinguished. The minus syndromes encompass the aetiologically different types of facial paralysis, i.e. idiopathic palsy, herpes zoster oticus and paralysis due to trauma or tumor. The plus syndromes consist of facial hemispasm and of facial myokymia. Clinical examination is supplemented by electrophysiological tests which play an important role in the assessment of the site (topodiagnosis) and the time course of the disorder. Of particular importance are electromyographic and neurographic investigations, the orbicularis oculi reflex and the stapedius reflex. Otolaryngological, radiological and cerebrospinal fluid investigatons may become necessary. Concerning treatment, there is an increasing tendency to administer corticosteroids as early as possible in idiopathic facial palsy. Surgery must be considered in cases with traumatic or tumorous lesions. Facial hemispasm is frequently managed by membrane stabilizers such as carbamazepine or phenytoin. A decompression of the nerve in the posterior fossa should be discussed in refractory cases. The management of myokymia depends on the underlying disorders (multiple sclerosis, polyradiculitis, pontine tumor).
