N2  - Painful disease states caused by infections are rare in neurological conditions. Yet, their diagnosis and differential diagnosis should be familiar to every physician. In this paper, the following syndromes are discussed: Cervical rigidity due to leptomeningitis, 'symptomatic' trigeminal neuralgia, in particular affection of the ophthalmic nerve, herpes zoster oticus, post-herpetic neuralgia, painful facial palsy, neuralgic amyotrophy, which has been recognized also in the lumbar plexus, polyneuritis, polymyositis, the problems of mononeuritis of infectious etiology, and segmental irradiations of pain due to intrathoracic or abdominal disease. Pain cannot be assessed by objective methods. Treatment should not be limited to drugs, including psychotropic ones, but should make use of methods such as transcutaneous stimulation of peripheral nerves.
