N2  - Because the improvement in appearance of the aging or fatty neck from a standard facelift was sometimes disappointing to both the patient and the surgeon, more extensive and deeper techniques have been developed. With the more aggressive methods in which the surgeon elevates the SMAS (superficial musculo- aponeurotic system), including platysmal and subplatysmal flaps, the dissection is deeper in the face and neck. Although the recent popularization of these techniques has enhanced the results of some rhytidectomies, there may be a higher risk of some complications as well as the introduction of some new complications. The wise surgeon will remember that his or her goal is improvement rather than 'cure', and heroic surgical indulgence associated with complications only makes for an unsatisfactory result and an unhappy patient. Obviously, a thorough preoperative evaluation, including informed consent with an explanation of the limitations, alternatives, risks, and potential complications, is of paramount importance in minimizing postoperative complications. The surgeon must be familiar with both minor and major complications and present them to the patient in a realistic manner.
