N2  - The facial paralysis that follows the ablation of an acoustic neuroma can be treated with the following methods: XIIth or XIth nerve anastomosis with the VIIth; cross-face nerve grafting; or, when the first two methods are not feasible, static and dynamic suspension with fascial slings and masticatory muscle flaps. We have carried out hipoglossal-facial anastomosis in 12 cases of forementioned palsy. Two cases have been operated three and four years after the onset of paralysis. Eleven cases have obtained a good facial reinnervation. In only one case a muscle contraction is not yet noticeable (ten months after the operation). All the other patients are satisfied with the results and don't complain about the minor problems due to the various degree of tongue atrophy.
