N2  - The majority of patients who present with Wegener's Granulomatosis have complaints referable to the nose or paranasal sinuses. However, other anatomical areas may be involved either alone or as part of a generalized disease - the kidney, eyes, joints, skin, muscle, ears, heart, and the nervous system. In the past, Wegener's Granulomatosis was fatal if untreated, with a mean survival of five months. More recently, because the diagnosis of Wegener's Granulomatosis is being made earlier, the results of treatment have improved with patient cures being reported. Wegener's Granulomatosis may involve the ear in one of three ways: Serous otitis media, either unilaterally or bilaterally, caused by nasopharyngeal ulceration and obstruction of the eustachian tube; destruction of the middle ear and mastoid with 'chronic otitis media' caused by actual involvement of the middle ear and mastoid cavity by Wegener's Granulomatosis; and a sensori-neural defect usually associated with a conductive hearing loss, caused by inflammation of the cochlear vessels. Occasionally the otological manifestation may be the first and only sign of Wegener's Granulomatosis. This is a case report of Wegener's Granulomatosis in a young man who had bilateral otitis media associated with a facial palsy, followed by a discussion of the difficulties encountered in making the correct diagnosis.
