N2  - The first case of intra-temporal facial neuroma was reported by Schmidt in 1930. Since then most contributions to the literature have been reports of single cases, although a few surgeons have been able to accumulate small series over the last two decades. The majority of facial neuromas within the temporal bone present with a facial palsy, but some patients have normal facial nerve function and complain only of hearing loss. Diagnosis is especially difficult in this latter group, but treatment also presents special problems. The standard treatment for facial neuroma is complete excision of the tumour, and almost always this requires resection of a segment of the facial nerve. The options for restoring facial nerve function are re-routing with end-to-end anastomosis, placement of a cable graft, or hypoglosso-facial anastomosis: however, all these methods inevitably involve a complete facial palsy for 4-18 months, followed by a variable degree of functional recovery and always accompanied by some mass action. The prospect of such a result is satisfactory for the patient who already has a facial palsy on presentation, but considerably less so for the patient with normal facial function pre-operatively. Of the five cases in this series, three presented without any facial weakness, one had only a slight paresis, and one had a complete palsy. It is the purpose of this paper to contrast the clinical features and treatment of these patients and to discuss their management in relation to a review of the literature.
