N2  - Corticosteroids and adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) have been used extensively in neurology since their clinical introduction in the late 1940s. With few exceptions the initial enthusiasm for these drugs has been replaced by more reflective scepticism and tempered by awareness of their potent side effects. A small number of double-blind trials have been performed and, perhaps surprisingly, most of these have failed to provide conclusive answers to problems of management. Some of the difficulties stem from the complex relationship between pathophysiological processes at a neuronal level and day-to-day 'function'; additionally there has been a relative paucity of techniques for making objective clinical measurements in man to guide treatment. The diseases discussed are mainly those seen in general hospital practice in the UK and the references, although by no means comprehensive, should provide interested readers with a guide to more recent reviews and trials.
