N2  - In Sweden, 400 to 500 new cases of tumours of the parotid gland are reported each year. They constitute 0.3% of all reported malignant tumours. Mixed tumours or pleomorphic adenomas are the biggest group. The pathohistological picture can be varied. To be able to estimate a possible malignancy it is necessary to look for the clusters of cells, for their polymorphism and for the extent of mitosis. The borders of the tumour may be broken, explaining the very great recurrency tendency, especially if any parotidectomy has been performed. The risk of malignancy increases as the tumour gets older. For a tumour of 5 year's standing it is about 1-2%. The type of cancer most typical of the salivary glands is adenoid-cystic carcinoma which is often called 'cylindroma' because of its histological appearance. Its great capability of infiltration alongside the nerves is well known. The clinical diagnosis of these different forms of tumours is difficult. Cytological puncture with a fine needle gives good results in 95%, without any risk. When a facial palsy arises this is a very bad sign.
