N2  - Of all general medical disciplines pregnancy involves the neurologist less often than most. The subject is best treated by considering first those conditions which pregnancy, parturition or the puerperium are known to precipitate and second the effects of pregnancy on pre-existing neurological diseases. There are some conditions which achieve clinical recognition during pregnancy, if only because the patient has never before been seen or examined by a doctor. As a broad generalization it is probably a sound principle to avoid executive action if at all possible both in the disorders precipitated by pregnancy (because most remit naturally in the puerperium) and in those established disorders which antedate pregnancy and which may be unaffected or even improved by it. It has been the author's experience that young women in pregnancy, and even when contemplating starting a family, have a scale of priorities quite different from older or non-pregnant patients. The pregnancy or its prospect has a personal value to the patient which the wise physician should not underestimate, and for which due allowance should be made in planning the management of any associated problem. This has a bearing on medication, since the public are now much more knowledgeable about agents which may produce (or have been alleged to produce) small babies or teratogenic effects. Physicians must, of course, base their decisions on the volume of knowledge in the medical literature concerning these matters, but they may be wise to accede occasionally to the fears of the mother-to-be if these appear deeply felt, if necessary abandoning rigid adherence to scientific principle.
