N2  - A 16 yr old girl was admitted to our service with chief complaints of an abruptly occurring severe headache, vomiting and facial asymmetry. The patient had no episode of disturbance of her consciousness after the onset. On admission she was alert and neurological examination revealed left-sided peripheral trigeminal, abducens and facial palsy with moderate bilateral horizontal nystagmus in her both eyes. No cerebellar sign and no weakness were noted on her limbs. Computed tomography revealed a small high density area in the left dorsal part of the pons. A left serial angiography was performed, but it failed to find out any abnormal findings such as arterio-venous malformation, cavernous angioma, telangiectasis, etc. The high density in the left dorsal pontine area, however, showed enlarging tendency with slow progression on repeat CT after admission over a month. It was hard to confirm the cause of this hemorrhage either due to pontine glioma or other local vascular disorders radiographically. Then, the left suboccipitial craniectomy and evacuation of the hematoma was performed under operative microscope on January 16, 1979. Several specimen around the hematoma was obtained, but neither malignancies nor vascular disorders were detected histologically. Postoperative course was uneventful. She recovered well. No neurological deficit was noted at the time of discharge. Since then, she has been working well as a nurse student for over 2 years. Cases of a juvenile pontine hemorrhage have been reported sporadically. It has been very difficult to diagnose it neurologically as well as angiographically. However, CT examination is now readily able to reveal the location of such hematoma with high density and contributes to introduce the diagnosis as well. Etiologies of the hematoma were identified to be cavernous angioma, arterio-venous malformation or telangiectasis in the majority of cases which had been performed the post-mortem examination. However, there are still idiopathic cases with unknown causes of the hematoma. It was interesting that the hematoma of our cases showed long-lasting high density with some enlarging tendency in repeat CT examination although clinical and operative searches had failed to find the etiology of the hemorrhage.
