KW  - meningioma
KW  - nervous system
KW  - therapy
N2  - Meningiomas of the lateral ventricles are uncommon and account for 0.5% to 4.5% of intracranial meningiomas. About 85% of the lateral ventricular meningiomas are reported to arise from the region of the trigon and it is noted that meningiomas arise around the foramen of Monro. A 53-yr-old female was admitted to our service with chief complaints of nuchal pain, left motor weakness and speech disturbance. On admission, neurological examination revealed aniscoria (right < left), bilateral papilledema without retinal bleeding, left facial palsy and left hemiparesis with mild disturbance of fine finger movement. She had limping gait with left hyperactive deep tendon reflexes. The retinal bleeding, however, appeared one week later after admission. Plain skull x-ray showed no pathological findings. Plain CT scan revealed an large oval-shaped isodensity area covering the foramen of Monro. The tumor showed a homogeneous enhancement by contrast medium injection. The markedly enhanced tumor was connected to a enhanced choroid plexus of the third ventricle. There was no continuation to a falx by coronal CT scans. Right carotid and left vertebral angiograms showed a homogeneous tumor stain on the capillary and venous phases. Main feeding arteries of the tumor were right anterior choroidal artery and rigth medial posterior choroidal artery. The draining vein was the right thalamostriate vein. From these findings, a meningioma arising around the foramen of Monro of the right lateral ventricle was suspected. Opening of anterior horn of the lateral ventricles was performed and total removal was successfully done by means of the vaporizing technique with LASER, which controlled much tumorous bleeding nicely. Histologically the tumor was composed of syncytial cells, foamy cells, lymphoid cells and partially capillary lumens which were formed by syncytial cells. These tumor cells were not stained by PTAH. Therefore, histology of the tumor was diagnosed an angioblastic type of meningioma. At present (about a half year after the operation), she has been doing all right without physical and neurological abnormalities.
