N2  - The authors report a patient with Paget's disease and progressive oropharyngeal dysphagia that was most likely secondary to a bulbar palsy, the result of basilar invagination with compression of the interior surface of the brainstem. The dysphagia responded to treatment with calcitonin, a hormonal therapeutic agent reportedly effective in some patient with Paget's disease and neurological involvement, whose action is to slow the process of the disease.
