N2  - Stroke in the distribution area described the vertebro-basilar arteries following chiropractic manipulation of the neck is discribed in a Norwegian medical journal for the first time. The patient, a 35 yr old previously healthy female, consulted a chiropractor after a ten day period of neck pain. During manipulation she experienced numbness in her right side. The symptoms disappeared after ten minutes but reappeared an hour later. In addition she felt weak on the same side and noted a shadow in her right upper visual field. The clinical examinaton revealed marked paresis, ataxia and hypaesthesia of her right side, and a slight right central facial palsy was apparent. Visual field examination showed a concomitant relative upper right quadrant anopsia. Cerebral computed tomography revealed signs of cerebral infarction in the left occipital pole and posteriorly in the internal capsule and thalamus. Cerebral angiography performed three weeks later showed a short narrowing of the vertebral artery at the level of the atlas. Lumen irregularities of the left posterior cerebral artery were present, suggesting a recanalization of an embolus. Signs of collateral circulation from the medial cerebral artery were present. Her clinical condition gradually improved from the day of presentation, but a followup two months later revealed some residual ataxia, paresis and impaired sensation on her right side. The literature concerning this known complication of manipulation therapy is reviewed. The possible mechanism was probably mechanical injury to the vertebral artery at the level of maximal mobility of the cervical spine.
