N2  - The frequent dehiscence of the canalis facialis while intending to decompress the facial nerve for treatment of Bell's palsy is pointed out. The existence of nerve dehiscence in over 33% of the cases cannot be ascribed to coincidence. In a comparative study it was found that this anatomical anomaly occurred in only 5 out of 600 middle ear explorations for otosclerosis and only 2 cases were reported among 50 anatomical temporal bone preparations. The question is discussed whether or not dehiscence of the facial canal is more susceptible to vasomotor insult.
