N2  - A 61-year-old woman with Cogan's syndrome was presented. She was admitted to our department because of severe pulsative headache on February 28, 1980. She also had been suffering from pain and redness in the right eye for the last 18 months and from deafness for the last 4 months. Ophthalmologic examination revealed interstitial keratitis and conjunctivitis in the right eye. There were no abnormalities in the fundi except for slight ateriosclerosis. Otologic examination revealed bilateral nerve deafness and low vestibular response to caloric stimulation. Neurological examination revealed childish behavior, right peripheral facial palsy, bilateral deafness and vestibular ataxia. Laboratory investigation revealed leukocytosis, mild anemia and high titers of C-reactive protein and rheumatoid factor. LE cell was negative and there was no hypergammaglobulinemia. The function of liver and kidney was within normal limit. Cerebrospinal fluid, electroencephalogram and CT brain scan were not remarkable. The pathological findings in the biopsy of temporal artery and kidney were not remarkably other than slight arteriosclerosis. The headache and keratoconjunctivitis improved by prednisolone, but deafness and facial palsy remained unchanged. The early cases of Cogan's syndrome were reported by ophthalmologists and otologists and ophthalmologic and otologic manifestations were stressed. However, Cogan's syndrome is not considered to be an expression of generalized vasculitis. After Cogan's syndrome and periateritis nodosa were compared, keratitis and iritis are frequently present, but intraocular manifestations such as retinal hemorrhage and exudate are relatively rare in Cogan's syndrome, and otologic symptoms are usually bilateral and advanced to complete deafness. In periarteritis nodosa intraocular manifestations are very frequently present, but keratitis is rare and otologic symptoms are often unilateral. Neurologic manifestations of Cogan's syndrome and periarteritis nodosa have similar symptoms such as headache, mental symptoms and neuropathy, but they are more frequently complicated in periarteritis nodosa than Cogan's syndrome.
