N2  - A total of 446 cases of chickenpox were seen at the Pediatric Department, National Taiwan University Hospital from January 1970 through December 1979. Of them 18 cases were found to have complications including eight of hemorrhagic progressive chickenpox, eight of meningoencephalitis, one of post-infectious thrombocytopenic purpura and one of Reye's syndrome with suspicious disseminated intravascular coagulation. Three cases with hemorrhagic progressive chickenpox also had complication of varicella pneumonia. All cases with hemorrhagic progressive chickenpox had received either immunosuppressive drugs, steroid hormone or both for 7 to 23 months before contracting chickenpox except one case who had received only one month of chemotherapy for brain stem tumor. Six out of eight of hemorrhagic progressive chickenpox had been suffering from lymphoproliferative malignancy and one from nephrotic syndrome before contracting chickenpox. Five of eight cases with hemorrhagic progressive chickenpox died (mortality rate: 62.5%); one with lymphoproliferative malignancy and two without lymphoproliferative malignancy survived. A case of varicella after steroid hormone treatment for nephrotic syndrome for about three months was complicated by Reye's syndrome. She died of disseminated intravascular coagulation and necropsy revealed fatty infiltration of the liver and mild hepatic necrosis of probably anoxic origin. Of eight cases with varicella meningoencephalitis three had cerebellar manifestations; four had encephalitis and only one had aseptic meningitis. None of them died, but three had residual neurologic sequelae at the time of discharge: one had facial palsy, one had ataxic gait and another had focal electroencephalographic abnormality.
