N2  - Paroxysmal lacrimation is rare; it occurs most ofen after a peripheral facial palsy. The hypothesis which is most often advanced to explain this crocodile tears syndrome suggests it involves a shunt disorder at the time of regeneration of nerve fibers after a facial nerve lesion above the ganglion geniculum. Salivary fibers stray to the lacrimal pathway which causes a faulty gustolacrimal reflex. The logical therapy for this condition is interruption of the reflex pathway; section of the nervus petrosus superficialis major immediately produces healing and a definitive end to the crocodile tears syndrome.
