N2  - Bell's paralysis is generally attributed to oedematous swelling of the facial nerve confined to a rigid wall channel with secondary ischaemic compression to resolve which immediate decompression is required. It is recalled that the structure of the facial nerve changes in its course along Fallopius' canal and a pathogenetic hypothesis to explain the mechanism by which pressure is increased within the nerve trunk and how this causes later events that damage nerve fibres to a varying extent is discussed. Some interesting indications are forthcoming as to the intervention modalities for decompressing the facial nerve.
