N2  - The main features of Mobius' syndrome are facial diplegia, and bilateral paralysis of the VI cranial nerves. It is therefore as though the patient was wearing a mask. It is a relatively rare affection and little is known about whether it is of nervous or muscular origin. In any event, it ultimately affects both nerves and muscles. Surgical management therefore aims at the reconstruction of a complete neuromuscular system. In doing this, the authors here recommend the use of the abundant local resources in preference to recourse to transplants from other parts of the body.
