N2  - Nineteen cases of non chromaffin paraganglioma of the head and neck explored from 1977 to 1980 are reported. Twenty-five angiographies were performed, including 14 with embolization. Sixteen patients were operated upon, 2 of them without prior embolization. No lasting complication occurred in this series; only 2 asymptomatic extravasations during embolization and one peripheral facial paralysis which regressed spontaneously within 3 days were observed. During a 6-month to 4-year follow-up, no objective or subjective sign of recurrence was seen in patients who had preoperative embolization. Out of 19 patients explored, only 3 had clinical symptoms suggesting secretory activity of the lesion. VMA or 5-HIAA levels were not measured in any of the patients. An extremely strict procedure aimed at providing a complete map of the lesion and accurate information on its primary bilateral multifocal character has been developed. It involves injection of the contralateral ascending pharyngeal artery and of the homolateral vertebral, internal carotid, internal maxillary, posterior auricular, occipital and ascending pharyngeal arteries successively. All injections are performed with a small calibre (4.1 F) catheter, irrespective of the patient's age. Whenever possible, embolization is carried out as injections proceed and through the same catheter, using small fragments of dura-mater and large fragments of Spongel. Towards the end of the examination, the dominant vertebral artery is injected through a thin-walled 4.8 F catheter. The information obtained by this method on the jugular venous system is sufficient to preclude jugulography, which was only performed once and early in this series. In the second part of this paper 4 cases creating special problems are discussed, i.e.: 1 case of mandibular metastasis from a previously excised carotid lesion; 1 case where typical angiographic and clinical signs of local recurrence were unconfirmed by surgery and histology; 1 tumour in the pterygoid area with oculomotor disorders and hyperaemia of the cavernous sinus area; and 1 small lesion surrounded with highly vascular bone tissue. The authors discuss the significance of peritumoral hypervascularity of healthy tissue: does it reflect secretory activity, local malignancy, or post-operative inflammation? Non chromaffin paragangliomas of the head and neck must be embolized when they are amenable to surgery. In inoperable cases embolization can alleviate symptoms provided it is aimed at pre-determined, well-defined targets. The authors consider that embolization may on no account be considered as an alternative to surgery when tumors are operable.
