N2  - A large trigeminal neurinoma extending from the middle fossa to the posterior fossa was successfully removed by a two-stage operation. The benefit of two-stage operation for this type of trigeminal neurinoma is discussed and microsurgical anatomy useful for this operation is described. A 34-yr-old man was admitted to our hospital because of right facial numbness, unstable gait and visual disturbance. Neurological examination revealed right trigeminal palsy, horizontal nystagmus, left pyramidal tract signs and choked discs. Radiological studies including CT scan and cerebral angiography disclosed a large cystic tumor in the middle fossa and extending into the posterior fossa, with the brain stem markedly displaced to the left. The initial operation was performed using a right frontotemporal approach. First, the tumor bulging from the base of the middle fossa was removed and then a large part of the tentorial incisura. Histological examination proved it to be a neurinoma. Postoperatively, the left pyramidal tract disturbance and nystagmus disappeared, but diplopia due to abducens palsy became evident. CT study confirmed the disappearance of the middle fossa tumor and correction of the brain stem displacement. Additionally, the residual tumor in the posterior fossa moved to the right C-P angle from the ventral of the brain stem. The second operation was carried out using the suboccipital approach a month later, and the residual tumor adhering to the 7, 8, 9, 10 and 11 cranial nerves was completely removed. The trigeminal nerve was totally tumefied and the abducens nerve was not visible. Except for right facial hypesthesia, abducens palsy and slight facial palsy, postoperative course was uneventful and the patient was discharged in good condition. Operative techniques for trigeminal neuronoma have not been well documented so far, probably because of the rarity and diversity of tumor size and extension. Schisano and Olivecrona recommended a suboccipital approach in a case of large trigeminal neurinoma with symptoms of brain stem compression. The authors, however, propose the advantage of a two-stage operation with first a supratentorial approach in order to avoid additional compression and damage to the brain stem which is already compressed and displaced. Microsurgical anatomy for the operation on a trigeminal neurinoma with a special emphasis on the abducens nerve is described.
