N2  - A rare case with an extradural meningeal fibrosarcoma which invades and protrudes into the skull bone was reported. The patient was a 14-yr-old boy who suffered from the sudden onset of peripheral facial palsy. Plain skull x ray and angiography showed no abnormal findings. It was diagnosed as Bell's palsy and was treated with a stellate ganglion block. Primarily, the region was recongized as the small one in the extradural middle fossa. However, the tumor developed very rapidly and occupied the whole middle fossa. The patient visited the author's clinic complaining of developing hypacusia with vomiting. The plain skull x ray showed the destruction of the rt pyramis, but there was no abnormal density in CT scan. Angiographically, abnormal vascularity showed in the rt skull base. It was diagnosed as a Cerebello-Pontine Angle Tumor and suboccipital craniotomy was done. The tumor was 1x1 cm in diameter and was located in the extradura around VII, VIII cranial nerves, and did not infiltrate into the intradura. The tumor, which was grey-white, glittering partially like gelatin, was totally removed. Pathologically, interlacing bandles of small spindle cells and intercellular reticulin were found. After the operation a total amount of 4900 rad of 60Co was given. And at the same time, Vincristin was administered intravenously, but was given up at the amount of 5,625 mg. After three weeks, the low density around the rt pyramis was seen in the CT scan. Otalgia and pain in the back of rt ear attacked and tormented him. Secretion from the tumor in the external auditory meatus was found. Angiography showed the enlargement of the tumor, which elevated the dura of the middle fossa. Four months after the initial onset, the second operation was done at the subtemporal region. It was neither totally removed nor decompressed because of massive bleeding. Neurologically II, III, IV, VII, VIII, IX, X cranial nerves were impaired and the patient suffered from severe facial pain. He died 8 months after the onset. This case showed no typically clinical course, so, it was impossible to diagnose it without histological examination.
