N2  - Facial palsy due to otitis media has become rare since the advent of antibiotics. However, not all otitic palsies can be cured by antibiotics alone. Eight cases with facial palsy due to acute otitis media have been reported, and the pathophysiology and treatment of this disease are discussed. From our observation of these cases, the following conclusions are made: (1) facial palsy, generally considered due to acute otitis media, is, in reality, a complication of mastoiditis; and (2) since treatment by antibiotics alone appeared to be less effective for quick recovery in cases of acute otitis media followed by facial palsy, mastoidectomy at appropriate time is definitely recommended.
