N2  - Although the prognosis in Bell's palsy is generally good, there is a risk of sequelae in 10 to 15% of cases (incomplete recovery, hemifacial spasms). This warrants emergency medical treatment and repeated neurosensory test with a view to undertaking surgical decompression in severe cases. Conservative treatment consists of combining antiedema agents, vasodilators and vitamins of the B group. After ten days, clinical and electromyographic evaluation should be repeated. The absence of clinical recovery combined with altered or abolished electrical conduction is an indication for surgical decompression of the facial nerve.
