N2  - In this paper the authors present 15 patients treated for irreversible facial paralysis. After making a study of the different etiological agents which may cause this kind of paralysis and a description of the surgical possibilities that present day surgery offers, the authors analyse the conditions of the criteria used for their treatment, drawing conclusions from the results obtained. They feel that Thompson's muscle grafts should be the treatment of choice for the surgery of the muscle in long standing palsies. Cross facial anastomosis, on the other hand, is the method of choice when the facial musculature is not totally atrophic and still has some degree of functionality.
