N2  - Two unusual cases of brain metastasis from cancer of the colon were reported. A 63 year old female (case 1), who underwent hemicolectomy for carcinoma of the transverse colon about a year ago, was admitted because of progressive unsteady gait, nausea and vomiting. She disclosed marked trunkal ataxia, right limb ataxia, right Horner sign, right facial palsy and bilateral horizontal gaze nystagmus. Computed tomogram revealed a low density area (4x5 cm) in the posterior fossa. Following the infusion of contrast material a scattered enhancement was noted within this low density area. Surgical removal was total and the pathological diagnosis of the tumor was adenocarcinoma, consistent with a primary colon carcinoma. A 52 year old male (case 2) had generalized convulsion and then walked unsteadily. Four months ago, he had low anterior resection of a carcinoma of the sigmoid colon. On examination, he showed mild dysarthria, left facial palsy and left limb ataxia. Computed tomogram revealed a mild increased density (5 cm in size) with surrounding edema in right frontal region. After infusion of contrast material there was marked enhancement of a mass lesion. On neurological examination, pursuit movement was saccadic, especially on the right, optokinetic nystagmus was suppressed on the left side and visual suppression of caloric nystagmus disappeared in both directions. However, after removal of the mass these abnormal findings were all improved. Surgery (subtotal removal) revealed metastatic adenocarcinoma, compatible with a primary carcinoma. We postulated that his ataxia was attributed to frontal ataxia and this was discussed from a neurotological point of view. Carcinoma of the large intestine relatively rarely metastasizes to the brain, possibly because of the filtering effect of the pulmonary and hepatic circulation.
