N2  - Parotid enlargement is a diagnostic and therapeutic challenge to all physicians. In a study of 193 patients with parotid tumours operated upon in an ENT department, the incidence of complications such as facial paralysis, tumour recurrence and gustatory sweating (Frey syndrome) is reviewed and discussed. The histological specimens collected during the 10-year period have been reevaluated and are listed in accordance with the WHO classification for salivary gland tumours. Biopsy of a parotid tumour should be avoided in view of the risk of local implantation of tumour cells. It is our opinion that the tumour should be removed via a superficial or total parotidectomy even if benign. The operation should be carried out in an ENT-department and by a surgeon well conversant in the anatomy of the facial nerve.
