N2  - Review based on 121 personal cases from 25 years. 33 paralyses (27.3%) were already present immediately after birth (10 children with malformations, 10 times caused mechanically and in 6 cases by hypoxemic or toxic damages). 88 paralyses (72.3%) occurred in elder infants and children: 37 cases of idiopathic (Bell's) palsy, 23 other diseases of viral origin (meningo-encephalitis, poliomyelitis, complication from polio-immunization), 8 bacterial infections (mastoiditis, purulent meningitis, tuberculous lymphadenitis) 7 tumours and 6 traumatic processes. Congenital palsies presented mostly as central paralyses and acquired ones more frequently as peripherical paralyses. The latter had two peaks in the age distribution, in preschool-children and in the middle school-age. A flow-chart for rational differential diagnostics is presented. According to the good prognosis of facial paralysis in children we recommend only conservative treatment with corticosteroids immediately after onset of the disease. An operative decompression of the facial nerve was never necessary. 38 children were followed-up: two thirds showed complete normalization of the facial function and the remaining third had only slight disturbances. Severe residuals were found in no child. The idiopathic cases have the best prognosis.
