N2  - The Moebius syndrome is a congenital anomaly characterized by paralysis of the sixth and seventh nerves. In some instances, there may be weakness of the adductors. The other muscles are normal. Visual acuity is not affected. Other congenital defects may be associated: polydactyly, and defects of the ear and of the tongue. The etiology is probably an embryonal aggression, because of aplasia of the facial and abducens nuclei. The impossibility to move the eyes laterally in either direction points to supranucleus involvement by defective development of the medial longitudinal fasciculus. Some people suggest that a myogenic disturbance should be the principal and unique etiology. There is no evolution, and no treatment.
