N2  - A patient with Moebius syndrome who developed benign intracranial hypertension is presented. The patient has the following features of Moebius syndrome: bilateral facial palsy, esotropia with severely limited abduction, paresis and hypoplasia of the tongue, bilateral clubbed feet, and thoracic lumbar scoliosis. At age 15, he presented to the hospital with headaches and bilateral papilledema and was diagnosed as having benign intracranial hypertension. The headaches and papilledema resolved on oral steroids. The 2 conditions are discussed with emphasis on their etiologies and pathogenetic mechanisms. An attempt is made to relate the 2 conditions through a common mechanism. Both syndromes may have a disorder of CSF dynamics which leads to an overaccumulation of CSF in the tissues of the central nervous system.
