N2  - Malignant external otitis (MEO) is a severe variant of external otitis. As originally described by Chandler in 1968, MEO is an infection of the external ear canal, usually due to Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which is associated with systemic invasion, significant neurologic sequelae, and a high mortality rate. The vast majority of cases have occurred in elderly adults with diabetes mellitus. We present a case of MEO occurring in a 6 yr old child, and have compared the findings in our case with those in the adult literature.
