N2  - Microsurgical techiques, which have already been exploited in other disciplines for a long time, have recently been greatly extended to include the field of reconstructive and plastic surgery, thanks to the progress made in the surgery of small vessels and peripheral nerves. This had led to the appearance of new therapeutic procedures, first in emergency for traumatological surgery: digital replantations (whose indications are now well codified), soon followed by replantations of other segments of the body, accidentally amputated, and providing a sufficient vascular pedicle. But, in addition to emergencies, repair surgery has also undergone a revolution with microsurgical transfers of microrevascularized and, if necessary, microreinnervated tissues. An example of this is our experience of over 110 free transfers since 1974. The free skin flap is one of the major acquisitions. Donor sites are many and various (groin flaps, delto-pectoral flaps, dorsalis pedis flaps, axillary flaps), but two sites offer the original peculiarity of using surplus tissue removed in esthetic surgery, the lateral mammary flap and the inferior gluteal flap. Sensory free skin flaps, which are both revascularised and reinnervated can be used to restore either protective sensibility in a lower member (dorsalis pedis, delto-pectoral, axillary and lateral mammary flaps) or finer and discriminatory sensibility in the hand (first web flaps from the foot, toe pulp flaps). Numerous other revascularized tissue transfers may be used in a wide range of repairs which could not be achieved with traditional techniques: scalp transfers to the hairy areas of the face; fat tissue transfers for hemi-facial atophy; reinnervated muscle transfers for facial palsy; bone transfers for major bone defects; greater omentum transfers to the cranial vault or on the leg; intestinal transfers for the repair of the oesophagus. But microsurgical transfers can also be used to mobilise several tissue structures 'en bloc' with a single vascular pedicle. This procedure includes composite tissue transfers. Musculo-cutaneous transfers may or may not be carried out with reinnervation of the muscle, with maximum use of sensori-motor transfers with skin and muscle reinnervation (neuro-vascular myo-cutaneous dorsalis pedis flap). Osteo-cutaneous tranfers offer an opportunity for daring repairs in lower members (osteo cutaneous iliac flaps with deep circumflex iliac vascular pedicle). Finally, second toe transfers can be used for repair of the thumb or a long finger for some severe mutilations of the hand. All these new methods clearly illustrate the wealth and variety of the possibilities open to reconstructive microsurgery, which has already been promoted from the level of a mere technique (micro-neuro-vascular repair) to become a veritable specialty in its own right (free tissue transfer surgery).
