N2  - 45 patients with hemifacial spasm were analyzed retrospectively. The age in which the initial spasm was ranged from 18 yr to 77 yr, with a median age of 47 yr. Younger children (under 20 yr), and older persons (over 70 yr) were rarely sick. Women are more frequently afflicted than men, in a ratio of about 5 to 1. 40 patients were treated by facial nerve block at the stylomastoid foramen. Facial nerve blocks were done 72 times; 42 times (58%) were needle insertion only. 15 times (21%) were needle insertion with a small amount of pure alcohol, and the remainder (21%) were unsuccessful blocks. Duration of the nerve block effect was generally less than one year. The spasm recurred usually within 6 months in those patients treated by needle insertion only. However, with pure alcohol, it was longer. Unfortunately, the use of alcohol always resulted in a longer duration of facial palsy after nerve block. It was revealed by the enquete that almost all the patients did not consider the facial nerve block as a 'godsend'. The most popular reasons for this were severe pain during nerve block procedure, facial palsy after nerve block, and the inevitable recurrence of the spasm within a year.
