N2  - Thirty seven cases Bell's palsy were treated with the stellate ganglion block (SGB). All cases were retrospectively analyzed by sex, age and seasonal rate of occurence plus the effect of SGB on the disease. The following results were obtained. It was in winter that Bell's palsy occurred with the highest frequency followed by spring. Bell's palsy occurred more frequently in males than in females and this was most noticeable in those in their fifties. The cases in which the disease occurred in winter proved worse than those in spring. Within 10 days after the onset, those cases treated with SGB had a good prognosis. However, when this treatment was started later, the prognosis was bad. One case after 25 years had passed, showed a little improvement with the SGB. From these results it is suggested, first, that cold stimulation likely causes Bell's palsy and inhibits recovery from the disease. Second, it is thought that Bell's palsy improved remarkably with SGB when started early after the onset because the increase of the blood flow before the manifestation of irreversible degeneration of the facial nerve is essential. It is concluded that SGB must be started as early as possible after the onset.
