N2  - An autopsy case of a 25-yr-old female patient with gradually developing dyspnea and quadriplegia was presented. The patient had an operative treatment of otitis media at the age of 17 when she had a transient postoperative facial palsy. Since then, drowsiness tended to increase gradually. In and after the middle of May 1972, she began to complain of neck pruritus, numbness of a part distal to the bilateral upper extremities and general weakness. These symptoms became intensified gradually, with manifestations of headache, dysphagia and hypersalivation. Due to a fever (38-39°C), she was admitted to our hospital on July 1, 1972. On admission she had bilateral myosis, hyperreflexia of lower extremities and hyporeflexia of upper extremities. Babinski's signs, Chaddock's signs and Mayer's signs indicated abnormalities bilaterally. The general conditions deteriorated gradually following her hospitalization. She became comatous on July 8 and died on the night of July 10. The autopsy revealed bronchopneumonia, gastric ulcer and cystitis. There were mild cerebral swelling an both tonsillar and uncal herniation. Demyelination, gliosis, perivascular cuff, microthrombi and necroses in the lateral side of the medulla, in the basis of the 4th ventricle and around the cerebral aqueduct were found microscopically. Nerve cells were normal. According to available literature, there are two types of brain stem encephalitis; a type without strong changes in nerve cells and axons as in the present case and the other type with these changes like the case reported by Mukaiyama et al. We discussed about these and the etiology.
