N2  - Reports of cerebellar injuries are less often noted than those of cerebral injuries, because the cerebellum anatomically holds a specific position and many patients with cerebellar injuries die due to direct brain stem injuries or hemorrhage into the posterior fossa. However, if patients do recover from the acute stage in cerebellar injuries, their prognosis is extremely good. A 13-year-old boy fell into a grave and an iron bar pierced his neck. He was admitted to our department for evaluation of disturbance of consciousness. At that time he was delirious and he had right facial palsy, a skew deviation and curious spontaneous ocular movement, but no paresis of extremities. A plain skull roentgenogram showed a fracture of the occipital bone and the intracranial bony fragment. We suspected that the iron bar had penetrated into the posterior fossa, but no surgical treatment was done because CT scan showed only a small hematoma of the right cerebellar hemisphere and deep seated bony fragments. He recovered day by day with conservative therapy and he was discharged with right facial palsy, right auditory disturbance and loss of right gag reflex. These symptoms would be induced by direct injury of cranial nerves. After six months he complained of little interference with his daily life and some neurological deficits. Skew deviation found in cerebellar injuries was first reported by Gordon Holmes. Thereafter there have been many reports about this matter. The relationship between skew deviations and the brain stem or cerebellum has been reported by many authors. It is generally accepted that a skew deviation is associated with brain stem lesions but accurate etiology of skew deviation is unknown. We think that the skew deviation is more likely to be induced by complicated brain stem lesions than by one brain stem lesion.
