KW  - plastic surgery
KW  - review
N2  - In the treatment of patients with unilateral facial paralysis, symmetry of movements is generally the highest aim. Re-innervation of the paralyzed muscles should first be considered. Electromyographically demonstrable denervation potentials indicate that the muscles are still viable. Re-innervation can be obtained by restoring the continuity of the facial nerve, e.g. by end-to-end anastomosis or by insertion of a free nerve graft. In cases where the proximal stump of an interrupted facial nerve is not accessible, free nerve grafts can be used to bring nerve impulses from branches of the normal, contralateral facial nerve across the midline of the face to nerve branches on the paralyzed side. In cases where the facial muscles have degenerated or are congenitally absent, a muscular 'motor' should be brought to the paralyzed side. This can be done with free muscle grafts or with transposed local muscles, usually the muscles of mastication. In order to give these muscles spontaneous dynamic action, cross-face grafts can again be used to innervate them. When patients do not choose or need to undergo these rather extensive operations, or when they are in poor general health, we resort to methods of static correction. Many of these can comfortably be performed under local anaesthesia. Because of the absence of movement and other drawbacks, these methods should, in our opinion, generally be considered last.
