N2  - The histochemical, histologic, electromyographic and electron microscopic findings in the experimental studies strongly suggest that whole muscle bellies transplanted two to three weeks after denervation will survive and become reinnervated.1-2. The importance of denervation before grafting has been proposed by other authors and more experimental work is needed to make the role of denervation clear. In clinical studies two modifications of the technique of transplantation to the eyelids have improved the results and will appear later in the series. The change of lining of the nasal tunnel from silicone sheet to vein graft eliminated infections of the nasal tunnel with concomitant necrosis of the tendons of he transplant. The transplant of two muscle bellies, one to the upper and one to the lower eyelid, proved to be unnecessary and the elimination of the muscle belly in the normal upper eyelid removed the risk of constriction of the normal rima. The results of the operations to improve eyelid function are satisfactory. In the unsuccessful procedures the failure more often was a result of tenodesis on both sides of the nasal tunnel than a poor take of the muscle graft. In the patients in whom transplant was made to the lips the static support of the mouth was as good as the conventional fascia lata transplant and, in addition, the muscle grafts gave some mobility to the paralyzed half of the lips even if the graft contractions at the paralyzed side, as a rule, were weaker than on the normal side. In the patients, in whom grafts were attached to partially paralyzed muscles the results depended upon whether the recipient muscle had a sufficient number of functioning motor units to support the reinnervation of the graft. It appears that at least 10 to 15 percent of the motor function of the paralyzed recipient muscle must remain to secure adequate reinnervation of the graft.
