N2  - The transferred muscle undergoes some degenerative atrophy and its bulk is reduced during the period of reinnervation. In the patients, the acquired contraction of the transferred muscle was weaker than its original excursion. As the rabbit experiments of Terzis et al. have demonstrated, the orthotopical or heterotopical transfer of skeletal muscles diminished their powers of contraction. The acquired contraction therefore is less than that which existed originally. Use of the larger muscles, such as the gracilis and latissimus dorsi muscles (not always transferred in their entirety), obtains better results than that of such smaller muscles as the extensor digitorum brevis muscle. When the transplant is too bulky, the excess portion can later be excised. For reconstruction of the facial expression, better results were obtained with muscles innervated through the seventh nerve either ipsilaterally or contralaterally. In long-standing Bell's palsy, a combination method with cross-facial nerve grafting is now recommended.
