N2  - A lesion of the facial nerve producing facial palsy most commonly occurs in its intratemporal course. Recent progress in microsurgery of the temporal bone has permitted a safe approach to the facial nerve from the internal auditory meatus to the stylomastoid foramen. Today nearly all intratemporal facial nerve lesions are accessible to surgical repair. The knowledge of this possibility is of utmost importance to plastic surgeons involved with the diagnosis and treatment of facial paralysis, because the restoration of continuity of the facial nerve is preferred to any type of substitution operation whenever possible. In order to frequency the most common causes of intratemporal facial palsy are idiopathic or Bell's palsy (39.7 percent), trauma (24.7 percent), tumors (12.5 percent), herpes zoster oticus (6.8 percent), and acute and chronic otitis media (5.5 percent). Electrodiagnosis should be used whenever the slightest doubt in the clinical evaluation of facial movements exists, in order to confirm or exclude total paralysis, and in every patient with complete paralysis in order to assess the underlying condition of the fibers of the motor nerve.
