N2  - We studied 18 patients with erythema chronicum migrans (ECM), Lyme arthritis, or both who developed neurologic abnormalities. Six of the 14 patients (43%) with ECM remembered having been bitten by a tick at the site of the lesion 3 to 20 days before its onset. Neurologic abnormalities, which appeared either while ECM was still present or one to six weeks after it had faded, included aseptic meningitis, encephalitis, chorea, cerebellar ataxia, cranial neuritis (including bilateral facial palsy), motor and sensory radiculoneuritis, mononeuritis multiplex, and myelitis, in various combinations, but encephalitis and facial palsy each also occurred alone. The usual pattern was fluctuating meningoencephalitis (nine patients) with superimposed cranial and peripheral radiculoneuropathy. At the beginning of the illness, associated symptoms included migratory arthralgias, chills and fever, malaise and fatigue, backache, and myalgias; signs included low-grade fever, malar rash, and lymphadenopathy. Frank arthritis was usually a later finding, beginning 1 to 9 months after the onset of the illness. During attacks, 15 of the 18 patients (83%) had cryoglogulins present in serum, which consisted of quantitatively greater amounts of IgM than IgG, and decreased or disappeared during remission. These findings support the hypothesis that circulating immune complexes may play a pathogenetic role in Lyme disease. Ten patients recovered neurologically within 2 weeks to 8 months, but 8 others continued to have residual weakness or cerebral symptoms. Systemic corticosteroids appeared to suppress meningeal symptoms rapidly, but did not seem to affect established parenchymal lesions. Two additional patients, who could only be classified as having possible Lyme disease, are noteworthy because hemiparesis was part of their clinical picture. Erythema chronicum migrans is the diagnostic marker for the illness. Without it, a course of recurrent meningoencephalitis during at least a period of several months with superimposed cranial neuritis or radiculoneuritis, particularly if accompanied by cryoglobulins or followed by arthritis, is quite suggestive of Lyme disease. When the diagnosis cannot be made on clinical grounds alone, a history of preceding tick bite, onset in the summer months, or geographic clustering with those who have erythema chronicum migrans, Lyme arthritis, or both, may be important clues.
