N2  - The use of polytomography, electroneurography, and level diagnosis have radically changed the approach to peripheral facial paralysis, and have resulted in a more precise etiology and diagnosis, evolution of the functional state of the nerve at an early stage of the paralysis, more reliable prognosis, and more selective therapeutic indications. Progress in microsurgical techniques in neuro-otology has created the possibility to propose appropriate therapy for all parts of the facial nervous system.
