N2  - The clinical and electrophysiological criterias for medical or surgical treatment of Bell's palsy are: clinical severity (complete, not complete) estimated by muscular testing; psychosocial aspect of the patient; electrophysiological tests. Electroneuronography looks the most used test (<95% of degeneration during the two first weeks good prognosis, ≥ 95% bad prognosis in half the cases); - integrated electromyography can separate these last bad cases (> 20 μV good prognosis, and < 20 μV bad prognosis): 14th day after onset looks the important date for this test. Prednisone or A.C.T.H. therapy avoids 95% of degeneration and surgical treatment looks indicated when the number of the degenerated fibers reaches 90%. The approach is discussed with no consenus: the 2nd and 3rd portions are more often decompressed. Total decompression is advocated by Pulec when eye is dry; he also notes its interest for finding little tumors not diagnosed. (10% of cases of author's decompressions). U. Fisch begins decompression in the last portion and decompresses the nerve until he finds the point where it comes back excitable. The extent of decompression and the approach are determinated by localisation tests (Schirmer-Blatt).
