N2  - On the basis of 67 cases, criteria for surgical treatment of traumatic facial palsy are given. As regards the chronology, of the 37 immediate cases, 26 were operated; of the 30 secondary cases 12 received surgical treatment. The clinical examination must look for fracture in the external auditory meatus. With audiometry and vestibulometry middle ear deafness indicated the 2nd portion while total hearing loss allows a translabyrinth approach. Topographic tests (Blatt, Schirmer) give doubtful results as the petrous nerve or chorda alone can be injured. EMG and electroneurography should be carried out. Among the roentgenographic approaches Schuller, frontal and sagittal tomographic techniques are utilised, although sometimes this diagnosis is quite difficult in practice; if the topographic results remain uncertain a total decompression may be considered. The delay and quality of recovery depend on the type of nervous lesion involved (in 48 operated cases). In 6 nerve section cases recovery took 4 to 7 months. In 21 cases with commotion, 13 recovered for more than 50%. Recovery in contusion and compression cases shows a less favourable prognosis (20 cases observed). The post operative audiologic evaluation showed 2 partial labyrinthisations and 1 middle ear deafness (pocket retraction).
