N2  - 46 cases of Guillain-Barre syndrome or acute polyradiculopathy are analysed. The disease affected all age groups and both sexes equally. A relatively higher incidence among Malays and absence among Indians was observed. One third of patients had preceding upper respiratory tract infection. Relapse rate was at least 5% in the short period of follow-up. Most patients developed maximal signs and symptoms within two weeks but some ran a subacute course for two months. The distribution of muscle weakness was noted in detail. Almost all patients had weakness of the lower limb; one-third had facial palsy and one quarter had respiratory muscle weakness or bulbar paresis. Severe sensory loss was virtually absent. Areflexia was a cardinal sign. Presence of bilateral radicular pain as elicited by a positive Laseque's sign was found to be an important bedside diagnostic clue, being present in 95% of cases tested. Two district clinical variants were observed - the Miller-Fisher Syndrome and an ataxic (pseudo-tabetic) form that ran a protracted course. 60% had raised CSF protein. Nerve conduction studies were of diagnostic rather than prognostic value in doubtful cases. Mortality rate was below 3% although 5 patients required assisted ventilation. Follow-up revealed that full recovery was the rule within 6 months with or without steroids, and that with prolonged weakness or ataxia they continued to recover even as long as after two years.
