N2  - Current surgical methods and tendencies are reviewed from an oto-laryngological viewpoint. In lope ears the combined method has stood the test very well. Reconstruction in anotia is not always satisfactory; the combination with deformities of the external auditory canal, of the middle and the inner ear, as well as of the facial cranium must be taken into account. The replacement of parts of the pinna is a minor problem. Reference is made to the possibility of reimplantation of separated parts. Current operations for deformities of the nose are mentioned. The use of conserved cartilage is described. In frontal defects paladon has proved a very successful inlay. Partial defects can be closed by pedicled island flaps. Stenosis of the larynx is no longer treated by bolt inlays of long duration; the implantation of septal cartilage including its mucoperichondrium leads much quicker to a permanent and adequate lumen. Smaller tracheal stenoses can be treated by circumscribed local management. Larger stenoses need either extensive plastic operations with large flaps or transversal resection. After total laryngectomy a specially formed canal between the upper and of the trachea and the hypopharynx allows a new voice to be developed. The transplantation of denervated muscles in facial palsy, special operations in paralysis of deglutition and some general problems are mentioned briefly. A voluminous list of recent literature gives the opportunity of studying further details.
