N2  - Forty-four cases of meningococcal meningitis in children at one hospital between 1971 and 1975 inclusive were studied to document the course and complications of this disease in children in the current therapeutic era. The mortality was 5%. Of the 41 survivors 76% were healthy 1 to 5 yr after the episode of meningitis. Permanent severe sequelae (facial palsy, optic atrophy and ptosis) were seen in 3 (7%) of the survivors, and mild hearing loss, hyperactivity and nervousness were noted in 7 (17%). Electroencephalography was not useful in determining management or prognosis. Both the mortality and the frequency of early and late complications among the survivors were lower than those reported from earlier studies.
