N2  - Pathology, etiopathogenesis, pathophysiology, symptomatology and diagnosis of Bell's palsy are discussed. Among peripheral facial palsies of diverse origin, Bell's palsy is most frequent. It is acute entrapment lesion of the facial nerve in the fallopian canal but not caused by obvious pathology of neighboring structures as, e.g., otitis media. Contrary to previous opinion, this entrapment takes place regularly in the labyrinthine segment and its introitus (Fisch and Esslen 1972). Pathophysiology of Bell's palsy is the same as in other sorts of acute entrapment neuropathies. Primary edema of the facial nerve (inflammatory or non-inflammatory) causes pressure rise in the facial nerve which in turn leads to secondary edema. In this way, a vicious circle may arise damaging both nerve fibres and vessels of the facial nerve. Diagnosis of Bell's palsy is stated in particular by means of examinations mentioned in chapter three and by means of its typical course as described in chapter four. Attention is paid especially to the examination of the region of the internal meatal fundus and its nervous structures. Permanent facial paralysis does not take place in Bell's palsy and another cause, especially space occupying lesions, must be looked for.
