N2  - Treatment of Bell's palsy and other types of peripheral facial palsies are discussed separately. Because Bell's palsy is an acute entrapment neuropathy, only immediate decompression of the labyrinthine segment of the facial nerve performed in the early stage of the disorder may be effective. This decompression may be either pharmacological, using glucocorticoids, or surgical. Pharmacological decompression may be carried out either by systemic or local administration of glucocorticoids. Systemic administration is well known but not generally used because it is not without risk. Moreover, due to numerous contra-indications to the general administration of glucocorticoids, approximately 30% of all patients suffering from Bell's palsy must not be treated in this way. In patients without contraindications, systemic administration of glucocorticoids ought to be abudant. Nevertheless, even in carefully selected patients (i.e., without known contraindication), complications occur in 1-2% of cases. Compared to general administration of glucocorticoids, application of ACTH is even less effective and more dangerous.
