N2  - Basic anatomical data concerning the facial nerve, especially its petrous portion, are summarized in the introduction. Contrary to other nerves of a similar diameter, the facial nerve consists of one single thick fascicle, in its labyrinthine and tympanic segment, surrounded by a tight perineural sheath. The introitus of the fallopian canal in the fundus of the internal acoustic meatus is the narrowest part of the whole fallopian canal - this fact being of crucial importance in cases of facial nerve entrapment. In chapter II, the most important facts concerning pathophysiology of the facial nerve are reviewed. The different grades of facial nerve injury are discussed and compared with the classification of Seddon, Sunderland and McComas et al. According to clinical observation and anatomical peculiarities of the facial nerve, a more accurate classification of grades of facial nerve lesions has been proposed: subclinical lesion (with local slowing of conduction velocity), local neurapraxia (excitability of the peripheral part of the affected nerve fibre preserved), extended neurapraxia (nerve fibre excitability lost) and partial neurotmesis (including both axonotmesis and lesion of the endoneurium). Pure axonotmesis which is not followed by aberrant regeneration is not observed in facial nerve lesions. Extended neurapraxia is sometimes called degeneration (denervation), less exactly, but this stage is reversible and may not be followed by true axonotmesis in all cases. New classification of the grades of facial nerve lesions seems inevitable because of discrepancies between previous classifications and clinical observations. In chapter III, various kinds of facial nerve examinations are reviewed critically. Without a doubt, examination of the motor function of the facial nerve is the most important. In cases of paresis, careful testing of the strength of mimic muscles ought to be carried out. When paralysis occurs, muscle testing has to be replaced by nerve excitability studies, especially the maximal nerve excitability test or evoked electromyography. When performing evoked electromyography, attention should be focussed on the amplitude of the evoked muscle action potential which is proportional to the number of motor fibres affected only by local neurapraxia. Among numerous methods of facial nerve examination, only nerve excitability studies enable us to differentiate between local and extended neurapraxia - this fact being of paramount importance during follow up of acute facial palsies. Among other methods of examination, Schirmer's test of lacrimation, otoscopy, X-rays of the petrous bone, examination of the function of neighbouring nervous structures, etc., are very important. Finally, the exact site of a facial nerve lesion and its cause ought to be determined. When applying rules of topognosis of the lesion to the facial nerve, the greater resistance of small diameter nerve fibres to damage (i.e., secretory fibres) must be borne in mind.
