N2  - A case of tuberculous otitis media is reported. The patient was a 45-yr-old male who had fistula symptom, bilateral facial paralysis and complete deafness. The authors reviewed 25 cases (30 ears) of tuberculous otitis media reported in Japan since 1965. They noticed the remarkable transition of clinical pictures of tuberculous otitis media in recent years. A granulous type of tuberculous lesion was frequently found in the recent cases. Based on the characteristics of the recent cases, the authors established the new criteria of the diagnosis of tuberculous otitis media: chronic otitis media resistant to various antibiotics except for antituberculous agents; a marked formation of granulation tissue in the tympanic cavity and its extension to the external ear canal; disorder in bone conduction; presence of active and/or cured lung tuberculosis; accompanying of the periaural lymphadenitis in children; positive intracutaneous tuberculin reaction; facial paralysis. Chronic otitis media presented with 3 out of 7 items should be suspected as tuberculous. Chronic middle ear disease presented with 5 out of 7 items can be clinically diagnosed as tuberculous otitis media. The final diagnosis should be established by biopsy of the lesion or by bacterial examination. The treatment consists of antituberculous agents and surgical removal of tuberculous regions.
