N2  - This paper results from a clinical study of 50 patients with idiopathic facial palsy (Bell's palsy). Incidence of Bell's palsy is constant in each year. Bell's palsy occurs most commonly in adults (20-40 yr). Both sexes and both sides are equally affected. Bell's palsy is slightly more frequent in cold weathers in the younger age group. One third of the patients have preceding histories of infection (12 cases), exposure to cold (4 cases) or compression (3 cases). It is suggested that some cases of Bell's palsy are due to viral infection or ischemia of the facial nerve. 60% of the patients recover completely within 50 days (rapid recovery), and most of the rest recover in 3 mth. If there are signs of recovery of facial palsy before the 20th day, rapid and complete recovery can be expected. The age of the patients and therapeutic measures, including administration of steroids, have little influence on the prognosis of facial palsy. Hyperacusis and pain behind the ear seem to be unfavorable prognostic symptoms.
