N2  - The cerebellum, being a special branch of the CNS has functional, structural and biochemical aspects of its own, which explains the existence of specific cerebellar affections. Thus, cerebellar atrophy is a manifestation of a persistent biochemical dysfunction, which may be of genetic origin or induced by any of a number of agents. It is not of fundamental interest to the physician, but sometimes serves as a clear sign of neurotoxicity, as the consequence of which a systemic disease may be discovered or a therapy changed.
