N2  - The effective obstetric intervention on newborns have been analysed. Forceps delivery leads to a high incidence of morbidity rate, facial palsy and intracranial injury. The outcome of the neonate is further modified by the indication for forceps application as well as associated factors such as poor ante-natal care, poor socio-economic status and parity of the mothers, which have been responsible for a high perinatal loss as compared to the controls. A high incidence of the respiratory distress syndrome was observed in cesarean-delivered infants as compared to infants delivered by the vaginal route. Internal podalic version was hazardous to the neonate, with a greater predilection to ward asphyxiation, still-birth and higher perinatal loss.
