N2  - A series of 38 patients with otitis media purulenta acuta is reviewed. In 9 cases (23.7%) the suppurative process runs a torpid, protracted course, with combined or perceptive hearing loss. Reduction of the protective power of the organism due to diabetes mellitus, multiple myeloma, heart defect and postpartum period is accepted as a contributory cause. Infection is usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus and influenza viruses, independently or in combination. Despite the treatment performed in 2 patients the otitis became chronic, one patient developing otitis media serosa with labyrinthitis and practical deafness, while the other patient was discharged as cured but with substantial impairment of the sense of hearing. In two of the patients with influenza otitis, untimely exudate evacuation resulted in the development of otitis serosa with labyrinthitis (in one case), and facial palsy (in the other).
