N2  - The authors report their experience with 32 cases of facial paralysis occurring during progressive chronic otitis. Twenty were preoperative. The conclusions which may be drawn are as follows: (1) In general it occurs in cases of severe chronic otitis with large destructive cholesteatomatous and osteitic lesions. (2) Surgery is often difficult, long, and full of unexpected findings and risks. (3) The development of facial paralysis in a case of progressive chronic otitis is an indication for immediate surgery, on the same basis as a fistula of the HSCC, which is, in fact, often present in association. (4) The lesions encountered are much more extensive at the time of secondary (11) rather than at primary surgery (9). (5) The extent of the lesions (13 cases of fistula or labyrinthine destruction) and auditory impairment (16 kophenias or subkophenias) are such that tympanoplasty is useless. The authors report their long term results: 13 cases of complete recovery, 4 nil and 3 partial (2 as a result of a superficial cervical plexus graft). They raise the problem of the management when a markedly contused nerve is discovered: simple liberation with incision of its sheath or immediate graft? The other 12 were postoperative. Five were cases operated upon by the authors. There was 100% recovery in all cases, but 1 patient, in whom the nerve followed an atypical course, required decompression surgery. In the other 7, the authors performed 3 decompressions followed by an excellent result, apart from 1 case in which the nerve was markedly contused. In 2 cases a superficial cervical plexus graft and a XII-VII anastomosis proved necessary.
