N2  - If the plainness resulting from paralysis of the facial nerve has a high psychological repercussion, these lesions can generally be improved with surgical treatment. An etiological treatment is often indicated: decompression or repair of the nerve (suture or graft). It has to be tried. When the treatment fails or is impossible, another treatment has to be carried out. Its indication depends on the clinical picture, on the etiology, on the electromyographic investigation and on the patient's wish. The only palliative treatment, which can restore an emotional and symmetrical face, is the trans-facial graft; if it cannot be carried out, neurotization will be tried and exceptionally a transplantation. Only in case of failure or of impossibility will palliative methods adapted to every case be used (myoplasty, Muhlbauer's magnets).
