N2  - The seventh cranial nerve is a complicated structure which contains sensory and parasympathetic elements in addition to the motor fibres. Following some introductory comments on its embryology and anatomy, the problems of localisation and the clinical symptoms of central and peripheral paralysis of the facial nerve are discussed. Further on there is a more detailed description of defined clinical syndromes, crossed paralytic types, peculiarities in infancy, important criteria concerning differential diagnosis of cerebral excitement and, finally for the practising doctor, special up-to-date clinical pictures showing paresis of the facial nerve.
