N2  - Idiopathic facial paralysis and paralysis due to zoster appear to be the result of pathological processes with identical sequelae: anoxic oedema of the nerve which is constrained within the inelastic confines of the Fallopian canal. In all cases, discovery of facial palsy should indicate urgent medical treatment in which vasodilators and steroids play an essential part. Other forms of treatment are debatable. At the same time as treatment is instituted, clinical assessment with electrophysiological studies of the paralysed neuromuscular complex, and tests to localise the lesion, both of which should be repeated and compared, allow the severity of the palsy to be determined, and the timing of later decompressive surgical treatment to be planned. No test in current use will give the information necessary to decide on very early operation. Where decompression is indicated, the authors use the technique of Professor P. Fleury which involves an enlarged endaural and transmeatal approach to a furrow which is sculpted in the posterior wall of the EAM while conserving bony support anteriorly - an essential feature; this allows easy decompression of the nerve in its second and third portions without risk to the hearing, and with maintenance of a strictly normal otoscopical appearance and a normal mastoid. A modification of this technique will even allow decompression of the geniculate ganglion. Finally, review of the post-operative results encourages decompression of the nerve during the second month, in order to obtain maximal recovery of the best quality.
