N2  - The authors review their experience with 32 cases of facial palsy occurring in active chronic suppurative otitis media. 20 cases were preoperative. They have drawn the following conclusions from studying these cases: Paralysis usually follows severe chronic otitis involving vast destruction by cholesteatoma and osteitis. The operation is often difficult, lengthy, and full of suprise findings and risks. The appearance of a facial palsy during active chronic otitis media is an indication for urgent surgery, in exactly the same way as a fistula of the lateral semi-circular canal, which is in any case often associated with it. The lesions discovered are often more extensive when the procedure is a revision one (11) than when it is a primary procedure (9). The extent of the disease (13 fistulae or destruction of the labyrinth) and of the effect on the hearing (16 cases of total or subtotal deafness) preclude any attempt at reconstruction. The authors report their longterm results: 13 cases with complete recovery, 4 with no recovery, 3 with partial recovery, 2 of which had a graft from the superficial cervical plexus. They discuss the problem of the action to take if the nerve is found to be grossly damaged: simple decompression with incision of the sheath or immediate graft? The other 12 cases were post-operative. 5 of them were operated on by the authors. All had 100% recovery, but in one of them the facial nerve took an unusual course, and revision surgery for decompression was necessary. For the other 7, the authors carried out decompressions in 3 and these were followed by an excellent result except in one case where the nerve was very contused. Two further cases were dealt with, one by using a graft from the superficial cervical plexus, and the other by a XII-VII anastomosis.
