N2  - In Van Buchem's disease bone formation dominates over the resorption of bone. There is pronounced osteosclerosis of the petrous bones and afterwards considerable enlargement of the mandible and chin. As this inherited syndrome progresses over the years, intracranial space is reduced. There is early compression of cranial nerves, e.g. peripheral facial palsy and trigeminal neuralgia. Later on, there may be anosmia, diminishing vision resulting from optic atrophy; moreover exophthalmus. The third and fourth nerves become affected if the superior orbital fissure is narrowed. Compression and deformation of the brain may lead to symptoms such as aphasia, hemianopsia and epilepsy. In advanced stages, the sellar hyperostosis causes injury to the hypophysis resulting in hypopituitarism, sometimes in pan-apituitarism. The spinal cord may also be compressed. A long cavity was seen in the cervical part of the spinal cord of one patient, causing a syringomyelia-like symptomatology. In the pathogenesis of Van Buchem's disease many factors play a role. Firstly one is confronted with an abnormal genetic predisposition, manifesting itself in a disturbed equilibrium between the activity of osteoblasts, stimulated by calcitonin, and that of the osteoclasts, activated by parathormone. If the thyroid gland produces a too high calcitonin level in plasma, strumectomia is indicated, followed by substitution-therapy. In the course of Van Buchem's disease the following stages may be distinguished: The early stage is characterized by irritation and/or paralysis of cranial-, sensory- and spinal nerves; The mature stage is characterized by brain- and spinal cord compression. At autopsy we found that the basal face of the frontal lobes of one patient showed a marked symmetrical impression, caused by pressure of the hyperplastic orbital parts of the frontal bone. Furthermore, an aspecific chronic arachnitis was found, causing a constriction of the optic chiasm. A slowly progressive pressure of the bone of the skull may cause, in that stage, neurological and psychiatric symptoms, such as deficient motor-coordination, reduction of memory and other psychic symptoms; The final stage is characterized by increased intracranial pressure followed by brain atrophy. Microscopic examination brought to light that strange extravasations had come about in the cortex and sub-cortex. We termed them 'pseudo-plaques'. They contained lipoproteins. Their shape varied from drop-like, rope of pearls, stars, rosettes, bunches of grapes or mulberies. The extravasations originate from the capillaries, penetrating their biochemically changed walls. These effusions inundated the cerebral parenchyma and brought about vasal compression into the environment, resulting in encephalonecroses, filled with exsudation. After some time the necrotic brain contained fatty acids. Glial fibrillar structures may be found in the final stage of the disease in the remnants of necrotic foci, resembling 'plaques trichosiques'. They are, however, not identical to genuine Alzheimer fibrillar plaques.
