N2  - Facial-nerve paralysis in children is uncommon, but it is not as rare as was once thought. Most of these paralyses occur later in growth and development as a result of acquired causes, (traumatic or infectisus) although a significant number occur during the neonatal period. infectious) of the presence of associated congenital anomalies, particularly as exemplified by congenital unilateral lower-lip palsies, the early diagnosis of neonatal facial paralysis assumes considerable significance for effective clinical managment. Treatment considerations take primary importance in the acquired facial paralyses and depend on standard tests for facial-nerve function as well as topographic tests for lesion localization. Persistent or recurrent 'idiopathic' facial paralysis requires a thorough diagnostic evaluation, which takes precedence over therapeutic considerations. In most cases, a known cause will be uncovered and will help determine any therapy required.
