N2  - There is still no universally accepted treatment for Bell's palsy. Most physicians use some form of medical therapy for affected patients. Although surgical decompression of the intratemporal facial nerve in patients with Bell's palsy is still highly controversial, there are instances in which it has proved beneficial. Such surgery, though, is based on sequential testing of facial-nerve function and the demonstration of progressive compromise to function (in spite of conservative medical management). More prospective, controlled, double-blind studies employing both medications and surgery are needed before an effective protocol for therapy can be established.
