N2  - The evolution of surgery for facial rehabilitation has been a fascinating study, beginning with static support and evolving through nerve grafts, muscle grafts, and nerve-muscle grafts. In spite of outstanding recent contributions, there are still patients in whom reconstruction is not possible. In these patients, symmetry and mimetic function must be restored by alternative procedures, such as muscle transfers, myomectomies, neuronectomies, rhitidectomies, and the like. At the present time, static symmetry is considered less desirable in facial rehabilitation if dynamic symmetry and motion of the face can be achieved. In principle, the proper innervation of the muscles of facial expression is by the facial nerve itself. This is possible following injury or surgical resection through direct nerve anastomosis or autogenous grafting. This restoration of the neuromuscular mechanism yields a physiologic result that may approximate the preinjury state and is reflected in the cosmetic and functional recovery achieved. However, in the current state of facial-nerve rehabilitation, one must realize that there is a continuing evolution of ideas. The future holds prospects for many new and exciting concepts that must yet come to clinical reality.
