N2  - The solved and unsolved problems of intra- and extratemporal surgery of the facial nerve are discussed by the authors. On the basis of an evaluation of the various diagnostic methods, the difficulties and deficiencies of prognosis are outlined. The data of the recent literature and personal experience gained in the material are dealt with, taking into consideration the technical performance of the reconstructive operations and the expected function. The results of 21 intra-, 11 extra- and 16 combined transplantations are reported. On the basis of the results, it is emphasized that in case of transplantation not so much the length of the transplant as the degree of the lesion is of decisive importance regarding the time of regeneration. Also, the earliest possible reconstruction is to be taken into consideration. In the author's opinion, in case of Bell's palsy, the discission of the nerve sheath is not an obligatory, but rather a facultative principle, regarding the importance of the conservation of the nerves in the epineurium. In case of Bell's palsy, the exclusion diagnosis must be confirmed exactly by as many as possible examination methods, since behind the clinical picture of Bell's palsy severe morphological changes (neurinoma, tumor metastasis, cholesteatoma, etc.) may be hidden.
