N2  - 49 cases of traumatic facial palsy due to head injury and 75 cases of Bell's palsy were examined. Traffic accidents with traumatic facial palsy are most frequently observed in elementary-school aged pupils than adults. In the traumatic facial palsy, male patients are observed far more frequently than female patients for ages from 20 to 39, the former having head injury more often. On the contrary, among patients with Bell's palsy of the same ages, the occurrence rate is almost equal for male and female. In cases of traumatic facial palsy, no correlation was noted between the recovery rate and the age of the patient. Head injury with traumatic facial palsy is observed most frequently in the temporal region. The lesion of the facial nerve inferred from the clinical symptoms does not always correspond to surgical findings. The EMG showed 3 types: the first type is observed mostly in the group with onset of facial palsy within one week from the date of injury, and this type of facial palsy recovers in several months. The second type is observed mostly in the group having onset of facial palsy within a month and this type also recovers in several months. The third type is observed mostly in the group with onset occurring within one hour; this type rarely shows recovery. In Bell's palsy, no such types with regard to the lapse of time could be clearly observed. Surgical indications are as follows: most cases of immediate traumatic facial palsy have a good surgical indication if there is no sign of recovery after several weeks. In the delayed traumatic facial palsy, because most of the cases show gradual recovery on EMG, after several weeks, it is not too late to perform surgical operation after 2-2.5 mth. Macroscopic evidence or dislocation of ossicles, etc. are helpful in dictating the surgical indication.
