N2  - Three patients with bilateral facial paralysis are described. The first patient presented with infectious mononucleosis and facial diplegia followed 14 days later by signs of polyradiculitis. The second patient had bilateral facial paracysis combined with acute polyradiculitis with tetraparesis and respiratory difficulties. The third patient was admitted with isolated facial diplegia, a possible monosymptomatic form of the Guillain-Barre syndrome. The various possible causes of bilateral facial paralysis are discussed.
