N2  - The prognosis for facialis paresis decisively depends on the time and type of treatment carried out. The diagnosis rests basically on the otological findings and on the results of X ray and functional middle and inner ear examinations. Between 1955 and 1975, 3938 patients with a chronic otitis media were operated in the Halle University ENT clinic and 48 patients were treated for a preoperative peripheral facialis paresis. Iatrogenous operation determined lesions have become more rare due to improved surgical techniques, although they still occur in about 1% of all cases. Follow up operations (1.9%) appear to be more dangerous than first time operations (0.9%). The treatment results underline the necessity for a very critical attitude in otogenous lesions of the facial nerve. Nothing could be worse for the prognosis than a hopeful waiting.
