N2  - The Research Project had been in progress from April 1, 1968 to March 1975 at Gudiyatnam Taluk of Tamil Nadu and it was financed by the Social and Rehabilitation Service of the Department of Health, Education and Welfare of the United States Government. The report is a book (covering 131 pages) on rehabilitation of leprosy patients. The results of the study are described in 8 chapters. This is a very useful index for leprosy patients with disability, whose condition is summarised in a 6 letter code. As a result of studying 5677 unselected leprosy patients, it was found that 33% showed some disability or other, and that involvement of upper limbs was more common than that of lower limbs. In the management of lepromatous leprosy folate and B-12 supplements may be valuable because of the high prevalence of folate and B-12 deficiency. Liver biopsies were done on 240 patients. Among 101 patients who showed no acid-fast bacilli in skin smears 22 patients revealed leprous granulomata in the liver. There was a sharp rise of leprous lesions in the liver as one moved from the polar tuberculoid (TT) to the polar lepromatous (LL) type. The clearance of these lesions depended on the duration of treatment. There was no significant correlation between the presence of tuberculoid granuloma in the liver and liver efficiency tests. A study of the social problems faced by 100 deformed and 100 non-deformed leprosy patients in the author's area revealed that the family and neighbors were friendly to 96-99% of non-deformed patients and to 84% of the deformed patients. Results of the study of nerves in 540 leprosy patients belonging to the 4 clinical types of leprosy were recorded. Electromyographic study of the muscles of the face has revealed the phenomenon of mis-reinnervation of these muscles in leprosy. Among 2416 leprosy patients with disability, the greatest prevalence is in the 2 decades over 30 yr. Deformities of the face were the most frequent single involvement. Regular administration of DDS in patients suffering from bacilliferous types of leprosy has been found to have beneficial effect on nerve function. It was proved that trophic ulcers can be treated at the home of the patients with plaster of paris (POP) casts or felt pad dressings. Under the local conditions, the patients were averse to having immobilization with POP cast at home. Fissures of anhidrotic feet were tested by soaking the feet for 45 min in water followed by a liberal application of vaseline and application of rubber latex solution to form a boot. When the boot was removed after 3 wk, fissures and hyperkeratosis had disappeared. In cases where repeated ulceration took place in the same area of the sole of the foot, devices to relieve weight bearing were necessary (e.g. fixed ankle brace walker and patellar tendon bearing shoes). These were provided for the village patients resulting in a significant decrease in the prevalence of plantar ulcers. Great deal of attention was paid to the retraining of farm workers who had to use plough and other tools in their normal work. Teaching of safe cooking methods also formed part of the training for women patients and men patients who had to handle fire in their occupation. The conclusion from the author's limited experience is that retraining and placement of disabled leprosy patients is possible and worthwhile. This cannot be done in the hospital. Among 494 leprosy patients investigated, 11% showed psychiatric disturbances. There was no correlation between the type of leprosy and psychiatric disorders. There appeared to be a significant correlation between the presence of deformity and the prevalence of mental illness.
