N2  - During the period of 1964 to 1976, we have treated 112 children under 6 yr old affected with facial palsy, which constituted 7.5% of the total 1501 patients with facial palsies seen during the same period. Incidences of facial palsies in the infants are signficantly different from those of the adults. In the former cases, there are lower incidence of Bell's palsy and higher incidences of otogenic and congenital palsies as compared to the latter. In order to surmount the diagnostic difficulties inherent in the infant palsy, the following examinations are essential; microscopic otoscopy; temporal bone radiography; electrodiagnostic examinations including NET, S-D curve and evoke electromyography; and, virological examination of blood serum. Administration of general anesthesia was often required in order to perform these procedures. In respect to findings through the above mentioned examinations, 3 interesting cases may be specifically mentioned: one case with masked mastoiditis; one, with bilateral facial palsy of otic origin; and another, with congenital palsy caused by muscular hypogenesia. Surgical treatment was highly recommended when the prognosis of the palsy appeared to be poor due to denervation of the facial nerve.
