N2  - The author reviews symptoms, reversibility of symptoms, prognosis, and treatment based on the large number of MS patients in the Gottingen documentation pool. As expected, spasticity, paresis, ataxia, visual and sensory disturbances and urinary dysfunction are dominating disorders in MS. Sexual dysfunction was found in 23 percent of male and 5 percent of female patients. Visual disturbances, diplopia, facial palsy, sensory disturbances of the face and perhaps tactile sensory disturbances were found to be more reversible than other symptoms. The author points out that 30 percent of patients are still at least partially employable 20 years after the onset of MS, and that the lifespan of the MS patient has increased considerably in the last 40 to 50 years. Spasticity, ataxia and bladder and bowel disturbances remain major problems in limiting working ability. (Cook - Newark, NJ)
