N2  - A neonate was accidentally given an infusion of 5% sodium bicarbonate and 20% glucose solutions into the right temporal artey. Skin necrosis and facial paralysis occurred, in the distribution area of the artery. The authors suggest the following points. To avoid the risk of an accident, the pulse should first be palpated before the infusion begins. Normal saline should be given first, and a watch kept for ischaemia. If ischaemia occurs during a hyperosmolar injection, heparin and streptokinase should be given at once, and thrombolytic therapy continued for as long as necessary. Should skin necrosis occur, then skin transplantation will be required to produce satisfactory healing and a good cosmetic result.
