N2  - When in 1972 the French Ophthalmological Society discussed the subject of this report they were undoubtedly hoping to remind ophthalmologists that this plastic surgery was an integral part of their speciality. It was immediately apparent to the chosen reporter that besides the honor that was being done to him there was an accompanying difficult task. An ophthalmologist above all, his experience had been insufficient to carry through such an enterprise on his own. It was thus that the 'Team' which has drawn up this report came to be formed. It comprises three ophthalmologists, two plastic surgeons and a neurosurgeon. The first chapters in this book deal with the surgical anatomy of the orbit, typical ophthalmological problems, such as enucleation and evisceration, and several aspects of maxillofacial fractures. More specific abnormalities, such as exophthalmia, facial clefts, colobomas, microsomia, microphthalmia etc., are described. Typical neurosurgical problems in craniofacial surgery and some typical eyelid plastic and cosmetic surgery are also described. In the main part of the book, however, emphasis is laid on the co-operation between the three specialities--ophthalmology, neurosurgery and plastic surgery--since most abnormalities demand a multidisciplinary approach. Lastly, the authors discuss the medicolegal aspects of orbitofacial injuries.
