N2  - The ever uneasy and precarious equilibrium between psychiatry and the law is well illustrated by a recent trial, concerning a man aged 22 yr who had been born with a rare and bizarre congenital disorder. Only some 100 cases of Moebius' syndrome or congenital facial diplegia, have been described and about 10% of these have been mentally subnormal. In a typical case there is degeneration of the corpus callosum and complete bilateral paralysis of the facial nerves, so that expressive movements of the face are absent. Paralysis of the ocular nerves limits eye movements. Various associated defects are common. No treatment is described in the literature and there is no previously reported association with criminality. When the patient was 20 he took up residential catering work in a hospital, with accommodation in the nurses' home. Here he encountered teasing and ostracism more cruel and sustained than ever before. On several occasions over a period of about 17 mth he burnt and damaged clothing. At his trial for arson and criminal damage the probation report covered his physical defects and the background to the offences. Notwithstanding the fact that in another case of arson the judge thought it wise to call for a psychiatrist's report the judge sentenced him to 4 years imprisonment. This remarkable case is further discussed from a psychiatrist's viewpoint.
