N2  - Infectious mononucleosis (IM) is a common systemic illness primarily involving children and young adults and due apparently to the Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). Although the typical case is easily recognized, unusual manifestations or serious complications occur frequently enough to pose problems in diagnosis and management. Neurologic complications are rare (1%) - these include meningoencephalitis, infectious polyneuritis (Guillain-Barre-Strohl syndrome) and mononeuritis. These complications may actually precede the full clinical picture or present as the sole manifestation of the illness, thereby causing a confusing clinical picture and delay in diagnosis. The described case illustrates how IM can present only as bilateral facial palsy without any other neurologic signs or symptoms.
