N2  - A curative case of intracranial myotic aneurysm with brain abscess, which occurred as a complication of bacterial endocarditis, was reported. A 25-year old female with a congenital supravalvular aortic stenosis was admitted to another hospital because of severe headache, vomiting and convulsion on Nov. 5, 1976. Right carotid angiography revealed a dumbbell shaped aneurysmal shadow at the distal portion of middle cerebral artery. Occlusion of major branches were also seen in the middle cerebral artery. She was referred to Tohoku University Hospital on Nov. 19, 1976. On admission she was retarded in her consciousness and neurological examination disclosed nuchal rigidity and right hemiparesis with central facial palsy. Funduscopic examination revealed bilateral papilledema. On physical examination, cardiac murmur, cyanosis and clubbing of fingers were also noticed. Laboratory study showed severe normochromic anemia, elevated sedimentation rate and positive CRP. Blood culture showed beta- hemolytic streptococcus. Second right carotid angiography was performed on Nov. 19 and it showed that the aneurysm had increased in size over a period of 14 days and increased opacification of right middle cerebral artery. EMI scan revealed a high density area and a ring-like shadow. From the clinical symptoms, findings of angiogram and EMI scan, diagnosis of intracranial mycotic aneurysm and brain abscess were established. Treatment with massive antibiotics and gamma-globlin was begun and the next day she was afebrile and her general condition rapidly improved. The right frontotemporal craniotomy was performed and excision of aneurysm and total removal of abscess were carried out. Postoperative course was uneventful and she was discharged with only paresthetic sensation in her left hand.
