N2  - The diagnosis of 'cold' peripheral facial paralysis is done by a process of elimination; it can only be made after having formally eliminated all other causes. Once the diagnosis is made, it poses essentially the problem of the prognosis for recovery. Fortunately, in the great majority of cases the progress is spontaneously favorable, hastened, however, by medical treatment, particularly by corticotherapy. Much more rarely, this facial paralysis is serious or even grave, and its recovery will be incomplete, leaving some formidable esthetic sequelae, like paralysis, but above all hypertonia, synkinesis or even hemispasm. It is for preventing or restricting as much as possible the sequelae of these grave forms that surgical decompression of the facial nerve may be proposed.
