N2  - The present work aims to the evaluation of the etiological and clinico-epidemiological aspects of the purulent meningites, observed in a Division of Infectious Diseases in the period 1968-1973, and chiefly of their neuro-psychical outcomes. As regards the etiology of the purulent meningites, in the authors' case report the meningococcal forms are prevailing and they are followed by the so-called abacterial meningites. Moreover, the authors remark some preference of the D. pneumoniae meningitis towards the elderly people, whereas the meningitis forms caused by Enterobacteriaceae appear to be rare in the neonatal period. No meaningful differences have been remarked in autumn-winter and during the spring. At the clinical level, frequent atypical onsets of the disease have been remarked and they have been characterized by a shifty and attenuated pattern. Among 393 patients affected by purulent meningitis, 359 patients recovered (i.e., 91.30% of the total, whereas 34 patients died (8.70%). As regards the meningococcal meningitis, 175 subjects out of 242 have recovered quickly, whereas 53 patients have recovered in a delayed manner. In the range of the meningococcal meningitis, 14 deaths have been also surveyed. The general lethality has been low on the whole in the authors' own experience, with the exception of the pneumococcal purulent meningitis, which have exhibited on the contrary a high lethality. In relation to the neurological complications, generalized convulsions have been mainly observed, followed by cranial nerve palsy, monoparesis and hemiparesis, which have exibited a lesser incidence. The authors report as permanent outcomes facial palsy, monoparesis, hemiparesis, hidrocephalus, hypoacusia, deafness and epilepsy. In order to evaluate the long-term neuropsychical outcomes, the authors have carried out a particularly interesting neurological, psychological and electroencephalographic investigation, which has put into evidence on the whole a slight incidence of neuropsychical steady sequelae, chiefly characterized by the so-called transitional syndrome.
