N2  - From 1967 to 1977 a total of 2144 patients consulted a Pain Clinic. In these patients, facial spasm accounted for 344, trigeminal neuralgia 313, facial pain and headache 244, pain of the upper extremities and shoulder 198, lumbo-sacral pain 195, cancer pain 194, and facial palsy 188. Patients with facial spasm were treated with physical facial nerve block, and patients with trigeminal neuralgia were given branch block with 0.5 ml ofpure alcohol or Gasserian ganglion block. Patients with facial pain and headache were subjected to ipsilateral stellate ganglion block with 8 ml of 1% mepivacaine, single or continuous cervical epidural block with 5-8 ml of 1-2% mepivacaine, total cerebro-spinal block with 20-30 me of 1.5% mepivacaine, or EAP (electric acupuncture). Lumbo-sacral pain was managed with single or continuous lumbo-sacral epidural block with 5-8 ml of 1-2% mepivacaine. For patients with pain of the upper extremities and shoulder ipsilateral stellate ganglion block, suprascapular nerve block with 8 ml of 1% mepivacaine, single or continuous cervical epidural block, or EAP was provided. For cancer pain, continuous epidural block or intra-thecal permanent block with 0.2-0.3 ml of 10-15% phenol in glycerin was given. Patients with facial palsy were subjected to ipsilateral stellate ganglion block. Of these, 198 patients were hospitalized and subjected to nerve block.
