N2  - Two cases of tubercular otitis media are reported. These cases were treated by ordinary means for chronic purulent otitis media for a period over a year without any improvement. Case one was a woman, aged 65, who complained of impaired hearing in both ears, otorrhea, vertigo and right facial palsy. Operative procedure on the affected ear showed a tumor-like or tubercular appearance of the tympanic mucoperiosteum. Histological examination of the removed specimen revealed a typical tubercular lesion. Case two was a woman, aged 31, found to be affected with lung tuberculosis and a chronic purulent otitis media with narrowing of the right ear canal. A bacterial examination of the otorrhea revealed positive findings of tubercular bacilli. Both cases were treated by anti-tuberculosis measures by use of streptomycin, PAS and INAH, and with topical application of streptomycin in the ear. Within a month after these treatments the ear discharge was gradually reduced and became much clearer in its content.
