N2  - Facial nerve dysfunction should be considered as a possible symptom of several underlying diseases which may involve the nerve from its origin near the brainstem to its periphery. Localisation of the site of facial nerve involvement can be made by careful clinical examination as well as by electrophysiological techniques. Specialised radiology is also helpful in revealing lesions within the temporal bone as well as lesions in the cerebellopontine angle. Apart from the common conditions affecting the facial nerve, the author discusses three important conditions, namely congenital cholesteatoma in the cerebellopontine angle or the petrous apex, facial nerve neuroma and meningioma. The author pleads for a careful study of the problem of facial dysfunction to enable the otolaryngologist to make the correct diagnosis and suggest appropriate therapy.
