N2  - Arising from 18 of the author's own cases of parainfectious isolated, or multiple, cranial nerve neuritis, this localization type of an inflammatory reation of the peripheral nervous system is described. The causal relationship with the most varied types of infectious disease, but also with immunization or serum administration, is indicative of the pathogenetic role of an immunologic mechanism. Whether this concerns an immune complex disease, as is assumed in serogenetic polyneuritis, or a cell mediated immune response, as in the Guillain Barre syndrome, requires further investigation. Knowledge of this type of manifestation of 'cranial polyneuritis' is of clinical importance in order to avoid confusion with cranial nerve syndromes from other causes, and to avoid carrying out unnecessary diagnostic measures. (57 references are cited)
