N2  - Between 1971 and 1975, 47 cases of traumatic facial paralysis were observed (3.7% of the patients admitted for skull traumatism). Facial paralysis was more frequent in cases of traumatism with loss of consciousness (4.3%) than without (2.3%). In 17 cases there was no line of fracture; in 12 cases there was only a fracture of the squamous part of the temporal bone without any clear radiation towards the petrous pyramid. In only 18 cases was there a fracture of the petrous part of the temporal bone. Peroperative observations sometimes contradicted radiological data by revealing fractures which did not show up on Xray films. Of the 26 cases of immediate facial paralysis, 9 presented rapid regression within the first 15 days and 17 underwent operation. The lesion of the facial nerve observed at operation was: in 7 cases a contusion, in 7 cases compression, in 3 cases section. Of the 21 cases of secondary facial paralysis, 15 soon showed spontaneous recovery and 6 underwent operation. Observations at operation were: in 5 cases a contusion, in 1 case compression caused by a fracture which did not show up on Xray film. The results of operation were on the whole very satisfactory in the cases of simple contusion. They were regular but often incomplete in the cases of compression. As for surgical repairs of section of the facial nerve by means of microscopic procedures, this is still rather risky and the therapeutic results are often disappointing (2 failures out of every 3).
