N2  - The case of transtympanic facial nerve paralysis has been presented with a review of the literature. A high index of suspicion plus a careful examination of the external auditory canal after removal of blood clots will help to make the diagnosis. Polytomography (hypocycloidal laminography) may be helpful in localizing a foreign body or temporal bone fracture. Prompt exploration, decompression, anastomosis or nerve grafting may be indicated in acute complete traumatic facial nerve paralysis. Partially delayed facial nerve paralysis should be followed by daily facial nerve conductivity studies, and most often will recover spontaneously.
