N2  - The 5 yr follow up results obtained by the surgical treatment of 90 patients suffering from Meniere's disease are presented. Saccotomy with myo synangiosis was carried out in presence of a reversible Meniere's disease (fluctuant hearing loss with normal hearing in the intervals of the disease). The operation, which is easy to perform, relieved 50% of the patients from their symptoms and carried the risk of total deafness in 4% of the cases. Vestibular neurectomy was the operation of choice for the irreversible stage of the disease when hearing was still useful. The operation, which requires special oto neurosurgical training, relieved the patients from episodic vertigo in 94% of the cases, preserved hearing in 80%, reduced tinnitus and pressure in the ear in 66% and 63% respectively of the cases, carried the risk of deafness in 4%, of delayed but reversible facial palsy in 3%. Vestibular neurectomy was followed by difficulties in compensating the postoperative unilateral loss of vestibular function if poor motivation for reintegration into normal activity or central vestibular dysfunction were present. Cochleo vestibular neurectomy was carried out in patients with a total loss of hearing. The operation, which requires a good knowledge of temporal bone anatomy, eliminated vertigo in 94%, reduced tinnitus in 75% of the cases, and carried the risk of C.S.F. leak in 3% of the cases.
