N2  - A case of malignant lymphoma in the spinal epidural space terminating in acute myelogenous leukemia was reported, and the relationship between the two diseases is discussed. The patient, a 23 yr old man, was admitted because of muscular deterioration of the left extremities and sensory disturbance at the level of C6/T1 and L5/S2. Left Horner's symptom and left peripheral facial palsy were noted at the time of hospitalization. Myelography revealed the compression of the spinal cord from the left to the right at the level of C6/T1 and S1/S2. There was no abnormality in the peripheral blood until 5 days after cervical laminectomy when peripheral leukocyte count was suddenly increased, immature cells were discovered, and the patient terminated in acute myelogenous leukemia in 5 mth. Histologically, cervical epidural tumor was highly cellular, the neoplastic tissue being composed of ill bordered dark cells with narrow cytoplasm and round or oval, and often indented dark nuclei. Fine reticular network was found among neoplastic cells on preparation of silver impregnation. The autopsy revealed marked infiltration of leukemic cells in the brain and spinal cord, especially in the perivascular and subarachnoid spaces. Bone marrow showed hyperplasia with numerous leukemic cells. There was no leukemic infiltration in the nerve roots and dura mater in the spinal region. There was mild pathogenic cell infiltration in lymph nodes, spleen, adrenal glands, lung, testis, and intestinal mucosa. The possibility of the presence of a common stem cell which might form the malignant lymphoma and myelogenous leukemia is discussed in relation to this case.
