N2  - The term 'Melkersson Rosenthal syndrome' was coined by the Basel otologist Erich Luscher in 1949. This syndrome bears the names of the Swedish neurologist Ernst Melkersson of Goteborg and of the German neurologist Curt Rosenthal of Breslau. Melkersson (1898-1932) died at the outset of his life work 4 weeks after his promotion to the position of lecturer, the cause of his death being a septic infection. Melkersson regarded the syndrome of periodic paralyses of the facial muscles in connection with 'angioneurotic' oedema attacks in the lips as a peculiar combination of symptoms. Rosenthal (1892-1937) a Schleswigean doctor, was the victim in Paris of a traffic accident while on his way to London for the 'Emergency Association of German Scientists Abroad'. He described this syndrome in more detail and pointed out the arthritic symptoms, the varying nervous manifestations and the lingua plicata (which now has lost its diagnostic value). This review article not too lengthy, but factual, as far as is possible in this still uncomprehended syndrome, describes the formerly confusing nomenclature, its signs and symptoms, the manifestations or the skin and its neurologic aspects (cranial nerves, peripheral nerves and central nervous system). The differential diagnosis is discussed in brief as are the changes in the EEG and the psychopathologic phenomena. As regards the neuropathology, only one case has been described which included an autopsy of the brain. The neurovegetative pathophysiology of the M.R. syndrome is given much attention to, while the treatment is discussed as well. This is a useful review; 35 references are cited. d
