N2  - A liposarcoma, which originated in the left intraauricular area and infiltrated the posterior fossa, was seen in a 25 yr old housewife, who was admitted because of symptoms of intracranial hypertension. She showed left facial palsy and deviation of the tongue to the left without any cerebellar signs. Neurologic examinations suggested a mass lesion in the posterior fossa. Suboccipital craniectomy was performed and the tumor (4x4x3 cm) in the extradural space was partially removed. Microscopically the tumor consisted of lipoblasts and diagnosed as liposarcoma. Intracranial liposarcoma, whether primary or secondary are very rare tumors.
