N2  - Etiology of idiopathic facial paralysis remains mostly unknown. Differentiated evaluation of CSF however for cytological or proteinic abnormalities should be performed with serological examinations. In 9 patients treated within 8 months in hospital diagnosis could be made by these procedures. Inflammation, if it is the cause of facial paralysis, can only reveal pleocytosis, if localised within or next to the leptomeninges; protein of CSF perhaps will increase, if local inflammation of the nerve is further from the subarachnoid space. 'Idiopathic facial paralysis' however will not exclude focal inflammation far from the subarachnoid space. Surgical decompression should not be performed without a previous examination of CSF because of its uncertain success.
