N2  - The authors present seven cases of proven recurrent nerve palsy of the cranial nerves. The duration of the disease is between one and thirteen yr. The number of recurrencies is four per patient. The recurrencies are characterized by headache (in general retrocular) or hemicranial pain, several types of ophthalmoparesis, fifth nerve involvement, facial nerve paresis and, more seldom, eighth nerve and optic nerve paresis. It is interesting to point out than when the third cranial nerve is involved there is a dissociated paresis. All together, the data tend to show a lesion of the cavernous sinus. Many of the symptoms could be mistaken with painful ophthalmoplegia. The painful ophthalmoplegia and the multiple nerve cranial palsy could be part of the same clinical entity. The first one could represent a local form, the second one a multifocal form or an extension of the primary process behind the cavernous sinus.
