N2  - The most frequent cause of peripheral facial paralysis is rheumatic (Bell's) paralysis. However, the diagnosis of this paralysis may be misleading, since in the background of the facial paralysis, sometimes not ischemia, but other morphological change may be hidden. Over 10 years the author has collected data revealing intratemporal involvement of the facial nerve in 18 patients, who were previously treated for 'Bell's palsy'. As the cause of the facial paralysis neurinoma of the facial nerve, in 5 patients, in 1 patient meningeoma psammomatosum of the facial nerve, and in 2 patients late metastasis of malignant mammary or salivary gland tumor, have been observed. In 6 cases intrapyramidal cholesteatoma, in 2, granulation of unknown origin, in 1 case diffuse hemangiomatosis and in 1 patient ectopic parotid tissue were found. Besides more perfect diagnostical possibilities, the importance of detailed and careful anamnesis is pointed out, since in the majority of cases, intact tympanic membrane was present. The justification of explorative decompression and if necessary its use on a broader scale, and with more courage, is advocated.
