N2  - A case of hypertensive intracerebellar hematoma which was surgically treated and cured, is reported. A 41 year old male had two cerebrovascular attacks with headaches and vomiting followed by left hemiparesis. Drowsiness and dysarthria appeared the next day. The patient was admitted to a hospital, where right facial palsy, loss of right gag reflex and paralytic hemiplegia on the left side were noted. On the 7 th day, the patient's consciousness became clear but the other neurological symptoms remained. On the 14th day, bradycardia and central hyperventilation appeared: the patient became drowsy again and was transferred to the authors' clinic. When the patient was admitted, he showed typical cerebellar signs such as nystagmus, ataxia, and slurring speech with pyramidal sign on the left side and cranial nerve paralysis on the right side, and also showed changes of vital signs as in a medullary syndrome in its late stages. Vertebral angiogram revealed an invasive process in the right cerebellar hemisphere. The old blood (30 g) was removed by suboccipital craniectomy. The hematoma cavity communicated with the IVth ventricle through a small perforation in the medial wall of the hematoma. Spontaneous intracerebellar hematoma, including of hypertensive origin, is not rare in autopsy reports but surgically treated cases have only rarely been reported. The main reason for the few survivals probably lies in the fulgurant course of this disease.
