N2  - In geriatric practice oropharyngeal dysphagia of neuromuscular origin is not rare. In its background, mostly cerebrovascular changes, bulbar paralysis, pseudobulbar paralysis or other such organic damage to the brain play a role. Realizing the geriatric significance of neuromuscular dysphagia, the authors, pondering over the clinical pharmacological data available to them, began using an anti cholinergic agent, Stigmosan. In old patients with dysphagia the effect of 1/2 to 1 tablet of Stigmosan half an hour before meals was very good in the majority of the cases; the 'swallowing' dysphagic patients, who not infrequently were also aspirating patients as well, became free of all complaints.
