N2  - Peripheral facial paralysis is a diagnostic challenge. Every effort must be made to determine etiology since often a treatable cause can be found. Patients with peripheral facial paralysis are often labeled as having Bell's palsy without any adequate evaluation. Bell's is an exclusive term reserved for patients in whom all known causes of acute acquired isolated peripheral facial paralysis have been ruled out. This point is stressed since 17 percent of the last 160 consecutive patients referred to us with a diagnosis of Bell's palsy were found to have an underlying cause.
