N2  - During the eight years (1967-1975) the diagnosis of Wegener's Granulomatosis (WG) was established in 12 patients hospitalized at the University of Iowa and VA Hospital, Iowa City. Eight were male, 4 female with an age range 6-69. Two patients, initially diagnosed as midline granuloma, subsequently evolved to a diagnosis of WG with the development of renal involvement after an interval of 3 and 4 years. Simultaneous sinus, pulmonary and renal involvement was seen in 4/12 patients; sinus and renal in 4/12; sinus and lung in 2/12; lung and renal in 1/12; and lung only in one patient whose tissue biopsy was interpreted as lymphomatoid Granulomatosis (LG). Other clinical findings included ophthalmic involvement (2/12), facial palsy (1/12), arthralgias (3/12), skin rash (3/12) and diabetes insipidus (1/12). Ten of 12 patients presented with a hemoglobin < 11.5 Gm; 7/12 with leukocytosis > 11,000; 7/12 with thrombocytosis > 500,000; and 11/12 with an elevated sedimentation rate. No consistent diagnostic pattern of serum immunoglobins (IgA, IgM, IgG) was observed. One patient was treated with prednisone alone for 2 years and subsequently lost to followup. Three of 12 patients have died: 1 patient received no therapy; 2 patients, who presented with thrombocytopenia, azotemia and renal failure, developed bone marrow suppression and were intolerant of cytotoxic therapy. Eight of 12 patients are alive: 6/12 are continuing on therapy with cytotoxic agents (cyclophosphamide) with satisfactory clinical responses; 2 patients are clinically in remission: 1 patient with LG treated 6 months with prednisone, and 1 patient with WG treated 17 months with azathioprine. Remission on these patients now dates 42 months and 21 months, respectively. One patient developed a unique complication of therapy consisting of extensive 'shelling out' of multiple large nodular lesions with the subsequent development of obstructive airways disease. This review supports the concept that prompt institution of cytotoxic therapy prior to the onset of renal deterioration has definite value in the management of this illness.
