N2  - Ischemia plays an etiopathogenic role not only in idiopathic paralysis but also in a remarkable number of traumatic, otic and viral facial paralysis. The common traits in the pathogenesis of these paralyses are: an inextensible facial bone canal, the absence of drainage orifices, and oedema. Decompression of the nerve must be obtained by cutting a fine, narrow gap in the bone canal, without broad denudation of the nerve. Total facial paralysis in which the gustatory disturbances do not disappear within 2 wk, or which in the course of 8 wk do not give signs of clinical recovery, must be operated.
