N2  - In a 56 yr old Japanese male whose father and one sister had died of the same disease, the onset of the illness was at the age of 46 yr with complaints of gait disturbances and sharp pains in the lower extremities. Subsequently, dysarthria and marked ataxia in the trunk and extremities developed. Six years later, apathy, urinary incontinence, atactic eye movements, bulbar palsy, fasciculation of the muscles and convulsions became manifest. The EEG showed no periodic synchronous discharge, while irregular theta and delta waves were extremely abundant. The CSF was normal except for an occasionally positive Wassermann reaction. The patient died from a subarachnoid hemorrhage at the age of 56, when the decerebrated state had lasted for about 3 yr. The total duration of the illness was about 11 yr. The brain weighed 930 g., showing atrophy of the cerebrum, especially in the frontal region. The cerebellum, midbrain, pons and spinal cord also showed atrophy. On the cut surface, the atrophy of the white matter, caudate nucleus and thalamus was severe, while that of the putamen and globus pallidus was less so. Atheromatous changes of the arteries were scarce. Histologic examination revealed that numerous plaques and status spongiosus associated with neuron loss, degeneration and astrocytosis were present throughout the cerebral cortex. A number of plaques, many of which were reminiscent of the kuruplaque, were also observed in both the molecular and granular layers of the cerebellum. Small to moderate numbers of plaques were found in the striatum, midbrain, pons and subcortical white matter of the cerebrum and cerebellum. A moderate to severe degree of neuronal loss and degeneration accompanying astrocytosis was observed in the basal ganglia, brainstem and cerebellum. In the spinal cord, loss of nerve cells was generally mild. Moreover, another finding in this case was the severe demyelination which was distributed, more or less, throughout the entire CNS. Comparing the degree of demyelination with the presence of protoplasmic astrocytes and fat laden macrophages, fibrous gliosis in these demyelinated areas was definitely mild. Changes of the cortex (status spongiosus, neuronal loss and degeneration, and presence of the numerous plaques) might indicate Creutzfeldt Jakob disease and kuru. However, the combination of these changes with widespread demyelination in this case is unique and has not been reported in the literature, before.
