N2  - The authors report a series of 18 patients with peripheral facial palsy resulting from removal of acoustic neuroma (16) or petrous bone fracture (2) and treated by spinal accessory or hypoglossal facial anastomosis. After a review of the literature concerning this operation, the authors report their own experiences based on 12 spinal accessory facial anastomoses and 6 hypoglossal facial anastomoses. Anastomosis is suitable in patients whose VIIth nerve was obviously destroyed during removal of a neuroma, and operation should be performed as early as possible. EMG data influences the choice of nerve more than as an indication itself. When VIIth nerve is presumed to be preserved, anastomosis should be performed only if no recovery of the facial palsy has appeared after the third month. The authors describe the operative procedure for spinal accessory and hypoglossal facial anastomosis. Magnification helps in accuracy of nerve suture. The authors report their results and emphasize the necessity of early surgery, using the microscope and the contribution of the patient.
