N2  - The subjects of this study were 73 traumatized new born babies among a total of 4163 vaginal deliveries at Tokyo Metropolitan Police Hospital over a 6 year period, from January 1969 to February 1975. Incidence, etiological factors, prognosis of these traumas and the preventive treatments of delivery are discussed in detail. On these 73 cases, various kinds of trauma were observed, such as cephalohematoma (46 cases, 1.11%), skeletal fracture (the clavicula, the humerus, and the temporal os) (11 cases, 0.26%), torticollis (10 cases, 0.24%), dislocation of the articulatio (sternoclavicular and humeroscapular) (2 cases, 0.05%), intracranial hemorrhage (2 cases, 0.05%), and facial palsy (2 cases, 0.05%). The various obstetrical factors were suggested to contribute to the cause of these traumas. The incidence of trauma was significantly increased as compared to the controls when the babies were either too big or premature and also as a result of obstetrical management such as breech extraction, vacuum or forceps deliveries. The result of this investigation suggested that adequate obstetrical management was essential in order to prevent the birth trauma especially when the causing factors were prospected.
