N2  - The technique of exposure, control inspection of continuity and decompression of the paralysed facial nerve in its intracranial course from middle cranial fossa approach is described. The method of nerve exposure in its petrous and tympanic parts and in the area of geniculate ganglion is discussed in detail. Certain personal modifications of this method are presented including particularly the epidural approach from superior tympanotomy (from the mastoid and tympanic tegmentum to reach the tympanic part and proximal portion of the mastoid part). Two cases of traumatic facial nerve palsy in the geniculate area are described in which trauma resulted in fracture of the cranial basis involving the pyramid. In both cases a satisfactory result was obtained with complete or considerable return of movements of the facial muscles. Cases of facial nerve function improvement after partial removal of pyramid or after resection of acoustic nerve tumors are described briefly.
