N2  - The authors conclude that the problem of the operative replacements in the stapedian surgery of otospongiosis constitutes a very special aspect of the operative techniques and a significant aggravating factor. The more a technique is developed the less it provokes failures calling for a replacement, but it also renders dangerous from the cochlear aspect the replacement necessitated by its failure. In practice, unanimity is now assured on the need for closing the oval window with a tissue graft, which combines the functional flexibility of an elastic graft with the protective role of a resistant and viable closing membrane for the oval window.
